window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"href": "ai-automated-actions.html", "title": "AI Automated Actions — User Guide", "text": "AI Automated Actions — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub AI Automated Actions — User Guide Let Odoo automatically ask an AI (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Mistral) to read a record and write something useful back — no coding needed. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? This module plugs a generative AI straight into Odoo. When something happens on a record — an order is confirmed, a document is attached — Odoo can automatically send that record's data to an AI model and get back written text. That answer is then posted into the record's chatter , saved into a field, or turned into a to-do activity, all inside Odoo. Why it exists: teams spend a lot of time on repetitive reading-and-writing — summarising a long email thread, drafting a reply, pulling key facts out of a PDF. This hands those tasks to an AI automatically, triggered by events you already track, so nobody has to copy-paste text into a separate chatbot. Three moving parts. A Provider (the AI company + your key) → a Server Action (the recipe: which model, what to ask, where the answer goes) → an Automation Rule (the trigger that decides when it runs). Set them up in that order. A few words you'll hear AI Provider The company whose AI you use — OpenAI, Anthropic (Claude), Google Gemini or Mistral. This is where you paste your API key. AI Model A specific \"brain\" from a provider (e.g. Claude 3.5 Haiku). Each has settings for creativity, length, and whether it can read files. Server Action (Generative AI) The AI recipe: which model, the prompt, what data to include, and where to put the answer. Automation Rule The trigger that decides when the recipe runs (e.g. \"when a record is created\"). Prompt Template The instructions you write for the AI. It can pull live data with placeholders like &#123;&#123; object.name &#125;&#125; . AI Log A record of what the AI did and any errors, under AI Logs — your first place to look if nothing happened. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the plain-language explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears in Odoo Choice / option Where to check One-time setup Onboarding aid for the much_automated_agent_actions module (Odoo 19). Field names, menus and options are drawn from the module source. Self-contained offline. Features marked \"coming soon\" in the module are not covered here. Use Handout view for a printable one-pager. const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'0 · Connect an AI provider',dir:'One-time · with an admin',scn:'Before anything works, an admin plugs in the account key for the AI company you want to use (e.g. Anthropic / Claude).',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Open the Providers list',ex:'AI > Configuration > Providers',mo:'AI > Configuration > Providers',st:[],biz:'Four providers ship ready to use — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, Mistral AI. You do not create them from scratch; you just open the one you want.',cfg:'Needs the AI Administrator access right.'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Paste your API Key',ex:'Provider form → API Key',mo:'AI > Configuration > Providers > (provider)',st:[],biz:'The API key is how Odoo proves it is allowed to use your paid AI account. Paste the secret key from the provider’s website into the API Key field.',cfg:'Without a valid key, actions fail silently — you will only see the error in AI > Logs, not a popup.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Activate the model you want',ex:'AI > Configuration > Models',mo:'AI > Configuration > Models',st:['Inactive','Active'],biz:'Many models come pre-loaded but switched off. Toggle on the exact one you plan to use (e.g. Claude 3.5 Haiku) and leave the rest off to avoid confusion.',cfg:'Keep the active list short — only what your team actually uses.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Tune the model (optional)',ex:'Creativity: Factual · Length: Short',mo:'AI > Configuration > Models > (model)',st:[],biz:'Set Creativity Level and Response Length so answers are consistent, and turn on Files/Images if the AI should read attachments.',cfg:'A Manager can click \"Check Parameter Support\" so Odoo learns which settings that model accepts — this prevents errors from unsupported options.'} ]}, {id:'action',label:'1 · Build the AI recipe (Server Action)',dir:'You configure it once per task',scn:'You want the AI to summarise each new record and post the summary into its chatter.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Create a Server Action',ex:'Name: \"Summarise request\"',mo:'AI > Actions > Server Actions',st:[],biz:'This is the reusable AI recipe you will attach to a trigger later. Set the Odoo model it applies to (e.g. Sales Order) first, because other fields filter on it.',cfg:'You can also reach it via Settings > Technical > Automated Actions.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Set the type to Generative AI',ex:'Action type → Generative AI',mo:'The action’s type selector',st:[],biz:'Choosing \"Generative AI\" unlocks the AI-only fields (model, prompt, output).',cfg:'If you don’t pick this type, none of the AI options appear.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Pick the AI Model',ex:'AI Model → Claude 3.5 Haiku',mo:'Action form → AI Model',st:[],biz:'Chooses which brain (and which provider key) runs this action.',cfg:'Only models you activated in step 0 show in the dropdown.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Write the Prompt Template',ex:'\"Summarise this in 3 sentences: &#123;&#123; object.name &#125;&#125;\"',mo:'Action form → Prompt Template',st:[],biz:'These are the instructions sent to the AI; the &#123;&#123; ... &#125;&#125; placeholders are replaced with the record’s real values.',cfg:'Use the \"Preview Prompt\" button to see exactly what the AI will receive for a real record before going live.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Choose what extra data to include',ex:'Include Chatter · Attachments · Report',mo:'Action form → Include… toggles',st:[],biz:'Feed the AI more context: the conversation history, attached files/images, a generated PDF report, or the full record data.',cfg:'Attachments only work if the model allows files/images and you stay under its Max Files limit.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Choose where the answer goes',ex:'Output Destination → Chatter',mo:'Action form → Output Destination',st:[],biz:'Decide whether the AI reply becomes a chatter note, is written into a field, or becomes a scheduled activity.',cfg:'\"Field\" overwrites whatever is already there; \"Create Activity\" needs an activity type and an assigned user.'} ]}, {id:'trigger',label:'2 · Make it run automatically',dir:'Automation Rule fires the recipe',scn:'You want the summary to run by itself every time a new record is created.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Open Automation Rules',ex:'AI > Actions > Automation Rules',mo:'AI > Actions > Automation Rules',st:[],biz:'This is the trigger layer that decides when your AI action fires.',cfg:'The list is pre-filtered to Generative AI rules.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Set the model and trigger',ex:'Model: Sales Order · On Creation',mo:'Automation rule form',st:[],biz:'Defines the event that starts everything — for example, whenever a sales order is created.',cfg:'Add filter conditions so the AI only runs for certain records (e.g. orders above a value).'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Attach your AI action',ex:'Add \"Summarise request\"',mo:'Automation rule > Actions to run',st:[],biz:'Links the trigger to the AI recipe you built in task 1.',cfg:'The AI runs in the background, so the answer appears a few seconds after the trigger, not instantly.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Watch it work / check logs',ex:'\"Agent is working on it!\" popup',mo:'AI > Logs',st:['Info','Error'],biz:'You get a popup that the AI is running, then another when it is done. If nothing appears on the record, the reason is in the logs.',cfg:'Filter AI > Logs by \"Error\" first — bad API key or template mistakes show up there.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad|block/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|active|ok|success|info/.test(s))return'ok';if(/inactive|draft|choice|manual/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "channelengine-integration.html", "title": "ChannelEngine Integration (Retired) — Reference", "text": "ChannelEngine Integration (Retired) — Reference ← Back to Guide Hub Retired · End of life ChannelEngine Integration A reference page for a retired product — written in plain language for colleagues supporting customers who still use it, and for anyone who just wants to understand what it did. Light / dark Print / PDF ⚠️ Do not sell this to new customers This product has reached end of life (retired, no longer developed). Please do not offer or sell it to new customers. This page exists to explain what it was and to help you support customers who are already using it. 1. First — what is ChannelEngine itself? ChannelEngine is an outside company and software platform (it is not ours). Its job is to be a single middleman between a business's own systems and the huge online marketplaces where people shop. A marketplace is a big website where many sellers list their products — think Amazon or eBay. Selling on lots of them is powerful but painful: each one has its own rules, its own screens, and its own way of sending you orders. ChannelEngine hides all that. You connect your systems to ChannelEngine once , and through it you can sell on 1,300+ marketplaces . A few you'll recognise: Amazon eBay bol.com Zalando Kaufland Otto Cdiscount Google Shopping …and ~1,300 more ChannelEngine keeps four things in sync across every channel automatically: product info, stock, prices, and orders . It's often called \" middleware \" — software that sits in the middle and passes messages between two other systems. 2. What the \"ChannelEngine Integration\" did Our product was the bridge between Odoo and ChannelEngine . On its own, Odoo (the business's ERP — the system that runs orders, stock, and invoicing) doesn't know anything about ChannelEngine. The integration connected the two so information flowed automatically, in both directions, with no copy-pasting. Your systems Odoo Products, stock, prices, orders, invoices ⇄ The middleman ChannelEngine One hub for all marketplaces ⇄ The shops Marketplaces Amazon, eBay, bol.com, … 3. What flowed where The easiest way to picture an integration like this is by direction — what Odoo sends out , and what it brings in : What Direction In plain words Products offers Odoo → out Your product details (name, description, images, barcode) and your \"offer\" — the price and stock for each channel — are pushed up so the marketplaces can list them. Stock Odoo → out Odoo keeps sending the current \"how many are left\" so the marketplaces don't sell something you no longer have (that's called overselling). Prices Odoo → out Price changes in Odoo are pushed up so every channel shows the right price. Orders in → Odoo When someone buys on a marketplace, that order comes back into Odoo as a normal sales order — ready to pick, pack and invoice. Shipments tracking Odoo → out When you ship the order in Odoo, the \"shipped\" status and the tracking number are sent back so the marketplace can tell the customer. Cancellations returns in → Odoo If a marketplace order is cancelled or the customer returns something, that update flows back into Odoo. Honest note: the exact buttons, menus and settings of our old ChannelEngine module aren't documented here — the code is retired and not in our current codebase. The flows above are how a marketplace integration like this normally works, and match ChannelEngine's own description of what it does. 4. Words you'll hear Marketplace / Channel A website where products are sold (Amazon, eBay…). \"Channel\" is just a more general word for the same thing. Offer A product as listed on one channel — i.e. the product plus its price and stock for that specific marketplace. GTIN / EAN The product's barcode number. Marketplaces use it to know exactly which product you mean. Middleware Software that sits in the middle of two systems and passes messages between them. ChannelEngine is middleware. Mapping Matching up two systems' data — e.g. \"our category X = the marketplace's category Y\" — so information lands in the right place. Overselling Selling more than you actually have in stock. Keeping stock in sync is what prevents it. 1P vs 3P On Amazon: 1P (\"first-party\") = you sell to Amazon and they resell it; 3P (\"third-party\") = you sell directly to shoppers on Amazon's marketplace. ChannelEngine can handle both. Fulfilment Who actually stores and ships the goods — you (seller-fulfilled) or the marketplace (e.g. Amazon's \"FBA\", Fulfilled By Amazon). 5. Why it's retired much. stopped building and maintaining this integration, so it reached end of life . Retired products get no new features or fixes and must not be sold to new customers. Customers already on it can keep using it, and we still help them. Selling to a new customer who wants marketplaces? Don't use this. Ask your team lead which marketplace approach is the current, recommended one to offer — this page should never be the answer for a brand-new project. 6. If a customer still uses it Where it lived in Odoo Like our other connectors, under the Integration Hub menu — with its own settings, a log (the system's diary of what happened; your first place to look when something seems off), and scheduled jobs that ran on a timer. The most common tickets \"An order didn't come into Odoo\" or \"the marketplace shows the wrong stock/price.\" Both are usually answered by checking the logs and the last scheduled run — the same way you'd troubleshoot the Shopware connector. When in doubt Because this product is retired and not in our current codebase, escalate anything tricky to a senior colleague rather than guessing. Reference page for the retired ChannelEngine Integration — end-of-life, do not sell to new customers. ChannelEngine is a third-party platform; the platform facts here are from its public materials ( channelengine.com ). The exact behaviour of our old module is not documented here because the code is legacy and not in the current codebase. Self-contained works offline."}, {"href": "enhanced-traceability.html", "title": "Enhanced Traceability — User Guide", "text": "Enhanced Traceability — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub Enhanced Traceability — User Guide Follow every lot and serial number through manufacturing and repair, so you can prove exactly what went into — and what's been done to — each product. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? This module extends Odoo's built-in manufacturing and repair tools so you can track lots and serial numbers all the way through production and after-sales service . It records which parts are currently inside a serialised product, adds repair \"service dates\" into the standard traceability report, and links helpdesk tickets to the exact product and lot. In short, it makes the paper trail behind a finished product richer and easier to read. Why it exists: when a product is recalled, fails a quality audit, or a customer disputes a repair, you need to answer \"what exactly is inside this unit, and what has been done to it?\" — fast and provably. This matters most in regulated or high-value manufacturing (electronics, medical, machinery). Good to know: despite the \"Manufacturing Traceability\" name, the extra features focus on the repair service side — service dates, live \"active components\", ticket links, and bulk repair tools. It relies on the Repair and Helpdesk apps being installed. What it adds over standard Odoo Service Date in the traceability report A new column showing, for any repair line, when that repair was completed. Scrap lines are hidden to keep the report clean. Active Components A live list of the parts currently inside a unit — automatically excluding anything removed or recycled during a past repair. Helpdesk ticket links A \"Tickets\" smart button on product and lot forms, plus Product / Lot fields on the ticket. Bulk repair tools Create many repair orders from selected lots, and Confirm / Start / End / Cancel many at once. A few words you'll hear Lot / Serial number The ID for a specific batch (lot) or a single unit (serial); the anchor for all tracking. Traceability report Odoo's report showing every stock move for a lot/serial, upstream and downstream. Upstream / downstream Upstream = where the material came from; downstream = where it went. Active components The parts currently installed in a serialised product, after repairs are accounted for. Service date The date a repair was completed — now shown in the traceability report. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears in Odoo Choice / selection Where to check Onboarding aid for the much_manufacturing_traceability module (Odoo 19). Requires the Repair and Helpdesk apps. Menus follow standard Odoo locations (Inventory / Repairs). Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'trace',label:'A · Trace a product’s history',dir:'Read-only investigation',scn:'A customer reports a fault on serial SN00123. You want its full genealogy plus any repairs done.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Open the lot / serial record',ex:'Open SN00123',mo:'Inventory > Products > Lots/Serial Numbers',st:[],biz:'Every tracked unit has a record here — the starting point for tracing it.',cfg:'Use the \"Last Delivery Partner\" filter to find units by who received them.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Open the Traceability report',ex:'Traceability button on the lot',mo:'Lot form > Traceability',st:[],biz:'Standard Odoo report showing every move for this serial — receipt, manufacturing, delivery, repairs.',cfg:'You see the full upstream/downstream flow in one tree.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Read the new \"Service Date\" column',ex:'Far right of the report',mo:'Traceability report (added column)',st:[],biz:'For any line that came from a repair order, this shows when that repair was completed.',cfg:'Scrap lines are hidden, so the report stays focused on real product flow.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Check \"Active Components\"',ex:'What is inside the unit now',mo:'Lot form > Active Components group',st:[],biz:'Shows the parts currently inside the unit, already accounting for anything removed or recycled in past repairs.',cfg:'If it is empty unexpectedly, confirm the lot’s Company is set — it will not compute without it.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Open linked tickets',ex:'Tickets smart button (life-ring)',mo:'Lot / Product form > Tickets',st:[],biz:'Jumps to helpdesk tickets logged against this exact serial.',cfg:'The button shows a count when tickets exist.'} ]}, {id:'bulk',label:'B · Create repairs in bulk',dir:'From selected lots',scn:'A batch of 20 units needs a recall rework; you want one repair order per unit without creating them by hand.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Go to the Lots/Serial list',ex:'List view',mo:'Inventory > Products > Lots/Serial Numbers',st:[],biz:'The list has a header button added by this module.',cfg:'Switch to list view so you can multi-select.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Select the lots',ex:'Tick all 20 recalled serials',mo:'Lots/Serial list (checkboxes)',st:[],biz:'Choose exactly the units that need a repair order.',cfg:'You can filter first to isolate the batch.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Click \"Create Repair Orders\"',ex:'Opens the Repair Order wizard',mo:'Lots/Serial list header button',st:[],biz:'Opens a wizard pre-loaded with your selected lots.',cfg:'The selected lots are passed in automatically.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Fill the wizard',ex:'Responsible · Tags · Date · Location · Notes',mo:'Repair Order wizard',st:[],biz:'These defaults are copied onto every repair order created.',cfg:'Set a responsible user so the repairs are assigned from the start.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Create — one repair per lot',ex:'20 repair orders, pre-filled',mo:'Wizard > Create Repair Order',st:[],biz:'Each repair order is created for that lot’s product, quantity and serial.',cfg:'If nothing is created, check a default repair operation type exists for the company.'} ]}, {id:'process',label:'C · Process & sign off repairs',dir:'End-of-day validation',scn:'15 repairs are done on the floor and need to be validated, closed, and signed off together.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Open the Repair Orders list',ex:'New Service Date & Tags columns',mo:'Repairs > Repair Orders',st:[],biz:'The added columns help you scan status at a glance.',cfg:'Sort by state before doing a mass action.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Select repairs in the same state',ex:'All \"Confirmed\", for example',mo:'Repair Orders list (checkboxes)',st:['Draft','Confirmed','Done'],biz:'The module blocks mixed-state mass actions.',cfg:'You will get \"Cannot apply mass action to repair orders in different states\" if they differ.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Run a mass action',ex:'Confirm / Start / End / Cancel',mo:'Repair Orders list > Action menu',st:[],biz:'Bulk-applies the chosen action across your whole selection (Start even auto-confirms drafts).',cfg:'Great for closing a day’s work in one go.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Set the sign-off',ex:'Tick \"Sign\" on a repair',mo:'Repair Order form > Sign',st:['Signed'],biz:'Stamps \"Signed by\" (you) and \"Signed at\" (timestamp) automatically, and logs it in the chatter.',cfg:'Use it as your proof the repair was reviewed.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Service date auto-fills',ex:'On Done, today’s date',mo:'Repair Order (service_date)',st:['Done'],biz:'When a repair reaches Done, the service date fills with today if empty — this is the date shown later in the traceability report.',cfg:'You can override it manually since the field is editable.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad|cancel/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|ok|success|signed|confirmed/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|manual/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "german-data-translations.html", "title": "German Data & Translations — User Guide", "text": "German Data & Translations — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub German Data Translations — User Guide Pre-loaded German accounting/HR master data plus polished German-language translations, so Odoo works the way German businesses and accountants expect. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? \"German Data Translations\" (internally branded Much OfD — \"Odoo für Deutschland\") is a pair of add-on modules. One pre-fills your database with German accounting and HR reference data on install; the other replaces Odoo's built-in German text with cleaner, more accurate translations. Together they save you from setting up German-specific data by hand and from living with rough machine-style German labels. Why it exists: Odoo ships English-first defaults and its standard German translation has gaps. German businesses also need specific local artifacts Odoo does not provide out of the box — the official AfA depreciation tables , German working-time and public-holiday calendars, and the BWA report every tax advisor expects. These modules fill those gaps so a German company can start immediately. Heads-up: this does not load German states or bank lists. What it actually loads is AfA depreciation asset models, working-time calendars, public holidays and BWA reports (Data module) plus improved German UI translations (Translations module). What each module provides Much OfD - German Data Loads on install: AfA depreciation asset models (by industry), working-time calendars, public holidays, and ready-made BWA financial reports (SKR03 SKR04). Much OfD - German Translations Replaces Odoo's German (de_DE) interface text across many apps with corrected, business-friendly wording — re-applied automatically on every upgrade. A few words you'll hear AfA (Absetzung für Abnutzung) Germany's official rules/tables for depreciating (writing down) the value of business assets over time. Asset model A reusable template that defines how a type of asset depreciates; this module pre-creates many, grouped by industry. BWA (Betriebswirtschaftliche Auswertung) A standard German monthly management report summarising revenue, costs and profit. SKR03 / SKR04 The two most common German standard charts of accounts; the BWA ships in a variant for each. Industry A category on AfA asset models; selecting industries activates the matching depreciation templates. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears in Odoo Choice / option Install / one-time Onboarding aid for the much. OfD modules ( much_german_data , much_german_translation , Odoo 19). Data is imported on install from bundled Excel files; translations re-apply on upgrade. Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'data',label:'A · Install & verify the German data',dir:'One-time setup',scn:'You just installed \"Much OfD - German Data\" and want to confirm the depreciation tables and industry selection are working.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Install the module',ex:'Apps → \"Much OfD - German Data\"',mo:'Apps',st:[],biz:'On install, a hook auto-imports the AfA tables, working times and public holidays from the bundled Excel files.',cfg:'The import needs the openpyxl Python library present on the server.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Turn on the AfA tables',ex:'Tick \"Use AfA tables for Assets Models\"',mo:'Settings > Odoo for Germany',st:[],biz:'This unlocks the industry selection used to filter which asset models are active.',cfg:'You must Save before the next step appears.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Select your industries',ex:'\"Update Industry\" → choose → Go Ahead',mo:'Settings > Odoo for Germany (Industry wizard)',st:[],biz:'Chosen industries activate their AfA asset models; the rest stay archived.',cfg:'A warning wizard shows exactly which industries will be added or removed before you commit.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Verify the asset models',ex:'Entries flagged \"from AfA table\"',mo:'Accounting > Configuration > Asset Models',st:[],biz:'These are the depreciation templates imported from the AfA table.',cfg:'AfA-sourced models are protected — a rule blocks archiving non-closed assets that did not come from the AfA table.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Open the BWA report',ex:'Pick the SKR03 or SKR04 variant',mo:'Accounting > Reporting > BWA',st:[],biz:'Confirms the BWA financial reports loaded correctly.',cfg:'Choose the variant that matches your chart of accounts.'} ]}, {id:'trans',label:'B · Improved German translations',dir:'Where they show up',scn:'You want Odoo\\'s interface to read in proper German and stay that way after updates.',steps:[ {m:'m-blue',t:'Set the language to German',ex:'User → Language = Deutsch (DE)',mo:'Settings > Users > (your user)',st:[],biz:'Translations only appear for users whose language is de_DE.',cfg:'Add German under Settings > Translations > Languages first if it is missing.'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Install the Translations module',ex:'\"Much OfD - German Translations\"',mo:'Apps',st:[],biz:'It layers corrected German terms over Odoo\\'s defaults across many apps.',cfg:'It only depends on base, so it is safe to install even if you do not use every covered app.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Check a corrected label',ex:'\"Expected Arrival\" → \"Erwartetes Lieferdatum\"',mo:'e.g. a Purchase Order',st:[],biz:'Demonstrates the improved German taking effect in the interface.',cfg:'Refresh the browser if an old label is cached.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Re-apply after upgrades',ex:'Apps → module → Upgrade',mo:'Apps > (module) > Upgrade',st:[],biz:'The upgrade force-overwrites terms, so the improved German always wins over Odoo\\'s built-in text.',cfg:'If a term ever reverts after an Odoo update, just upgrade this module again.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|ok|success/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|manual/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "german-documents-din5008.html", "title": "German Document Templates (DIN 5008) — User Guide", "text": "German Document Templates (DIN 5008) — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub German Document Templates (DIN 5008) — User Guide Make your Odoo invoices, quotations, orders and delivery slips print in the layout German business letters are expected to follow. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? DIN 5008 is the official German standard for how a business letter is laid out on the page: where the recipient's address goes, where fold marks sit, and where the sender info and \"info block\" belong. This module (the much QWeb Updater suite) rebuilds Odoo's printed documents to follow that layout, and adds a settings screen where you customise logos, footers, colours and extra text — no coding. It is a suite: one core module plus small extensions for Sales, Accounting, Purchasing and Inventory. Why it exists: in Germany, business correspondence is expected to follow DIN 5008 — it is what customers, vendors and authorities recognise as \"proper\". There's a practical payoff too: the recipient address lands exactly in the window of a standard German envelope, and fold marks let the page fold to fit. Odoo's default layout is not DIN-compliant out of the box. The base A4 grid, fold marks and hole-punch marks come from Odoo's standard l10n_din5008 layout. This suite builds on top of it and adjusts the header, address block, logo, colours and footer. What it changes on the printed page Recipient address block Repositioned to the DIN 5008 window-envelope location. Header logo A resizable logo and a one-line company sender line above the address, in your primary colour. Line-item table Table headers recoloured to your company's primary/secondary colours. Footer Rebuilt as four equal columns — either your free text, or auto-filled company details, VAT ID and bank account. Extra fields Optional \"Order Title\" and \"Description before Table\" that print above the item table. Affected documents: invoices credit notes , quotations sales orders , purchase orders RFQs , and delivery slips (which also gain country of origin, HS codes and weights). A few words you'll hear DIN 5008 The German standard defining business-letter formatting (margins, address position, info block, fold marks). Anschriftfeld (address field) The fixed zone where the recipient address prints, positioned to show through a window envelope. Falzmarken (fold marks) Small ticks in the left margin showing where to fold the page for a DIN envelope. Info block (Informationsblock) The right-hand box with document metadata — date, customer number, reference. QWeb Odoo's templating engine that renders every printed report; this module edits those templates. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears / prints Choice / option One-time setting Onboarding aid for the much. QWeb Updater / DIN 5008 suite (Odoo 19). Fold/hole-punch marks come from the base l10n_din5008 module. Field labels (\"Order Title\", \"Description before Table\") are from the module README — confirm wording in your installed language. Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'A · Set up the footer & logo',dir:'One-time customisation',scn:'You just installed the suite and want your documents to show a proper 4-column footer and a correctly sized logo.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Open the Qweb Updater settings',ex:'The control panel added by the module',mo:'Settings > Qweb Updater',st:[],biz:'This is the central screen for all DIN 5008 customisation. You need Settings/Administration access rights.',cfg:'No tab? Confirm the much_qweb_updater module is installed, then refresh.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Enable the free-text footer',ex:'Reveals Font Size + Column 1–4',mo:'Settings > Qweb Updater > Free Text Footer',st:[],biz:'With it off, the footer auto-fills from standard company data. With it on, you control all four columns.',cfg:'Leave it off if the default company footer is fine for you.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Fill the four footer columns',ex:'Address · Contact · VAT ID · Bank/IBAN',mo:'Settings > Qweb Updater',st:[],biz:'Each column prints at equal 25% width across the bottom of every document.',cfg:'Use a smaller Font Size (px) if your footer text is long, so it stays on one row.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Adjust the logo size',ex:'Set Logo Width / Height (mm or px)',mo:'Settings > Qweb Updater > Adjust Logo',st:[],biz:'Makes the header logo bigger or smaller than the default.',cfg:'If the logo overlaps the address, click \"Edit Paperformat\" and increase the top margin / header spacing — the screen links you there.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Save — it applies everywhere',ex:'All reports update immediately',mo:'Settings > Qweb Updater > Save',st:[],biz:'Changes apply to every report right away; no restart needed.',cfg:'Print one document to check the address lines up with your window envelope.'} ]}, {id:'print',label:'B · Print a compliant invoice',dir:'Everyday printing',scn:'You are sending a customer invoice and want a heading line plus intro text on the printed PDF.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Open the invoice',ex:'Any customer invoice',mo:'Accounting > Customers > Invoices',st:[],biz:'Open the invoice you want to send.',cfg:'The extra print fields are on the invoice form.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Fill the extra print fields',ex:'Order Title + Description before Table',mo:'Invoice form (added fields)',st:[],biz:'\"Order Title\" prints as a heading and \"Description before Table\" as an intro sentence, both above the line-item table.',cfg:'Leave them blank and nothing extra prints — they are optional.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Print / Preview',ex:'Print → Invoice',mo:'Invoice > Print > Invoice',st:[],biz:'Odoo renders the PDF through the DIN 5008 layout — address in the window position, your coloured table header, and the configured footer.',cfg:'Check the recipient address alignment before mailing a large batch.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|ok|success/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|manual/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "ihub-onboarding.html", "title": "much. Integration Hub — Onboarding", "text": "much. Integration Hub — Onboarding ← Back to Guide Hub much. Integration Hub — onboarding How iHub connects Odoo to external systems, with real examples from the Shopware integration. Light / dark Print / PDF 1. The big picture iHub is a standardized middle layer. Instead of every connector being a bespoke build, they all reuse the same seven building blocks. In code, an integration is two linked records: the shared ihub.integration (the super_id ) plus a connector-specific model (the sub_id ). Where the integration hub sits Odoo and external systems exchange data through the integration hub, which contains seven building blocks. Odoo ERP records External systems Shops, banks, 3PL much. integration hub Integration Scheduled actions Queue Background jobs Mapper Logs Cronwatch The seven building blocks 👆 Click any coloured block inside the hub above to see what it does. (function(){ const D={ integration:['m-purple','Integration','One saved connection to an external system — a shop, a bank, a 3PL. It holds the settings, credentials, on/off state and logo; everything else hangs off it. ihub.integration '], scheduled:['m-blue','Scheduled actions','Timers (crons) that run a sync automatically — for example “import new orders every hour”. Several ship switched off by default, so a stopped sync is often just a disabled timer.'], queue:['m-teal','Queue','A durable to-do list for heavy work: tasks are dropped on it and worked through in batches, surviving restarts and retrying safely. ihub.queue '], jobs:['m-blue','Background jobs','Runs a slow task on its own thread so the screen never freezes. A “zombie” job is one that died mid-run (e.g. a server restart); a watcher retries it. ihub.job.thread '], mapper:['m-coral','Mapper','A translation table between the outside system’s values and Odoo records — e.g. shop “Invoice” → payment term, “19%” → tax. ihub.mapper.item '], logs:['m-amber','Logs','A searchable, timestamped diary at five levels (debug → critical). Error logs are kept even if the operation rolls back. ihub.event.log '], cronwatch:['m-pink','Cronwatch','A smoke detector for the timers: if a scheduled action is switched off or deleted, it emails the people following it — so a silent stop becomes a visible alert.'] }; const box=document.getElementById('ihubDef'),t=document.getElementById('ihubDefT'),p=document.getElementById('ihubDefP'); window.ihubShow=function(k){const d=D[k];if(!d)return;box.className='ihub-def '+d[0];t.textContent=d[1];p.innerHTML=d[2];}; })(); 2. The three-level stack For shops, the layering is real: the Shopware connector only knows how to talk to Shopware's API; it plugs into order base , which owns the generic shop logic; which plugs into iHub . Order base calls a generic import_orders() and iHub's handler pattern routes it to import_orders_shopware_shop() — so swapping Shopware for Shopify changes only the top layer. The three-level integration stack for Shopware Odoo and a Shopware store connected through three layers: the Shopware connector, order base, and the integration hub. Odoo orders · stock products Shopware 6 e-commerce store Admin API much. Shopware connector OAuth · search · sync API (sub_id) much. order base orders · products · stock · returns much. integration hub the shared building blocks (super_id) 3. The workflows Start with the one-time setup , then the six live workflows. The teaching point across all of them: the same iHub blocks appear in different combinations — only product import leans on the queue, only heavy syncs spin up a job, but every workflow logs and every cron can be watched. Connector cron Queue Background job Mapper Record / API call Log Cronwatch Built for internal onboarding · grounded in the iHub user documentation (Knowledge article 1569) and the source of much-integration-hub, much-order-base-integration and much-order-shopware-integration. Use the buttons above to switch theme or print a handout (printing expands every workflow). const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'0 · Setup / first run',dir:'One-time configuration',scn:'Before any sync runs: install, create the integration, enter Shopware credentials, test, enable, and prepare mappers.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Install & create',ex:'Integration Hub → Integrations → New',biz:'Activate the much. Shopware app in Apps, then create an integration record: give it a name, set the Integration Type to shopware_shop, and pick the Odoo company it applies to.',mo:'ihub.integration + shop.connector',st:['Inactive'],cfg:'One integration per company · integration_group shop-connector · starts Inactive'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Enter credentials',ex:'Shopware host URL + OAuth client id/secret',biz:'In the Integration Settings tab, fill the Shopware Admin API base URL and the OAuth client credentials (integration access key id and secret from the Shopware admin).',mo:'host_url · api_key · api_password',st:[],cfg:'Client-credentials grant against POST /api/oauth/token · bearer token cached with expiry'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Test connection',ex:'Verify Odoo can reach Shopware',biz:'test_connection_shopware_shop resets the bearer token, re-authenticates, and calls Shopware’s health endpoint. Green means the credentials and URL are valid.',mo:'test_connection_shopware_shop()',st:['success','failed'],cfg:' GET /_info/version · returns (ok, message)'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Enable integration',ex:'Inactive → Test or Active',biz:'Click Enable Integration. iHub auto-detects whether this Odoo instance is staging or production and sets the state accordingly; scheduled actions then start running.',mo:'enable_integration()',st:['Inactive','Test','Active'],cfg:'An Active integration cannot be deleted — disable it first'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Configure crons',ex:'Set which syncs run and how often',biz:'The Scheduled Actions (Jobs tab) control each sync’s cadence, priority, and which integration triggers it. Custom parameters can be passed per cron.',mo:'ir.cron · Custom Cron Job Parameters',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:'Run Manually button for on-demand · integration_type_ids link a cron to this type'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Prepare mappers',ex:'Pre-map payments, carriers, taxes, categories',biz:'Decide how unknown values behave per mapper type (Automatic / Manual / Fail), and optionally pre-fill known mappings so the very first import is clean.',mo:'ihub.mapper.type · ihub.mapper.item',st:['Automatic','Manual','Fail'],cfg:'Base ships delivery_carrier / product_category / payment_term mapper types · on-import and on-export set independently'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'First run & logs',ex:'Run manually, then review Logs',biz:'Trigger an import manually and check the event logs. Mark logs Checked once verified; a Critical error can auto-create an Odoo activity for the responsible user.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Not checked','Checked'],cfg:'Activate IHub Logs first in Settings → Event Logs'} ]}, {id:'oi',label:'1 · Order import',dir:'Shopware → Odoo',scn:'Order SW-10042 (Anna Mueller, paid by Invoice) is placed in Shopware → a confirmed Sales Order in Odoo.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Daily cron fetches new orders',biz:'auto_import_shop_orders_connector runs; the Shopware connector queries the Admin API for orders created since the last sync, skipping completed and cancelled ones.',mo:'import_orders_shopware_shop()',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:' POST /api/search/order with a createdAt range · cron ir_cron_import_shop_orders_connector '}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Stage as shop order',ex:'shop.sale.order created (draft)',biz:'The raw Shopware JSON is stored first as a staging record, so processing is decoupled from fetching and nothing is lost if a later step fails.',mo:'shop.sale.order',st:['draft','linked','failed'],cfg:'external_number SW-10042 · full order+included JSON kept in payload'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Map values',ex:\"'Invoice' → payment term · 19% → account.tax\",biz:'External values are translated to Odoo records: payment method, shipping carrier, tax rate and currency each resolve through a mapper (auto-create, manual, or fail).',mo:'ihub.mapper.item',st:['Automatic','Manual','Fail'],cfg:'Invoice→account.payment.term · DHL Express→delivery.carrier · 19%→account.tax · EUR currency'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create Odoo order',ex:'sale.order with 3 lines, state = sale',biz:'to_odoo_sale_order() builds the real order — customer, billing/shipping addresses, product lines, delivery line and the SUMMER20 promo — then can auto-confirm and invoice.',mo:'sale.order',st:['draft','sale','done'],cfg:'Anna Mueller · Blue Widget ×2 @ 49.99 · DHL 5.99 · promo −10.00 · shop order flips draft→linked'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Info on success, Error on failure',biz:'Every import is logged for traceability. A failure leaves the shop order in failed state and raises an Error (or Critical) log.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error','Critical'],cfg:'ihub_info / ihub_error, linked to the shop.sale.order via related'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'The order-import cron is watched',biz:'Order import is business-critical — a watcher emails followers if this cron is ever disabled or deleted.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'A missed run means orders silently stop arriving in Odoo'} ]}, {id:'pi',label:'2 · Product import',dir:'Shopware → Odoo',scn:\"The Cotton T-Shirt (variants Red/M, Red/L) is imported from Shopware → an Odoo product template with variants , images and 19% tax.\",steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Cron fetches active/updated products',biz:'auto_import_shop_product_connector queries Shopware for active products changed since the last sync, with all their variants, options, media and tax.',mo:'import_products_shopware_shop()',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:' POST /api/search/product filtered on active + updatedAt'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Queue (V2 pipeline)',ex:'Big catalogues split into queued stages',biz:'For high volume the import is broken into ordered queue stages so a huge catalogue never runs as one giant transaction — each stage processes in batches.',mo:'ihub.queue',st:['Active','Archived'],cfg:'fetch_parents → fetch_variants → create_template → create_variants → download_images → set_pricing · batches of 100 / 50'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Map values',ex:\"property groups 'Color/Size' → attributes\",biz:'Shopware property groups become Odoo product attributes; the category trail and tax rate resolve through mappers.',mo:'ihub.mapper.item',st:['Automatic','Manual','Fail'],cfg:\"Color/Size → product.attribute · 'Apparel>Men' → product.category · 19% → account.tax\"}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create Odoo product',ex:'template + 2 variants, images, prices',biz:'A product.template plus one product.product per variant is created, images downloaded in parallel, and shop link records keep the Shopware UUIDs.',mo:'product.template · product.product',st:['draft','linked'],cfg:'SKUs TSHIRT-001-RED-M / -L · barcodes · gross 17.84 € · linked via shop.product.template'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Per-batch progress; image errors logged',biz:'Each batch and any image-download failure is logged so a partial import is fully visible.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],cfg:'High volume can generate many entries — Debug is for troubleshooting only'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'The product-import cron is watched',biz:'Alerts followers if the catalogue sync stops running.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'Email on deactivate / delete'} ]}, {id:'pe',label:'3 · Product export',dir:'Odoo → Shopware',scn:\"An Odoo Cotton T-Shirt is pushed to Shopware — property groups, price, tax, category, variants and images.\",steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Export on change or on demand',biz:'export_products() only runs on an active integration; it normalises templates and variants into one payload structure.',mo:'export_products_shopware_shop()',st:['Active only'],cfg:'Decorated @active_integration · handles template-only vs full-variant updates'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Background job',ex:'Batch payload built, images uploaded',biz:'The export runs asynchronously; images use a two-step create-then-upload so a large catalogue never blocks the UI.',mo:'ihub.job.thread',st:['In progress','Done','Partial','Failed'],cfg:'Images: POST /api/media then /_action/media/{id}/upload'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Map values',ex:'category → Shopware category; attrs upserted',biz:'Odoo values are translated the other way: category resolves via get_mapper_for_export and property groups are upserted in Shopware.',mo:'ihub.mapper.item (export)',st:['Automatic','Manual','Fail'],cfg:'property_group Color(Red,Blue) / Size(M,L) upserted before the product'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Push to Shopware',ex:'POST /api/_action/sync upsert product',biz:'The product, its variants (linked by parentId), price, tax, visibilities and category are sent in one sync call.',mo:'/api/_action/sync',st:[],cfg:'net/gross price · tax 19% · sales-channel visibilities · media linked via product_media'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Result logged; external UUIDs saved back',biz:'Success and failure are logged, and the new Shopware UUIDs are written back onto the shop records for future updates.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],cfg:'Shop records now hold external_id → next export updates instead of duplicating'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'The export cron is watched',biz:'Alerts followers if scheduled product export stops.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'Email on deactivate / delete'} ]}, {id:'se',label:'4 · Stock export',dir:'Odoo → Shopware',scn:\"Warehouse stock changes in Odoo → new stock levels pushed to Shopware, but only for products that actually changed.\",steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Daily cron, oldest exports first',biz:'auto_export_product_stock picks products ordered by their last export date, so nothing is starved.',mo:'export_stock()',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:'cron ir_cron_export_product_stock · ordered by last_export_stock_date'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Incremental check',ex:'Skip products whose stock is unchanged',biz:'Live qty_available is compared against the cached exported_qty per location; only real changes are sent, in batches of 250.',mo:'shop.product.location.stock',st:['changed','unchanged'],cfg:'_prepare_incremental_stock_export · cache refreshed whenever a stock.quant is written'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Push to Shopware',ex:'sync {id, stock} · e.g. {stock: 150}',biz:'Stock levels are upserted by product UUID. Products deleted in Shopware are detected and archived on the Odoo side.',mo:'/api/_action/sync',st:[],cfg:'pre-checks existence via POST /api/search/product · archives vanished products'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Update cache',ex:'exported_qty + timestamp written back',biz:'After a successful push the cache is updated so the next run can skip it — this is what makes the export incremental.',mo:'update_exported_stock()',st:[],cfg:'writes exported_qty and last_export_stock_date'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Counts logged',biz:'How many products were checked, sent and skipped is logged.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],cfg:'Connection failures become Error logs; the run can retry next cron'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'Stock cron is watched (critical)',biz:'Overselling risk if stock stops syncing — a watcher guards this cron closely.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'Followers emailed the moment it stops'} ]}, {id:'sh',label:'5 · Shipment export',dir:'Odoo → Shopware',scn:\"A delivery for order SW-10042 is validated in Odoo → the Shopware delivery is marked shipped with tracking DHL1234567890 .\",steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Picking validated (done) or daily cron',biz:'When a delivery is validated it is flagged to_be_exported_shop; a cron then exports all flagged pickings.',mo:'export_shipment_shopware_shop()',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:'cron ir_cron_export_shipment · @active_integration'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Determine status',ex:'ship · ship_partially · retour…',biz:'get_order_status compares shipped vs ordered quantities to decide whether this is a full ship, a partial ship, or a return.',mo:'get_order_status(picking)',st:['ship','ship_partially','retour','retour_partially'],cfg:'partial deliveries are handled correctly, not forced to full'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Push delivery state',ex:'delivery open → shipped',biz:'The Shopware delivery state machine is advanced via a dedicated action endpoint after reading the current delivery.',mo:'/_action/order_delivery/{id}/state/ship',st:['open','shipped'],cfg:'fetches the current delivery first · sendMail:false'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Push tracking',ex:'PATCH trackingCodes: [\"DHL1234567890\"]',biz:'The carrier tracking reference is written onto the Shopware delivery so the customer sees it.',mo:'/api/order-delivery/{id}',st:[],cfg:'appends to any existing tracking codes'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Logged; picking flagged exported',biz:'The export result is logged and the picking is marked so it is not re-sent.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],cfg:'to_be_exported_shop set back to false'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'The shipment cron is watched',biz:'Alerts followers if fulfilment stops flowing back to Shopware.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'Email on deactivate / delete'} ]}, {id:'ri',label:'6 · Return import',dir:'Shopware → Odoo',scn:\"A customer return for order SW-10042 comes back from Shopware → an incoming stock.picking in Odoo, plus an optional credit note.\",steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger',ex:'Cron fetches new returns',biz:'auto_import_shop_returns_connector pulls returns/RMAs raised in Shopware since the last sync.',mo:'import_order_returns_shopware_shop()',st:['Active','Inactive'],cfg:'cron ir_cron_import_shop_returns_connector '}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Stage as return',ex:'shop.order.return created (draft)',biz:'The return payload is stored as a staging record before it touches Odoo stock.',mo:'shop.order.return',st:['draft','linked'],cfg:'external_number + raw payload kept for audit'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Match & map',ex:'find the original sale.order + lines',biz:'The return is matched to the original Odoo order and each return line is matched to its product (creating it if allowed).',mo:'check_order_line_product()',st:['Automatic','Manual','Fail'],cfg:'links back to the sale.order imported in workflow 1'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create return',ex:'incoming picking + optional credit note',biz:'process_odoo_order_return creates an incoming stock.picking for the returned quantities and can reverse the invoice into a credit note.',mo:'stock.picking · account.move',st:['draft','linked'],cfg:'uses operation_type_id for returns · revert_invoice() raises the credit note'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log',ex:'Success / failure logged',biz:'The return processing outcome is logged, and the staging record flips draft→linked.',mo:'ihub.event.log',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],cfg:'a mismatch (e.g. qty exceeds delivered) raises an Error log'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Cronwatch',ex:'The returns cron is watched',biz:'Alerts followers if returns stop importing.',mo:'ihub_cronwatch.watcher',st:['Cron active','Cron down'],cfg:'Email on deactivate / delete'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|critical|down|archiv|cancel|retour|unchanged/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|active|linked|info|shipped|ship|changed|checked|success/.test(s)&&!/not checked/.test(s))return'ok';if(/progress|retry|partial|warning|manual|inactive|draft|open|not checked/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' States '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Under the hood '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' · '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "import-export-orders-products.html", "title": "Import &amp; Export — Orders &amp; Products", "text": "Import Export — Orders Products ← Back to Guide Hub Import Export — Orders Products A developer-level walk-through of how the Shopware connector imports products and orders into Odoo: every phase, every field, and what is mandatory versus optional. Grounded in the actual connector code. ← Back to Shopware guide Light / dark Print / PDF 1. The one idea to hold on to Every import happens in two stages . First the connector fetches raw JSON from the Shopware Admin API and parks it, untouched, in an intermediate \"shop\" record (its payload field). Then, separately, it processes that payload — mapping the pieces it needs into a real Odoo record. Why split it? Fetching is decoupled from mapping. If the mapping fails, the fetched data is safe and the record can be reprocessed; each stage runs on its own scheduled job; and every failure is written to the iHub log without losing anything. The two-stage import pipeline Shopware Admin API is fetched in stage one into a staging record holding the raw payload, then stage two processes it into a real Odoo record. Shopware Admin API Stage 1 Import / Fetch Staging record shop.product.template shop.sale.order holds raw payload Stage 2 Process Odoo record product.template sale.order ⏱ daily import cron ⏱ process cron 📝 every step → iHub log The colours (building blocks) Scheduled job (cron) Shopware API call Staging record (raw payload) Mapper (external ↔ Odoo) Odoo record created Filter / skip rule Log Words you'll meet Payload The full raw JSON Shopware sent, stored as-is on the staging record. Nothing is thrown away — mapping just reads what it needs from it. Staging record An in-between Odoo record ( shop.product.template / shop.sale.order ) that holds the payload before it becomes a real product or order. Mapper A saved link between a Shopware value (a tax, a category, an attribute) and its Odoo equivalent, so the connector knows which Odoo record to use. Mandatory Without it, the record is not created — the import stops for that item and waits for you to fix it. 2. Follow it step by step Pick a flow, then click any phase to see the business meaning, the exact method/endpoint in the code, the record states, and — where it matters — the field mapping with what's required. A real example runs through each flow. Required not created without it Conditional required only in some cases Optional filled if present 3. Field reference (quick tables) The same field detail, gathered in one place for when you just need to look something up. 4. What is deliberately NOT imported Orders skipped at fetch: orders in completed or cancelled state; orders that already have a shipped delivery; anything outside the date window ( createdAt since the last import). Products: only templates are queried ( parentId = null ); variants arrive nested as children . A record that is itself a child is skipped — its parent creates it. much. Consulting · Onboarding · Shopware ↔ Odoo import/export deep-dive. Built from the connector code ( much-order-shopware-integration · much-order-base-integration ). Use the buttons above to switch theme or print to PDF (the printout expands every phase). /* ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── WORKFLOWS — each step is a phase. Fields: m building-block colour class blk pill label (the building block) t phase title ex short technical identifier (method / endpoint / cron) biz plain-language explanation tech line shown under \"In the code\" st status chips fields [{o:odoo, s:shopware source, n:'req'|'cond'|'opt', k?:note}] cap caption for the field table cfg a note / tip ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */ const WF=[ {id:'pi',label:'Product import',dir:'Shopware → Odoo · daily',scn:'Example: the “Cotton T-Shirt” is added in Shopware with two variants, Red / M and Red / L . The next daily run brings it into Odoo as a product template with two variants.',steps:[ {m:'m-coral',blk:'Cron',t:'Daily import job fires',ex:'ir_cron_import_shop_product_connector',biz:'Once a day a scheduled action wakes up, works out the date to import from (the last import date), and calls import_products for each Shopware connector. Nothing for you to do.',tech:'auto_import_shop_product_connector() → import_products(from_date, to_date, update_existing)',st:['Automatic','Daily'],cfg:'You can also run it by hand from Integration Hub > Shopware > Import > Products for one product now.'}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Import sales channels',ex:'GET sales-channel',biz:'First it pulls the shop’s sales channels so imported products know where they are sold. Each one is upserted into a small helper table.',tech:'import_sales_channel_shopware() → upserts sw.sales_channel (ext_id, name, customerGroupId, paymentMethodId)',st:[],cfg:''}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Import taxes',ex:'action_import_taxes()',biz:'Taxes are fetched and matched to Odoo taxes before any product is created, because each product/variant needs a tax.',tech:'action_import_taxes() → account.tax (via mapper, name-based, \"(VAT incl.)\")',st:[],cfg:'If a tax can’t be matched later, the product still imports — the failure is only logged.'}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Query products',ex:'POST search/product',biz:'It asks Shopware for products that are new (or updated, if \"Update existing\" is on) since the last run. Only parent templates are requested; their variants come back nested. It pulls deep associations: properties/options, categories, children (variants), cover image and visibilities.',tech:'filter parentId = null · range on createdAt (or updatedAt if update_existing) · associations: properties, categories, children, cover→media, visibilities',st:['Standard','High volume'],cfg:'For big catalogues the \"High Volume\" background job splits this into phases so the system stays responsive.'}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Fetch category tree',ex:'POST search/category',biz:'For each product it resolves the category path (e.g. All / Clothing / T-Shirts) so it can be mapped to an Odoo product category.',tech:'_handle_prd_data_product_import() → search/category by categoryTree ids',st:[],cfg:'Odoo stores one category trail per product; the first trail is kept.'}, {m:'m-amber',blk:'Staging',t:'Stage the product',ex:'shop.product.template.create_multiple()',biz:'The raw JSON (product + included variants + category data) is bundled and saved on a staging record. Nothing is mapped yet — this just parks the data safely and commits it.',tech:'payload = json({data, included, category_data}) · external_id = Shopware product id · cr.commit()',st:[],cfg:'A product already staged but not yet processed just gets its payload refreshed, not duplicated.'}, {m:'m-purple',blk:'Mapper + record',t:'Process → build the Odoo product',ex:'create_odoo_product_from_shop()',biz:'Now the payload is translated. parse_from_shopware_json() reads the fields below and creates the product template, its variants, attribute lines, images, category and the mapper that links Shopware id ↔ Odoo id. If \"Update existing\" is on and the product already exists, it is updated instead.',tech:'parse_from_shopware_json() → ProductTemplate / Product dataclasses → product.template + product.product',st:['Created','Updated'], cap:'Template fields', fields:[ {o:'name',s:'name',n:'req',k:'Odoo cannot create a product without a name'}, {o:'description_sale',s:'description',n:'opt'}, {o:'categ_id',s:'categoryTree → mapper',n:'cond',k:'Required only if the product HAS categories; must resolve via a mapper or the import errors'}, {o:'image',s:'cover → media → url (downloaded)',n:'opt'}, {o:'attribute_lines',s:'children → options / property groups',n:'cond',k:'Auto mapper with no record = error; manual unmapped = warning + skipped'} ], cfg:'Example: the T-Shirt template gets name \"Cotton T-Shirt\", a Colour + Size attribute line, and category T-Shirts.'}, {m:'m-teal',blk:'Odoo record',t:'Variants created',ex:'product.product',biz:'Each Shopware child becomes an Odoo variant with its own price, code, barcode, weight, stock and image. These are the variant-level fields.',tech:'per variant in ProductTemplate.variants',st:[], cap:'Variant fields (per variant)', fields:[ {o:'list_price',s:'price[0].gross (variant, else template)',n:'req',k:'Always read'}, {o:'default_code',s:'productNumber',n:'opt'}, {o:'barcode',s:'ean (duplicates get -1, -2…)',n:'opt'}, {o:'weight',s:'weight (default 0.0)',n:'opt'}, {o:'qty (stock)',s:'availableStock',n:'opt'}, {o:'taxes_id',s:'tax relation → mapper',n:'cond',k:'Non-blocking: logs an error if unmapped'}, {o:'sales_channel_ids',s:'visibilities → salesChannelId',n:'opt'}, {o:'attributes',s:'variant options (e.g. Colour=Red, Size=M)',n:'cond'} ], cfg:'Example: Red/M → default_code SW-TSHIRT-RM, and Red/L → SW-TSHIRT-RL, each with its own price and stock.'}, {m:'m-gray',blk:'Log',t:'Result logged',ex:'ihub_error / commit',biz:'Success is committed per product; a failure rolls back just that one product and writes an error to the iHub log — the rest keep going.',tech:'try/except per product · self.env.cr.commit() · ihub_error(summary, details, related)',st:['Success','Error'],cfg:'Image-download problems are warnings only and never stop the import.'} ]}, {id:'oi',label:'Order import',dir:'Shopware → Odoo · daily',scn:'Example: order SW-10042 from Anna Mueller — 2× “Cotton T-Shirt” Red / M, paid by credit card, shipping to Berlin. It comes in as an Odoo sales order ready to pick.',steps:[ {m:'m-coral',blk:'Cron',t:'Daily import job fires',ex:'ir_cron_import_shop_orders_connector',biz:'Once a day the order-import scheduled action calls import_orders for each connector, from the last import date to now.',tech:'auto_import_shop_orders_connector() → import_orders(from_date, to_date)',st:['Automatic','Daily'],cfg:'Manual: Integration Hub > Shopware > Import > Orders, paste the order id, Execute.'}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Import taxes',ex:'action_import_taxes()',biz:'As with products, taxes are matched first so order lines can carry the right tax.',tech:'action_import_taxes()',st:[],cfg:''}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Query orders',ex:'POST search/order',biz:'It builds a request with all the associations it needs (deliveries, shipping method, currency, line items, billing + shipping addresses, transactions, payment method, state) and asks for orders in the date window. Completed and cancelled orders are excluded up front.',tech:'generate_order_import_request_body() · default filters exclude state completed & cancelled · range on createdAt',st:[],cfg:''}, {m:'m-pink',blk:'Filter',t:'Skip already-shipped orders',ex:'_order_has_shipped_delivery()',biz:'After fetching, any order that already has a delivery marked \"shipped\" is skipped — those are handled elsewhere. (Done after fetching because Shopware can’t reliably filter on nested delivery state.)',tech:'_order_has_shipped_delivery(order, included) → continue',st:[],cfg:''}, {m:'m-amber',blk:'Staging',t:'Stage the order',ex:'shop.sale.order.create_multiple()',biz:'Each surviving order is saved as a staging record holding the raw payload, with state Imported (draft). Then committed.',tech:'state = draft (\"Imported\") · external_number = orderNumber · external_id = order id · cr.commit()',st:['Imported'],cfg:'An order staged before but never processed just gets its payload refreshed.'}, {m:'m-blue',blk:'Shopware API',t:'Acknowledge back in Shopware',ex:'POST _action/order/{id}/state/process',biz:'It tells Shopware the order is now being handled by moving it to \"In progress\" (no email sent).',tech:'set_imported_order_status_in_progress() · payload {documentIds:[], sendMail:false}',st:['In progress'],cfg:''}, {m:'m-purple',blk:'Mapper + record',t:'Process → build the Odoo order',ex:'process_order_creation() → to_odoo_sale_order()',biz:'parse_from_shopware_order() translates the payload; then the sales order is created with its partners, lines, payment term, pricelist and carrier. On success the staging record flips to Linked; on error to Failed (and rolls back).',tech:'to_odoo_sale_order() → sale.order · state draft → linked / failed',st:['Imported','Linked','Failed'], cap:'Order header fields', fields:[ {o:'name',s:'orderNumber (+ optional prefix)',n:'req'}, {o:'date_order',s:'createdAt',n:'req'}, {o:'pricelist_id',s:'currency.shortName',n:'req'}, {o:'payment_term_id',s:'paymentMethod.name → mapper',n:'req',k:'HARD BLOCKER — no mapped term, no order'}, {o:'warehouse / company / user / team',s:'connector configuration',n:'req',k:'Comes from the connector, not Shopware'} ], cfg:'Example: SW-10042 → order name \"SW-10042\", credit-card payment term mapped, EUR pricelist.'}, {m:'m-teal',blk:'Odoo record',t:'Partners & lines',ex:'res.partner · sale.order.line',biz:'Three addresses become partners (contact, invoice, shipping) and each Shopware line item becomes an order line. A line’s product MUST already exist in Odoo (or auto-create, if enabled) — otherwise that line, and the whole order, is not created.',tech:'get_partner_ids() · get_order_lines() · check_order_line_product()',st:[], cap:'Line & partner fields', fields:[ {o:'order line → product',s:'productId (template = payload.parentId)',n:'req',k:'HARD BLOCKER — unmapped & auto-create off = ValidationError'}, {o:'product_uom_qty',s:'quantity',n:'req'}, {o:'price_unit',s:'unitPrice',n:'req'}, {o:'tax_id',s:'price.calculatedTaxes[].taxRate → mapper',n:'opt'}, {o:'partner name',s:'firstName + lastName',n:'req'}, {o:'street / city / zip / country',s:'address fields (street, city, zipcode, country.iso)',n:'opt'}, {o:'email',s:'orderCustomer.email (contact only)',n:'opt'} ], cfg:'Example: Anna Mueller becomes the customer; 1 line \"Cotton T-Shirt Red/M\" ×2 at its unit price + a shipping line.'}, {m:'m-coral',blk:'Cron',t:'Left-over drafts get processed',ex:'ir_cron_process_shop_orders',biz:'Besides processing right after import, a second daily job sweeps up any orders still in Imported (draft) state and tries again — oldest first, up to 300 at a time.',tech:'auto_process_shop_orders() → search state=draft, order_id=False, limit 300',st:['Imported','Linked'],cfg:'This is why a fixed mapping is picked up automatically next run — just fix the mapper and wait, or reprocess.'}, {m:'m-gray',blk:'Log',t:'Result logged',ex:'ihub_info / ihub_error',biz:'A success writes an info log linking the Shopware number to the new Odoo order; a failure writes an error and the record stays Failed for you to inspect.',tech:'ihub_info(...) on success · ihub_error(...) + state=failed on error',st:['Linked','Failed'],cfg:'Optionally the order is auto-confirmed and an invoice created, per the connector settings.'} ]}, {id:'ex',label:'Exports (overview)',dir:'Odoo → Shopware',scn:'Exports run the other way — Odoo is the source. These are summarised here; the step-by-step business guide lives in the main Shopware Connector guide. Field-level export detail can be added next.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',blk:'You trigger',t:'Product export',ex:'Products > action > Export to connectors',biz:'You push an Odoo product to Shopware from the product action menu (or List view > Actions for a batch). Turn on \"Export on save\" to make it automatic on every save. The first export stores the Shopware id so later exports update instead of duplicating.',tech:'much_order_shopware_product_export',st:['Manual','On save'],cfg:''}, {m:'m-coral',blk:'Cron',t:'Stock export',ex:'daily scheduled action',biz:'Every day Odoo sends current available stock for synced products to Shopware, so availability stays correct. Only changed numbers are sent; products deleted in Shopware get archived in Odoo.',tech:'much_order_shopware_stock_quant_export',st:['Automatic','Daily'],cfg:''}, {m:'m-amber',blk:'On validation',t:'Shipment export',ex:'on delivery validation',biz:'When the warehouse validates a delivery, Shopware is marked shipped and the tracking number is sent — immediately if \"Auto-export Shipment\" is on, otherwise on the next daily run. Partial shipments are supported.',tech:'much_order_shopware_stock_picking_export',st:['Immediate','Daily'],cfg:''}, {m:'m-pink',blk:'On validation',t:'Return export',ex:'on return receipt validation',biz:'Validating a return receipt updates the matching Shopware order to returned (partial returns supported). Refunds/credit notes are a separate step in Odoo Invoicing.',tech:'order return import/export flow',st:['Returned'],cfg:''} ]} ]; /* ── rendering ── */ let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|wrong|bad/.test(s))return'bad';if(/link|done|active|confirm|automatic|immediate|ok|success|created|in progress|returned/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|daily|manual|inactive|choice|high volume|standard|imported|updated|on save/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st&&st.length?' States '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function fieldTable(fields,cap){ if(!fields||!fields.length)return''; let h=' '+(cap?' '+cap+' ':'')+' Odoo field From Shopware Need '; fields.forEach(f=>{ const label={req:'Required',cond:'Conditional',opt:'Optional'}[f.n]||''; h+=' '+f.o+' '+f.s+(f.k?' '+f.k+' ':'')+' '+label+' '; }); return h+' '; } function detHtml(s){ return ' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' ' +(s.tech?' In the code '+s.tech+' ':'') +chipHtml(s.st) +fieldTable(s.fields,s.cap) +(s.cfg?' 💡 Note '+s.cfg+' ':''); } function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.blk+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=detHtml(w.steps[sel]); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); /* ── handout (doc) view: every phase expanded ── */ function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' · '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.blk+' '+s.ex+' '+detHtml(s).replace(/^ .*? /,'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc(); /* ── reference tables (flatten all field tables) ── */ function renderRef(){ const box=document.getElementById('reftables');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ w.steps.forEach(s=>{ if(s.fields&&s.fields.length){ h+=fieldTable(s.fields,(w.label+' — '+(s.cap||s.t))); } }); }); box.innerHTML=h; } renderRef();"}, {"href": "links.html", "title": "Testing Environments &amp; Useful Links", "text": "Testing Environments Useful Links ← Back to Guide Hub Testing environments useful links The web tools you'll use as a new hire — what each one is for, and when to reach for it. Light / dark Print / PDF Good to know who to ask much. Consulting · Onboarding · Testing environments links const LINKS=[ {ic:'🧪',color:'m-teal',name:'Shoovels',url:'https://app.shoovels.com/', desc:'Our shared, always-on testing Odoo environment. Path once you\\'re in: Shoovels → Select product → QA Testing .', hire:'This is your day-to-day home for manual QA. Start here to test features in a shared Odoo without setting anything up yourself.', note:''}, {ic:'🚀',color:'m-blue',name:'Odoo.sh — Projects',url:'https://www.odoo.sh/project', desc:'For starting fresh projects. Use it when you want a clean codebase from a specific Odoo version.', hire:'Reach for this when you need a clean, isolated environment built from scratch for one version — e.g. to reproduce something without our customisations getting in the way.', note:'⚠️ Projects are deleted after 30 days — don\\'t keep anything important only here.'}, {ic:'🪞',color:'m-blue',name:'Product-odoo-19-tests (staging)',url:'https://product-odoo-19-tests-staging-qa.odoo-host.dev/odoo', desc:'A live reflection of the Product-odoo-19-tests branch — the Odoo 19 version of our version-specific staging hosts.', hire:'The hosted staging that mirrors our Odoo 19 test repo. Use it to see our actual test code running on Odoo 19, exactly as it is in the branch.', note:''}, {ic:'🪞',color:'m-coral',name:'Product-odoo-18-tests (staging)',url:'https://product-odoo-18-tests-staging-qa.odoo-host.dev/odoo', desc:'A live reflection of the Product-odoo-18-tests branch — and the remaining versions have their own equivalent hosts.', hire:'The hosted staging that mirrors our test repo. Use it to see our actual test code running for a given version, exactly as it is in the branch.', note:''}, {ic:'🪞',color:'m-amber',name:'Order-shopware-tests (staging) — v17',url:'https://order-shopware-tests-staging-qa.odoo-host.dev/web#action=menu&cids=1', desc:'A live reflection of the order-shopware-tests branch — our Odoo 17 testing environment (Order Base / Shopware test host).', hire:'The hosted staging for the Odoo 17 line. Use it to test our code on Odoo 17, exactly as it is in the branch.', note:''}, {ic:'🤖',color:'m-gray',name:'Runbot',url:'https://runbot.odoo.com/', desc:'Every Odoo environment / version as vanilla (plain, unmodified Odoo).', hire:'Odoo\\'s official build farm. Great for checking how standard Odoo behaves for any version — a reference point when you need to know whether something is our customisation or standard behaviour.', note:'🔑 Login: admin / admin. You can do anything here — except delete.'} ]; document.getElementById('links').innerHTML=LINKS.map(l=>` ${l.ic} ${l.name} ${l.url} Open ↗ What it is ${l.desc} When to use it (new hire) ${l.hire} ${l.note?` ${l.note} `:''} `).join(''); const NOTES=[ {ic:'🐚',color:'m-teal',t:'Shoovels', html:'The Odoo container lives inside Shoovels QA . If the server is down, ask in #dev-ops-questions on Slack.'}, {ic:'📦',color:'m-blue',t:'Odoo Container', html:'The Odoo container runs inside Shoovels QA . If the server is down, post in #dev-ops-questions — or reach out directly to:', people:['Robinson Pompeu','David Akalugo','Frank Mogbuk']}, {ic:'🎛️',color:'m-coral',t:'Odoo Studio — do customers use it?', html:' We don\\'t recommend it. We normally prefer to be the ones who add the features and do the coding, rather than letting customers build with Studio.'}, {ic:'✉️',color:'m-amber',t:'Email testing', html:' Emails don\\'t work in the testing environment. The current way to test email is an Odoo.sh replica (using the main branch).'} ]; document.getElementById('notes').innerHTML=NOTES.map(n=>` ${n.ic} ${n.t} ${n.html} ${n.people?` ${n.people.map(p=>` 👤 ${p} `).join('')} `:''} `).join('');"}, {"href": "mcp-claude-odoo.html", "title": "AI Assistant (MCP) — Claude ↔ Odoo", "text": "AI Assistant (MCP) — Claude ↔ Odoo ← Back to Guide Hub AI Assistant (MCP) — connect Claude to Odoo What MCP is, why we use it, and the full setup to let Claude read act on your Odoo data. Written for a junior technical joiner. Light / dark Print / PDF 1. What is MCP, in plain words MCP = Model Context Protocol. It's a technical standard introduced by Anthropic (the company behind Claude). It's a common way to connect an AI assistant — Claude, but also other AI tools — to an external system , so the AI can actually read and work with that system's data . Claude Desktop the AI assistant ↔ MCP server runs on your computer ↔ Odoo your data + code Example: connected to Odoo, you can ask \"give me all January invoices above €10,000\" and Claude pulls the data and summarises it for you. 2. Why we set this up Today you have the code and the test environments — but the AI can't see the data . MCP adds that data layer, so Claude can combine code + real data . That's far better for investigating issues (e.g. \"explain why this code was written this way, using the code and the data\" ). Permissions. much. also publishes a free Odoo Marketplace module that sits on top of this and controls what the AI can touch (e.g. Sales but not Accounting; read-only vs create/update). That's configured on the Odoo side. Through MCP, you get exactly the permissions your own Odoo user already has in the UI — nothing more. 💡 Which client? The MCP server runs locally (on the machine where Claude Desktop is installed), not on the Odoo server. Claude Desktop is recommended for releases (it holds reusable skills — pre-made prompts). Any MCP-compatible client is configured in a similar way. 3. Set up the AI assistant with Claude Desktop 1 Install UV on your computer 📍 Terminal macOS $ curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh Copy Windows powershell -c \"irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex\" Copy 💡 UV gives you uvx , which Claude will use to run the Odoo MCP server. 2 Find the uvx path 📍 Terminal You'll need the full path in the config later. $ which uvx Copy Usually returns something like /Users/yourusername/.local/bin/uvx . Copy it. 3 Restart your terminal 📍 Terminal Close and reopen your terminal so the new uv is on your PATH. 4 Open the Claude Desktop config 📍 Claude Desktop → User ▸ Settings ▸ Developer Click Edit Config to open claude_desktop_config.json . Open it in VS Code so the JSON stays correctly formatted. 5 Add the odoo server to mcpServers 📍 claude_desktop_config.json Add (or merge) this block, then fill in your own values: Copy { \"mcpServers\": { \"odoo\": { \"command\": \"YOUR_UVX_PATH\", \"args\": [\"mcp-server-odoo\"], \"env\": { \"ODOO_URL\": \"YOUR_ODOO_URL\", \"ODOO_DB\": \"YOUR_ODOO_DB\", \"ODOO_USER\": \"YOUR_ODOO_USER_EMAIL\", \"ODOO_API_KEY\": \"YOUR_ODOO_API_KEY\" } } } } Values: command = the uvx path from step 2 · ODOO_URL = your Odoo instance URL (mind the trailing slash) · ODOO_DB = database name (optional, auto-detected if omitted) · ODOO_USER = your Odoo login email · ODOO_API_KEY = the key from the next step. 6 Generate your Odoo API key 📍 Odoo → Settings → Users → (your user) → Account Security Click New API Key (you may need to confirm your password first). Name it e.g. Claude Desktop , set an expiry (~1 year), and copy the key into ODOO_API_KEY . 💡 A key is the recommended auth. Alternatively you can use ODOO_USER + ODOO_PASSWORD instead of a key. 7 Optional trick — grant read access to everything you can see 📍 Same config, inside env Because the exact Odoo-side permission config may be unknown, you can add this line so Claude can read anything your user already has access to. Add it right after ODOO_API_KEY (watch the JSON commas!): Copy \"ODOO_YOLO\": \"read\" This only lets the AI do what you are already allowed to do — it doesn't grant new permissions. 8 Save restart Claude Desktop 📍 Claude Desktop Save the file and fully restart Claude Desktop. 9 Check the status 📍 Settings ▸ Developer Running ✅ = success, start asking Claude about your Odoo data. Failed ❌ = click View Logs , copy the text, and paste it to Claude — it'll help you troubleshoot. 4. Environment variables Variable Required Description ODOO_URL Yes Your Odoo instance URL. ODOO_API_KEY Yes* API key (recommended auth method). ODOO_USER Yes* Login email (with ODOO_PASSWORD , if not using a key). ODOO_PASSWORD Yes* Password (if not using a key). ODOO_DB No Database name (auto-detected if omitted). * Use either ODOO_API_KEY , or both ODOO_USER and ODOO_PASSWORD . Remote / web clients. Add \"--transport\", \"streamable-http\", \"--port\", \"8000\" to args and connect the client to http://localhost:8000/mcp/ . 5. Verify the scheduled action In Odoo, go to Settings ▸ Technical ▸ Automation ▸ Scheduled Actions and confirm MCP Log Cleanup exists and is active. It runs once a day and deletes log entries older than the Log Retention (days) setting (nothing is deleted if retention is 0 ). ✅ You're ready. You can now query and manage your Odoo data through your AI assistant — within the permissions your user has. much. Consulting · Onboarding · Connecting Claude to Odoo via MCP · Based on the \"MCP server for Odoo\" documentation and the setup session with Lou Prevot. function cp(id,btn){ var el=document.getElementById(id); var txt=el.querySelector('.copy') ? el.textContent.replace(btn.textContent,'').trim() : el.textContent; navigator.clipboard.writeText(txt).then(function(){var o=btn.textContent;btn.textContent='Copied!';setTimeout(function(){btn.textContent=o;},1200);}); }"}, {"href": "much-datev-training-german-accounting.html", "title": "much. DATEV — German Accounting, Applied", "text": "much. DATEV — German Accounting, Applied ← Back to Guide Hub much . DATEV New Joiner Handbook · Add-on chapter · Internal German Accounting, Applied The accounting ideas from the Odoo handbook change shape when they hit DATEV. This chapter is the mental model that makes the export files make sense — only the parts that matter for DATEV. much. GmbH · Odoo 19 · SKR03/04 · slots in after Chapter 3 (Vocabulary) 🧠 The one big idea Odoo thinks in balanced entries and net lines . DATEV thinks in single gross rows where a small tax key does the VAT maths. The export doesn't translate word-for-word — it collapses each Odoo entry into DATEV's one-row-per-booking, gross-plus-key shape. Learn that one shift and everything downstream clicks. § German Accounting, Applied to Odoo First the German-bookkeeping baseline Odoo already gives you, then the four things that change shape once that data has to go to DATEV. 0 · The German baseline — what Odoo already gives you Before anything DATEV-specific, these are the German-bookkeeping fundamentals. In Odoo they come from the German localization ( l10n_de plus the SKR chart) — you mostly configure them, you don't build them. The job here is knowing where each one lives: German concept What it is Where it lives in Odoo Doppelte Buchführung · Soll / Haben Double-entry bookkeeping — every posting has equal debits (Soll) and credits (Haben). Every account.move ; Odoo enforces the balance. Accounting → Journal Entries. SKR03 / SKR04 · Sachkonten The two standard German charts of accounts; every G/L account is a numbered Konto . Installed by the localization. Accounting → Configuration → Chart of Accounts . Debitoren / Kreditoren Customers and vendors kept as their own numbered sub-accounts (Personenkonten), not just contacts. Customers / Vendors ( res.partner ) with receivable/payable accounts. Umsatzsteuer / Vorsteuer Output VAT on sales and input VAT on purchases — 19% standard, 7% reduced — feeding the periodic USt-Voranmeldung . Taxes ( account.tax ). Accounting → Configuration → Taxes . Belegprinzip · GoBD “Keine Buchung ohne Beleg” — no entry without a document; GoBD mandates retention immutability of records. PDF attachments on invoices; posted entries are locked . 🧑‍💼 Why this leads into DATEV: under GoBD the official books live with the Steuerberater in DATEV, not in Odoo. Odoo keeps this baseline cleanly — which is exactly what lets it hand the data over. Everything below is where that handover forces Odoo's data into DATEV's shape. 1 · One booking = one row (not a balanced entry) In Odoo every posting is a balanced journal entry : two or more lines whose debits (Soll) equal credits (Haben). DATEV's Buchungsstapel is different — each business transaction is a single row carrying an amount, a Soll/Haben letter, a Konto and a Gegenkonto . DATEV fills in the other side automatically. So the export can't copy Odoo's lines one-to-one. For each entry it fixes one account as the Gegenkonto (the move's main account — for a sales invoice, the customer's Debitor), skips that main-account line and the separate tax line, and turns each remaining ordinary line into one gross row booked against that Gegenkonto. One Odoo invoice becomes one DATEV row A balanced three-line Odoo journal entry for a 119 euro invoice collapses into a single gross DATEV Buchungsstapel row: Umsatz 119,00, Haben, Konto 8400, Gegenkonto 10001, BU 9. In Odoo — one balanced journal entry (account.move) 10001 · Debitor — Anna Müller Soll 119,00 main → Gegenkonto 8400 · Umsatzerlöse Haben 100,00 becomes the row 1776 · Umsatzsteuer 19% Haben 19,00 tax line → skipped Soll 119,00 = Haben 100,00 + 19,00 — balanced collapses to one gross row + tax key In DATEV — one row (Buchungsstapel) Umsatz S/H Konto Gegenkonto BU-Schlüssel 119,00 H 8400 10001 9 Gross incl. VAT · DATEV derives the €19 from BU 9 and books it to the tax account itself. A €119 sales invoice: three balanced Odoo lines → one gross DATEV row. The Debitor is the fixed Gegenkonto ; the revenue line becomes the Konto ; the VAT line is dropped because BU 9 recreates it in DATEV. 🔎 In the code: the row is assembled in much_account_datev_csv_export/models/core/account_move.py — _get_line_match_values() sets account (Konto), counteraccount (Gegenkonto) and letter = \"S\" if aml.balance 0 else \"H\" (Soll/Haben). _should_ignore_line() drops the main-account line, tax lines, and any line where debit == credit . 2 · DATEV books gross — a tax key does the VAT In Odoo a taxed invoice has a net revenue line plus a separate tax line. DATEV usually does neither : the row carries the gross amount (net + VAT together, the Umsatz column) and you tag it with a BU-Schlüssel (Buchungsschlüssel / Steuerschlüssel). DATEV reads the key, works out the VAT, and posts it to the correct tax account on its own — no explicit VAT line. Our export writes the tax-included amount to Umsatz ( _get_gross_value() returns abs(amount) ) and puts the Odoo tax's DATEV code into the BU column ( account.tax.l10n_de_datev_code , via _get_code_correction() ). The handful of keys you'll actually see: BU-Schlüssel Means Amount in the row 9 19% domestic VAT (Umsatzsteuer / Vorsteuer) Gross — DATEV splits out the 19% 40 The control key — “no automatic tax on this line” Gross, but DATEV adds no VAT 94 §13b reverse charge (see below) Net — the exception 💡 Key 40 is the safety default. When a line has no mappable tax, the export falls back to the configured DATEV Key — control_datev_key , default \"40\" in much_account_datev_base — so DATEV never invents VAT we didn't intend. 3 · The exception: §13b reverse charge is net Under §13b UStG (reverse charge) — typically an EU or foreign supplier — the German buyer , not the supplier, owes the VAT. The supplier invoices net , so the DATEV row for a reverse-charge booking (BU-Schlüssel 94 ) carries the net amount, and DATEV books both the input- and output-VAT itself. This is the one place the “DATEV is gross” rule flips, and getting it wrong is expensive: treat a §13b row as gross and you divide an amount that was never grossed-up — understating the expense and both VAT lines and leaving a suspense line (ticket #55495 ). ⚠️ How the code knows: a reverse-charge tax is recognised by its invoice tax repartition factors netting to zero — an input-VAT line cancelled by an output-VAT line. _is_reverse_charge_tax() in much_account_datev_csv_import/wizards/base/import_datev_wizard.py checks len(tax_reps) = 2 and abs(sum(factor_percent)) 1e-6 , and the importer then skips the ÷(1 + rate) it applies to normal gross codes like BU 9. 4 · Account numbers: Sachkonten, Personenkonten length DATEV numbers everything, and the digit length is not cosmetic — it decides which number is a G/L account and which is a customer/vendor. 📒 Sachkonto (G/L account) Fixed length — 4 digits by default (min 4), set once and matched to your SKR chart. Config: length_general_accounts . Example: 8400 revenue. 👤 Personenkonto (Debitor / Kreditor) Always one digit longer than the G/L length, so it sits “under” the G/L range. Config: length_personal_accounts . Example: 10001 Debitor. That +1 rule is why a 4-digit chart gives 5-digit customer/vendor numbers. Debitoren (customers) are auto-assigned from 10000 , Kreditoren (vendors) from 70000 — the l10n_de_datev_identifier_customer and l10n_de_datev_identifier fields on the partner. 🇩🇪 SKR03 vs SKR04 is just which chart of accounts you picked at company setup — SKR03 is process-/expense-oriented (the older, very common one), SKR04 mirrors the financial statements. Our modules don't choose it; they only need Length General Accounts to match whichever chart the company uses, or the numbers won't line up in DATEV. Under the hood — the identifiers Same concepts, engineer altitude. Everything below is real and greppable. Concept Identifier Where One gross row per line _build_datev_export_line() · _should_ignore_line() csv_export/models/core/account_move.py Konto / Gegenkonto / Soll-Haben account · counteraccount · soll_haben same, _get_line_match_values() Gross amount (Umsatz) _get_gross_value() → abs(amount) same file BU-Schlüssel from the tax _get_code_correction() → tax_ids[0].l10n_de_datev_code same file Control key (default 40) control_datev_key (default \"40\" ) datev_base/models/core/res_config_settings.py §13b detection (net, not gross) _is_reverse_charge_tax() csv_import/wizards/base/import_datev_wizard.py Account lengths (+1 rule) length_general_accounts (≥4) · length_personal_accounts datev_base/models/core/res_config_settings.py Debitor / Kreditor numbers l10n_de_datev_identifier_customer · l10n_de_datev_identifier datev_base/models/core/res_partner.py Add-on chapter for the much. DATEV Integration handbook · much. GmbH Slots in after Chapter 3 (DATEV Vocabulary) · built on the standard German localization ( l10n_de ) ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){ if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')} }); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})});"}, {"href": "much-datev-training.html", "title": "much. DATEV Integration — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "much. DATEV Integration — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub much . DATEV New Joiner Handbook · Internal Odoo → DATEV Integration How our much. DATEV modules connect Odoo accounting to DATEV — explained for someone new to both Odoo and German bookkeeping. much. GmbH · Odoo 19 · SKR03/04 Chapters 1 What is DATEV? 2 The much. Modules 3 DATEV Vocabulary 4 Configuration 5 Debitor Kreditor 6 Export: Buchungsstapel (CSV) 7 Export: CSV + PDF Bills 8 Export: XML (Belege) 9 Import from DATEV 10 DATEV API (DATEVconnect) 11 Buttons Status Cheat-Sheet ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome — start here This handbook builds on the Odoo Accounting Invoicing handbook. If invoices, journal entries, debits/credits and reconciliation are still fuzzy, read that one first — everything here assumes you know what a journal entry is. Here we cover the piece that's specific to Germany and to us : getting Odoo's accounting data into DATEV , the software our clients' tax advisors (Steuerberater) use. These are the much. DATEV modules our team builds and maintains. 🧠 The one big idea In Germany, the Steuerberater (tax advisor) does the official books in DATEV . Odoo is where the business actually works day-to-day. So Odoo needs to hand its bookings over to DATEV in DATEV's exact format. That handover is the only thing these modules do — three ways: download a file , import a file , or push automatically over the DATEV API . 1 What is DATEV? The accounting standard at the heart of German bookkeeping. DATEV is a large German cooperative that makes the accounting and tax software used by almost every German Steuerberater (tax advisor) and many companies. When a German business keeps its books, the numbers almost always end up in DATEV so the tax advisor can review them, file taxes, and prepare the annual accounts. A typical setup: the company runs Odoo (sales, invoices, bills, bank), and its tax advisor runs DATEV. Every month the company's bookings must travel from Odoo to DATEV. Historically that meant printing lists or copying files by hand. Our modules automate it. 🏢 In Odoo The business records invoices, bills, payments and bank entries — the daily work. 🧑‍💼 In DATEV The Steuerberater reviews the bookings, files VAT, and prepares official year-end accounts. ℹ️ Why not just use one system? German tax advisors are deeply standardised on DATEV, and the law/audit trail lives there. So the practical answer is: keep working in Odoo, but speak DATEV's language when handing data over. 2 The much. DATEV Modules Six modules that layer on top of Odoo's standard German localization ( l10n_de ). You don't need all of them — you enable what a client needs. Module What it does much. DATEV: Base The foundation. Adds the DATEV settings tab, the Debitor/Kreditor numbers on customers vendors, and the shared Download DATEV File + Download Bills tools. CSV Export Exports Odoo journal entries partner master data as DATEV CSV files (the Buchungsstapel , EXTF format), optionally zipped with invoice PDFs. CSV Import Imports DATEV booking files (EXTF CSV) back into Odoo as draft journal entries. XML Export Exports individual invoices/bills as DATEV XML records ( Belegverwaltung online ) so DATEV can book them automatically. API Integration Connects Odoo directly to the DATEV cloud (DATEVconnect) so bookings, master data PDFs push automatically — no manual files. SDD Change Tracking Small auto-installed helper: watches SEPA Direct Debit mandate changes and flags that partner for DATEV re-export. 💡 Think of it as a ladder: Base is required, CSV Export is the workhorse, and you add Import , XML or the API depending on how the client's Steuerberater wants to receive the data. 3 DATEV Vocabulary DATEV is German and uses its own terms. Learn these eight and everything else clicks. (Full list in the glossary .) DATEV term Plain English = In Odoo Berater-Nr. (Consultant No.) Number identifying the tax firm Set on the Company Mandant-Nr. (Client No.) Number identifying this client's file Set on the Company SKR03 / SKR04 The two standard German charts of accounts Chart of Accounts Sachkonto A general-ledger account Account (in the CoA) Debitor A customer sub-account (range 10000+) Customer + DATEV number Kreditor A vendor sub-account (range 70000+) Vendor + DATEV number Soll / Haben Debit / Credit ( S / H ) Debit / Credit Buchungsstapel A batch of bookings (the CSV file) Posted journal entries 🇩🇪 EXTF is the name of DATEV's file format. The first line of every DATEV CSV is the \"EXTF header\" — a fingerprint telling DATEV the firm, client, period and file type. You never type it; the module builds it. 4 Configuration Set up once per client. Get these right and exports \"just work\"; get them wrong and DATEV rejects the file. Step 1 — Consultant Client numbers (required!) The Consultant Number (Berater-Nr.) and Client Number (Mandanten-Nr.) live on the Company (standard German-localization fields, not our custom tab). Every export refuses to run without them — you'll see: \"Please configure the DATEV consultant number and client number in the company form settings.\" Settings → Users Companies → Companies → (your company) → DATEV fields Step 2 — The \"DATEV Export\" settings tab Everything else is on our tab: Settings → DATEV Export Setting What it means Length General Accounts (default 4) How many digits your G/L accounts (Sachkonten) have. Must match your SKR chart. Minimum 4. Personal Accounts → Direct Booking How customer/vendor sub-accounts are numbered. When on, reveals Length Personal Accounts . Receivable Format (default 10000) Starting number for auto-assigned Debitor (customer) accounts — DATEV convention. Payable Format (default 70000) Starting number for auto-assigned Kreditor (vendor) accounts. DATEV partner number generation When Debitor/Kreditor numbers get assigned: On posting of first entry (default) or During export . Edit Columns / Edit Partner Columns Fine-tune exactly which DATEV columns appear in the bookings CSV and the partner CSV. Defaults are the standard DATEV layout — rarely changed. KOST1 / KOST2 (if Analytic on) Map two Odoo analytic plans to DATEV cost centres ( Kostenstellen ). Set once — can't be changed later. G/L Export Batch Size (default 1000) Max journal entries per file when exporting big date ranges. Export Encoding Latin-1 (CP1252) or UTF-8 . Default CP1252 — which is what DATEV expects. DATEV Key (default 40) The BU-Schlüssel (booking key) used to switch off DATEV's automatic tax calculation on a line. ⚠️ SKR03 vs SKR04 is chosen when the company is set up (via Odoo's German localization) — there's no separate selector in our tab. Length General Accounts must match that chart , or account numbers won't line up in DATEV. 5 Debitor Kreditor (Customer/Vendor numbers) DATEV doesn't book against \"the customer\" — it books against a numbered sub-account . Each customer and vendor needs a DATEV number. ⬇️ Debitor = Customer Field DATEV Customer Identifier . Auto-assigned from the Receivable Format range (10000, 10001…). ⬆️ Kreditor = Vendor Field DATEV Supplier Identifier . Auto-assigned from the Payable Format range (70000, 70001…). On any customer/vendor you'll find these fields (under the Set DATEV Identifier permission). Leave a number blank and the system assigns the next free one automatically; or paste an existing DATEV number to keep continuity with what the Steuerberater already uses. Numbers must be unique. Actions on a partner Generate Customer Identifier / Generate Supplier Identifier — assign a number now. Download DATEV CSV — export just this partner's master-data record. Exported to DATEV / DATEV Export Date — read-only status. A DATEV Update Required flag re-includes the partner in the next export whenever a watched field (name, address, VAT, bank, receivable/payable account…) changes — so master data in DATEV stays current. 🔁 This is what the SDD Change Tracking module extends: change a customer's SEPA mandate and it automatically flags them for re-export, so DATEV always has the latest bank/mandate details. 6 Export: Buchungsstapel (CSV) The workhorse. Turn Odoo's posted bookings into a DATEV booking batch the Steuerberater can import. What's exported: your posted journal entries — customer invoices, credit notes, vendor bills, refunds, and bank/cash/general entries — as a DATEV Buchungsstapel CSV. The first row is the EXTF header (firm, client, fiscal-year start, account length, date range); the rest are the bookings. How to export — two entry points A On an invoice/journal-entry list : select records → Actions → Download DATEV File . B On the General Ledger report (DE companies): the Download DATEV File button, using the report's date filter. Both open the Download DATEV Format chooser with three formats: Format You get CSV The plain Buchungsstapel. If new customers/vendors are involved, a ZIP with the bookings file ( EXTF_accounting_entries.csv ) + the partner master file ( EXTF_customer_accounts.csv ). CSV + PDF (ZIP) The CSV(s) plus each invoice's PDF and a document.xml that links every PDF to its booking (Chapter 7). XML Routes to the XML export (Chapter 8). What happens to customers vendors (Debitor/Kreditor) On receivable/payable lines, the module swaps Odoo's account for the partner's DATEV sub-account number . Debit shows as S (Soll), credit as H (Haben). If a partner has no number yet, one is created from the configured range and saved on the partner. 📦 Big date ranges? The General Ledger export shows a batch selector ( \"Move X to Y\" ) with a Next Batch button, honouring your G/L Export Batch Size . A Missing DATEV Account filter helps you catch entries with no account set before exporting. 7 Export: CSV + PDF Download Bills Send the bookings and the source documents, so DATEV shows the invoice PDF next to each booking. CSV + PDF (ZIP) Same as the CSV export, but the ZIP also contains every invoice's PDF plus a document.xml manifest. That manifest is the Beleglink (BEDI) — it tells DATEV which PDF belongs to which booking, so the Steuerberater sees the original document right beside the entry. This is the format clients love, because it removes all the \"where's the invoice for this line?\" back-and-forth. Download Bills (.zip) — PDFs only A simpler tool (from the Base module): select invoices → Actions → Download Bills (.zip) to get just the PDFs, named Bulk_Invoices_ timestamp .zip — no CSV. Handy when someone only needs the documents. A daily clean-up cron removes these temporary zips automatically. 💡 Each invoice remembers its DATEV Export Date and Zip Export Date , so you can always see what's already been sent. 8 Export: XML (Belegverwaltung online) A structured, per-invoice data record that lets DATEV book the invoice automatically — no manual typing on the DATEV side. Where the CSV is a batch of bookings , the XML export produces one DATEV XML record per invoice/bill (schema Belegverwaltung online ). It's not a scan — it carries the structured data: amounts, tax, invoice/due/delivery dates, invoice number, the partner's DATEV number, order reference, VAT IDs and bank details. DATEV reads it and proposes the booking automatically. How to export 1 Only posted invoices, credit notes, bills or refunds qualify. 2 Select them → Actions → Download DATEV File → XML . 3 One record → a single .xml ; several → a .zip of one XML each. Each XML is also attached to its invoice and noted in the chatter. Invoices then show read-only Exported as XML and XML Export Date fields, with matching search filters so you can track what's been sent. ℹ️ Each XML carries the standard DATEV note \"Kopie nur zur Verbuchung berechtigt nicht zum Vorsteuerabzug\" — a legal disclaimer that this copy is for booking only, not for reclaiming input VAT. 9 Import from DATEV The reverse direction — bring DATEV booking files back into Odoo as draft entries (e.g. bookings the Steuerberater made, or historical data). How to import Accounting → Import DATEV CSV The Import DATEV wizard asks for: Counter-Account — whether the counter account is in the Konto or Gegenkonto column. Import Journal — the target journal (must be flagged Is DATEV Import Journal? ). Year and Is Legacy Data Imported? — for historical/opening-balance data. Files — one or more DATEV EXTF CSVs. One calendar month per file (multi-month files are rejected). Each grouped set of rows becomes a draft journal entry — left in draft so you review before posting. ⚠️ Import matches, it does not create master data. Accounts (Sachkonten), taxes (via BU-Schlüssel), currencies and analytic accounts must already exist — a missing one aborts the import. Only a missing partner is tolerated (it falls back to the journal's default receivable/payable account and logs a warning). It does not import a chart of accounts. Checking what happened Open Accounting → DATEV Import Logs (read-only, newest first). Each log has a Level — Info (blue) or Data Issue (amber) — and details. After a run you get either \"import success\" or \"completed with warnings\"; filter by Level = Data Issue to see problems like \"The partner does not exist.\" 10 DATEV API (DATEVconnect) The premium option: skip files entirely. Odoo talks straight to the DATEV cloud and pushes bookings, master data and PDFs automatically, on a schedule. One-line pitch: post an invoice in Odoo and it appears in DATEV by itself, with the PDF attached — and you're emailed if anything fails. Connecting (one-time OAuth) 1 On the DATEV Integration record, click Get Access Token . 2 Odoo opens DATEV's own login page — the user signs in with their DATEV credentials and authorizes the scopes. 3 DATEV redirects back; Odoo stores the tokens. Buttons then become Refresh / Revoke . Thanks to the offline_access scope, the connection lasts ~2 years. 🔒 The user logs in on DATEV's page — we never handle their DATEV password , and the OAuth client secret is fetched automatically (the accountant never types it). Use Is Sandbox Mode? to test safely before going live. Export settings Export Frequency — Daily / Monthly / Quarterly. Journals to Export , Export Start Date . Data to Export — toggles for master data / accounting entries / PDF documents. PDF Export — Optional or Mandatory (with a size limit). Category / Folder / Register — where files land in DATEV. Running monitoring Manual push: select records → Actions → Export to DATEV API , or on the integration record Export via API (with an Include Already Exported? option and a duplicate warning). Automatic push: the DATEV: Export Data cron runs on your chosen frequency. Track everything in DATEV Export Jobs (read-only): each batch shows a Status (Created → Started → Success / Failed / Cancelled), the last step, the DATEV job id, retry count, and a Logs button. Failed jobs have a Retry Job button. If something breaks (expired token, failed export), admins get a grouped \"Critical Error in DATEV Integration\" email with a link straight to the logs. 💡 File vs API — how to choose: use CSV/XML file export when the Steuerberater wants to import files themselves; use the API when the client wants true hands-off, automatic syncing. 11 Buttons Status Cheat-Sheet Where to click, and what the status fields mean. Actions on invoices / journal entries Button (Actions menu) Does Download DATEV File Opens the chooser: CSV · CSV + PDF (ZIP) · XML. Download Bills (.zip) Zips just the selected invoices' PDFs. Export to DATEV API Pushes selected records to DATEV over the API. General Ledger → Download DATEV File Date-range Buchungsstapel export from the report. Status fields (read-only, on the invoice's DATEV group) Field Meaning DATEV Export Date When it went into a CSV export. Zip Export Date When its PDF went into a Bills zip. Exported as XML / XML Export Date When it was exported as a DATEV XML. Exported to DATEV API Whether it was pushed via the API. Useful search filters Missing DATEV Account (entries with no main account) · XML Exported / Not Exported · Exported to DATEV API . ★ DATEV Glossary German bookkeeping terms, decoded. DATEV The German cooperative whose software tax advisors use for accounting and tax filing. Steuerberater A German tax advisor — the professional who keeps the official books in DATEV. EXTF DATEV's import/export file format. The first CSV line (the \"EXTF header\") identifies firm, client, period and file type. Buchungsstapel A batch of bookings — the accounting-entry CSV. = Odoo's posted journal entries. Berater-Nr. (Consultant Number) Identifies the tax firm. Set on the Odoo Company; exports fail without it. Mandant-Nr. (Client Number) Identifies this specific client's file in DATEV. Set on the Company. SKR03 / SKR04 The two standard German charts of accounts. Chosen at company setup. Sachkonto A general-ledger account (a \"Konto\"). Gegenkonto = the counter/contra account on a booking. Debitor A customer sub-account (DATEV range 10000+). = Odoo customer + DATEV Customer Identifier. Kreditor A vendor sub-account (DATEV range 70000+). = Odoo vendor + DATEV Supplier Identifier. Soll / Haben Debit / Credit — written as S / H in the file. BU-Schlüssel (Buchungsschlüssel) DATEV tax/booking key. Maps to Odoo taxes; the default key 40 switches off DATEV's auto-tax on a line. Belegfeld 1 / 2, Belegdatum, Buchungstext The document reference, dates and posting text on each booking row. Beleg / Belegverwaltung online Documents (invoice PDFs) and DATEV's online receipt management — fed by the XML export and the API's PDF push. Beleglink (BEDI) The link that ties a PDF to its booking (the document.xml in a CSV+PDF zip). KOST1 / KOST2 (Kostenstelle) DATEV cost centres. Map to Odoo analytic plans. DATEVconnect DATEV's cloud API — what our API Integration module connects to for automatic syncing. Internal training · much. GmbH · built on the standard German localization ( l10n_de ) Prerequisite reading: the Odoo Accounting Invoicing handbook · Odoo 19 · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){ if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')} }); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={}; links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){ entries.forEach(function(e){ if(e.isIntersecting){ links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')}); var a=map[e.target.id]; if(a){a.classList.add('active')} } }) },{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "much-products.html", "title": "much. Products — Overview", "text": "much. Products — Overview ← Back to Guide Hub much. Products What each much. product does, who it's for, its status and where to find it. Ask to update this as products evolve. Light / dark Print / PDF much. Consulting · Product overview · Questions, or a new product to add? Contact David Maisuradze . const PRODUCTS=[ { ic:'🤖',color:'m-pink',name:'AI Automated Actions Agents',status:'Stable',statusCls:'ok', sub:'Generative AI inside Odoo automated / server actions', desc:'Integrates generative AI (ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Mistral) into Odoo\\'s native automated or server actions — enabling intelligent document processing, data analysis, and automated response generation across any Odoo model.', who:'Odoo users who want to build powerful, intelligent automations into their workflows without needing technical coding skills.', resources:[{label:'Odoo App Store Page',url:''},{label:'GitHub Repository',url:'https://github.com/much-GmbH/much_automated_agent_actions'}], meeting:'20/07 (to be scheduled)',testing:{label:'Testing link',url:''}, note:' Heads-up on versions: this module was built mainly for older Odoo versions . Odoo 19 already ships with AI actions by default , so the much. module is most useful for customers on versions before 19 — or those who want the extra AI providers and flexibility it adds on top of the native feature.' }, { ic:'⚙️',color:'m-purple',name:'Integration Hub',status:'Stable',statusCls:'ok', sub:'Central framework for all much. integrations', desc:'The framework that powers every much. integration. Provides installation, configuration, monitoring, logs, mappers, queues, webhooks and scheduled jobs for all connections between Odoo and external systems.', who:'Any customer using a much. integration product.', features:'Integration Hub (iHub) manages the full lifecycle of connections between Odoo and external systems (e-commerce, accounting, logistics). Key features: Integrations (connections with Staging / Active states), Scheduled Jobs (automated sync intervals), Background Jobs (heavy tasks without blocking Odoo), Logs (full visibility with severity levels), Mappers (value translation between systems), Queues (controlled processing of incoming data), Webhooks (real-time data push) and Cronwatch (alerts when a job stops running). Without iHub, no much. integration works.', resources:[{label:'Knowledge article',url:'https://erp.muchconsulting.de/odoo/knowledge/1569'}],meeting:'07/07' }, { ic:'🛒',color:'m-teal',name:'Order Base integration',status:'Maintenance',statusCls:'warn', sub:'Base framework for all e-commerce order integrations', desc:'Defines how orders from external platforms are imported into Odoo — customers, addresses, products, taxes and deliveries. The shared layer every shop connector is built on.', who:'Customers using Shopware, Shopify or ChannelEngine integrations.', features:'Order Base Integration (OBI) manages the full data lifecycle between Odoo and external shop platforms (Shopify, Channel Engine, etc.). Key features: Shop Connector (credentials & connection state), Order Import (orders into Odoo with customer matching), Order Cancellations (full / partial, updates pickings), Order Returns (return pickings + credit notes), Product Import (catalogues, variants, attributes), Product Export (push products to platform), Shipment Export (tracking data on delivery), Stock Export (batch stock sync) and Scheduled Actions (5 cron jobs per connector out of the box). Without OBI, no much. e-commerce integration works.', resources:[{label:'GitHub description',url:'https://github.com/much-GmbH/much-order-base-integration'}],meeting:'07/07' }, { ic:'🔌',color:'m-blue',name:'Shopware',status:'',statusCls:'', sub:'Platform connector built on top of OBI and iHub', desc:'Handles the full Shopware 6 ↔ Odoo data lifecycle: OAuth credentials & connection state, order import (Standard and High Volume modes), product import and export, stock export (batch, daily), shipment export with tracking, return export, and one-time tax & currency mapping.', who:'Customers running a Shopware 6 online store integrated with Odoo.', features:'The Shopware Connector is much.\\'s platform-specific integration layer, built on top of Order Base Integration (OBI) and iHub. Key features: OAuth-based credentials & connection management, Order Import (Standard mode for regular volume; High Volume mode for large catalogues, running in 6 background phases), Product Import (variants, images, SKUs, barcodes), Product Export (manual or automatic on save), Stock Export (daily batch sync; auto-archives products deleted from Shopware), Shipment Export (tracking numbers on delivery validation), Return Export (on return-receipt validation) and Tax Import & Currency Mapping (one-time setup buttons). Without the Shopware Connector, no Shopware ↔ Odoo integration runs.', resources:[{label:'Knowledge article',url:'https://erp.muchconsulting.de/odoo/knowledge/1541'},{label:'Git Repository',url:'https://github.com/much-GmbH/much-order-shopware-integration'},{label:'Sales Product Page',url:''}],meeting:'08/07' }, { ic:'🔄',color:'m-coral',name:'Procuros Connector',status:'',statusCls:'', sub:'EDI trade documents over the Procuros network', desc:'Lets an Odoo company trade over EDI (Electronic Data Interchange) — the way large retailers and grocery chains require, since they don\\'t accept PDF orders or emailed invoices. It turns Odoo purchase orders, sales orders, deliveries, invoices, credit notes and payments into Procuros EDI documents and back again — automatically, on a schedule, with full logging.', who:'Odoo companies trading with large retailers / grocery chains over EDI — either as a buyer (Purchase-to-Pay) or a supplier (Order-to-Cash).', features:'Built on iHub (scheduling, logging, mappers) and symmetric — the same module serves both sides of a deal. Key documents / flows: Order handshake (PO → Sales Order), Order Response (accept / change / reject), Shipping Notice (despatch → receipt), Invoice → vendor bill, Credit Note, and Remittance Advice (payment). Trading partners are identified by GLN and products by GTIN (GS1 standards). Without it, an Odoo company cannot exchange EDI documents over Procuros.', resources:[{label:'GitHub Repository',url:'https://github.com/much-GmbH/procuros-connector'},{label:'Knowledge article',url:''}],meeting:'to be scheduled' } ]; function resChip(r){return r.url?` ${r.label} ↗ `:` ${r.label} `;} document.getElementById('products').innerHTML=PRODUCTS.map(p=>` ${p.ic} ${p.name} ${p.sub} ${p.status?` ${p.status} `:''} ${p.desc} ${p.note?` ${p.note} `:''} Who it's for ${p.who} ${p.features?` Key features ${p.features} `:''} Resources ${p.resources.map(resChip).join('')}${p.testing?resChip({label:'🔗 '+p.testing.label,url:p.testing.url}):''} ${p.meeting?` 🗓 Meeting: ${p.meeting} `:''}${p.status?` Status: ${p.status} `:''} `).join('');"}, {"href": "much-release-guide.html", "title": "much. Odoo Addon Release — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "much. Odoo Addon Release — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub much . Release New Joiner Handbook · Internal Cutting an Odoo Addon Release How to release one of our Odoo addon repositories, step by step — written for someone doing it for the very first time. much. GmbH · git + GitHub · example repo: much-order-shopware-integration Chapters 1 What a release is 2 Branch map vocabulary 3 Find what to release 4 Ask for the version 5 Bump the manifests 6 Commit the bump 7 Push the QA branch 8 Merge into Stable 9 Create the version branch 10 Write the release notes 11 Wrap up cheat-sheet ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome — start here This handbook walks you through releasing one of our Odoo addon repositories . If you've never done a git-based release before, don't worry — every step is spelled out, and nothing is assumed. You should be comfortable with the basics of git (branches, commits, pushing) and have the GitHub CLI ( gh ) installed and logged in. Everything else you'll learn here. The examples use much-order-shopware-integration , but the process is identical for every addon repo. 🧠 The one big idea A release is simply: bump the version number , move the tested code from the QA branch onto the Stable branch , freeze it as a version branch , and write release notes . That's it. The golden rule that keeps you safe: always ask for confirmation before any git push or anything hard to undo. 1 What a release is Turning \"tested work on the QA branch\" into an official, named version our customers can install. Each of our repos is an Odoo addon repository — a folder of modules (each module is a sub-folder with its own __manifest__.py that, among other things, declares a version ). Throughout a sprint, developers open pull requests; once a PR is reviewed and tested it gets merged into the QA branch . So at any moment the QA branch holds \"everything we've finished and tested but haven't shipped yet.\" A release is the deliberate moment we say \"this batch is good — ship it.\" Four things happen, and each exists for a concrete reason: What we do Why it's needed Bump the version in every module's manifest Odoo decides whether to run a module's upgrade scripts by comparing the installed version to the new one. If the number doesn't change, Odoo thinks nothing changed and skips the upgrade — so customers would keep running old code. Merge QA → Stable branch Stable ( X.latest ) is the branch production systems actually pull from. Until code reaches Stable, no customer has it. Create a version branch A permanent, frozen snapshot named for the exact version, so months later we can see (or re-deploy) precisely what shipped as 19.0.1.1.4 — even after Stable has moved on. Write release notes So the team, the client, and their Steuerberater/end-users know what changed — and support can trace a bug to the release that introduced or fixed it. 🧪 QA branch Where tested work accumulates. A release always starts here, and it's the source of truth for \"what's new.\" 🏷️ Version branch A frozen, named snapshot (e.g. 19.0.1.1.4 ). Nobody commits to it again — it's the deliverable, kept for the record. ℹ️ Who runs this? Usually the assistant runs it for you via the /release command, pausing to ask for your confirmation at each push. This handbook explains what that command does and why , so you understand each step — and can do it by hand if the tooling ever isn't available. 2 Branch map vocabulary Four names built from one number. Learn these and the rest is mechanical. (Full list in the glossary .) Everything derives from the major version — for example 19 , which is the Odoo major we build for. From that single number you can derive every branch name in the release, which is exactly why the process is so mechanical: Name What it is why it exists Example MAJOR The Odoo major version everything is built for. Determines all three branch names below. 19 QA branch Where finished, tested work lands during the sprint. A release starts here because it's the collecting point — the newest good code lives here. 19.latest-qa Stable branch The production-facing branch customers pull from. Kept \"clean\" — only released code reaches it, so it's always safe to deploy. 19.latest Version branch An immutable snapshot named exactly for the release. Never committed to again; it's the historical record of \"what shipped as this version.\" 19.0.1.1.4 __manifest__.py Each module's descriptor. Holds the \"version\" string we bump; Odoo reads it to decide whether to upgrade the module. \"19.0.1.1.4\" Reading the version number Our versions follow Odoo's five-part convention MAJOR . 0 . x . y . z . Understanding the segments tells you (and the reviewer) how big a release is: Segment In 19.0.1.1.4 Meaning 1 — Odoo major 19 The Odoo version. Rarely changes within a repo's life. 2 — Odoo minor 0 Odoo's own minor; we keep it at 0 . 3 — module major 1 Bumped for big/breaking changes to our module. 4 — feature 1 Bumped when a new feature ships. 5 — patch 4 Bumped for bug-fix-only releases (the most common — like this one). 💡 This is why the version is a human decision (Chapter 4), not automatic: only a person knows whether this batch is \"just fixes\" (bump the last digit) or \"a new feature\" (bump the fourth). The three fixes in our example release were all bug fixes → …1.1.3 became …1.1.4 . ⚠️ Golden rule, again: ask for a clear \"yes\" before every push or destructive action. There are three pushes in a release (QA, Stable, version branch) — each gets its own confirmation, because each one publishes something to origin that other people and systems will pull. 3 Find what to release Work out where you are, then list what's new since the last release. Step 1 — Determine the context Before touching anything, you need two facts: which branch am I on , and which major version am I releasing . Get them wrong and you could bump the wrong repo line entirely. git branch --show-current # → prints e.g. 19.latest-qa Finding the major: it's either handed to you (e.g. you ran /release 19 ) or you read it off the branch name, which follows the pattern X.latest-qa — so 19.latest-qa → major 19 . If neither is available (say you're on a feature branch), stop and ask rather than guessing. Why the branch matters: a release must start from the QA branch, because that's the branch that holds the newest tested code. If git branch --show-current shows anything other than 19.latest-qa , ask the reviewer whether to switch ( git checkout 19.latest-qa ) before continuing — don't switch silently. Step 2 — Show the commits since the last tag Now answer \"what's actually new?\" The last release left behind a tag (a permanent marker on the commit that shipped). Everything committed after that tag is what this release will contain. First, find that tag: # newest v19 tag, sorted by when it was created git tag --list 'v19*' --sort=-creatordate | head -1 # → v19.0.1.1.3 Two flags matter here: --sort=-creatordate orders tags by creation time (the leading - means newest first) rather than alphabetically — important because v19.0.1.1.10 would sort before v19.0.1.1.9 alphabetically, giving you the wrong \"latest.\" head -1 then keeps only the top (newest) one. Then list the commits between that tag and where you are now ( HEAD ): git log v19.0.1.1.3 ..HEAD --oneline --no-merges v19.0.1.1.3..HEAD is a range : \"everything reachable from HEAD that wasn't already in the tag\" — i.e. exactly the new work. --oneline prints one short line per commit (hash + subject) so it's easy to scan. --no-merges hides merge commits, leaving only the real code changes — these are what you'll categorize into the release notes. Display the result clearly (a table is ideal). Then run it again with --merges to pull out the PR numbers — merge commits carry text like \"Merge pull request #97\", and you'll cite those numbers in the notes: git log v19.0.1.1.3 ..HEAD --merges --oneline # → d44bb37 Merge pull request #97 from much-GmbH/56976-fix-vat-19 🛑 No new commits? Stop. If the range is empty, the last tag already points at HEAD — there is genuinely nothing to release. Tell the reviewer and end here; bumping a version with no changes would just create noise. 4 Ask for the version The new version number is a human decision — never guessed. Ask the reviewer plainly: \"What version should this release be?\" and do not proceed until they answer. This is deliberately a manual gate — see Chapter 2, only a human knows whether this batch counts as a patch, a feature, or a breaking change, and that judgement decides which digit moves. v19.0.1.1.3 → you ask → 19.0.1.1.4 Why not just auto-increment the last digit? Because it would silently mislabel releases. A release full of new features that only bumped the patch digit would tell customers \"minor fixes\" when it's actually a feature drop — the version number is a promise about what changed, so a person confirms it. ⚠️ The most common beginner slip: the v prefix. The git tag carries it ( v19.0.1.1.3 ) because tags conventionally do. The manifest version and the branch name do not ( 19.0.1.1.4 ). So when the reviewer says \"make it v19.0.1.1.4\", you write 19.0.1.1.4 into the manifests and name the branch 19.0.1.1.4 — the v only appears in tag-related places like the changelog URL. 5 Bump the manifests Every module carries its own version, so every module gets bumped to the new number — together, in lockstep. First, locate every manifest that actually declares a version. A repo can have helper folders (tests, static assets) without manifests, so we search rather than assume: # list every manifest file that contains a \"version\" key find . -name \"__manifest__.py\" | xargs grep -l '\"version\"' Reading that command: find . -name \"__manifest__.py\" walks the whole repo and prints every manifest path; the pipe hands those paths to xargs grep -l '\"version\"' , which opens each one and prints only the files that contain a \"version\" line ( -l = \"list filenames, not the matching text\"). The result is your worklist. In each listed file, change the \"version\" value to the new version — format 19.0.1.1.4 , no v prefix . When you're done, tell the reviewer how many files you updated, so they can sanity-check nothing was missed. Why bump them all at once? Odoo treats each module's version independently — it upgrades a module only if that module's number increased. If you bumped some manifests and forgot others, half the repo would upgrade and half wouldn't, leaving a customer on a mismatched, half-updated install that's very hard to diagnose. Keeping every module on the same version is a deliberate convention that makes \"which release is this system on?\" answerable at a glance. 💡 Some files may already be on the new number — a fix commit sometimes bumps a single module ahead of the pack (in our example, much_order_shopware_product_import was already at 19.0.1.1.4 ). That's expected; just bring the remaining modules in line so all of them match. Bumping the ones already there is a harmless no-op. ⚠️ Only edit the \"version\" line. A manifest also holds depends , data , author info and more — don't touch any of that during a release bump. Changing depends here, for instance, would alter what the module installs and belongs in a real feature PR, not a version bump. 6 Commit the bump Stage only the manifest files and commit with the exact message format. git add */__manifest__.py git commit -m \"chore[*] update manifest version Signed-off-by : {name} {email} \" git add */__manifest__.py stages only the manifest files — the */ glob matches each module folder. Staging narrowly matters: it guarantees the commit contains the version bump and nothing else , so the history reads cleanly and a reviewer can trust that a \"update manifest version\" commit really only did that. Fill in {name} and {email} from your git config — these identify who cut the release: git config user.name # → Joaquim Vicente git config user.email # → joaquim.vicente@muchconsulting.com Why this exact format? chore[*] — our commit-prefix convention. chore marks non-feature, non-fix housekeeping (so it's correctly skipped when the release notes are categorized); the [*] scope means \"affects all modules,\" which a version bump does. The blank line then Signed-off-by : trailer records accountability for the release — the same convention used across our repos, so history stays uniform and greppable. ✍️ Keep the subject line exactly chore[*] update manifest version . Consistency here is what lets us later filter release commits out of changelogs automatically — a one-word difference breaks that. 7 Push the QA branch The first of three pushes. Get the bump onto GitHub before you start moving code around. 🛑 Ask for confirmation first. A push writes to origin , where teammates and CI will immediately see it — that's why every push in this guide waits for a clear \"yes.\" git push origin 19.latest-qa Why push QA first, before merging? The next step fast-forwards Stable to match QA. If QA on GitHub is behind your local copy, that merge would push commits to Stable that don't yet exist on the QA branch remotely — leaving the two branches inconsistent for anyone who pulls. Publishing QA first guarantees GitHub's QA and Stable end up pointing at the exact same, already-visible commit. A successful push prints the moved range, e.g. d44bb37..9794277 19.latest-qa - 19.latest-qa — that \"old..new\" hash pair is your confirmation it landed. 8 Merge into Stable Bring the freshly-bumped QA work onto the production branch customers install from. git checkout 19.latest git merge 19.latest-qa -m \"Merge branch '19.latest-qa' into 19.latest\" Step by step: git checkout 19.latest switches you onto the Stable branch (so the merge lands there), then git merge 19.latest-qa pulls QA's commits into it. What \"fast-forward\" means and why it's good Because Stable hasn't diverged — nobody commits to Stable directly, it only ever receives releases — QA is simply \"Stable plus the new commits.\" Git can therefore just slide the Stable pointer forward to QA's tip without inventing a merge commit. That's a fast-forward , and you'll see git say Fast-forward in the output. It's the clean, expected result. (If git instead reports a real merge or a conflict, something committed to Stable out-of-band — pause and check with the team before proceeding.) 🛑 Ask for confirmation, then push Stable — this is the moment the release becomes visible to production: git push origin 19.latest ℹ️ Why merge into Stable at all? Stable ( X.latest ) is the branch deployment systems and customers pull from. Code that only lives on QA has been tested but not released ; merging is the act that promotes it to \"shipped.\" 9 Create the version branch Freeze this exact release under a branch named for the version — the permanent record. Right now you're on Stable, sitting on the exact commit that is the release. Create a branch named exactly as the version from that point, then push it with tracking: git checkout -b 19.0.1.1.4 # then, only after confirmation: git push -u origin 19.0.1.1.4 git checkout -b 19.0.1.1.4 creates and switches to the new branch in one go (the -b = \"make a new branch here\"). It branches off wherever you currently are — which is why we do this while standing on Stable, so the snapshot captures exactly what shipped. 🛑 Confirm before this third and final push too. Why -u , and why a branch at all? The branch is the immutable, human-named marker of the release. Stable will keep moving with future releases, but 19.0.1.1.4 stays frozen — so months later \"check out exactly what we shipped as 19.0.1.1.4\" is one command. -u (short for --set-upstream ) links your local branch to the new remote one, so a later bare git push or git pull knows where to go without you re-typing the remote and branch. It's a one-time convenience set at creation. ⚠️ The workflow stops at the version branch — it does not create a git tag unless you're explicitly asked to. (Tagging is sometimes done separately as part of publishing.) Don't invent a v19.0.1.1.4 tag on your own; if the reviewer wants one, ask and do it deliberately. 🔀 Exception — these products skip the version branch. For MCP server , Odoo AI automated Actions , Paypall and Procuro , do not create a version branch as described above. After running /release (e.g. /release 19 ), instead of branching for this version, simply merge latest into main — e.g. git checkout main then git merge latest . (instead of creating a new branche for this Version, just merge latest into main!) 10 Write the release notes Two files: one technical for GitHub, one friendly for end users. Read the diffs — don't just copy commit subjects. Gather the data first Today's date as DD Mon YYYY (e.g. 13 Jul 2026 ). Contributors (deduplicated, each prefixed with @ ): gh api repos/ {OWNER}/{REPO} /compare/ {LAST_TAG}...{VERSION_BRANCH} \\ --jq '.commits[].author.login' Full changelog URL: .../compare/{LAST_TAG}...v{NEW_VERSION} Read the actual diff of each commit and write a real, human description — git show {hash} --stat then git show {hash} . Include the PR number if the message has one (e.g. #97 ). Check for new dependencies : look at depends in __manifest__.py diffs and any requirements.txt change. Categorize each non-chore, non-merge commit Commit starts with… Goes under fix… Fixes feat… New Features break… / contains BREAKING Breaking Changes File 1 — RELEASE_NOTES_v{NEW_VERSION}.md (technical) Follow the skill's exact template, in this order: Breaking Changes · New Features · Fixes · Deprecated Features · Contributors · full changelog link · Installation/Upgrade instructions · Feedback and support . Support address for these notes: product@erp.muchconsulting.de . File 2 — USER_RELEASE_NOTES_v{NEW_VERSION}.md (user-friendly) Same facts, written for end users — plain language, no developer jargon. Use the emoji sections: New Features :tada: , Improvements :microscope: , Bug Fixes :hammer: (each fix described in user terms, referencing its PR number). Feedback address here is different : product@muchconsulting.de . 📧 Two different emails! Technical notes → product@erp.muchconsulting.de . User notes → product@muchconsulting.de . Easy to mix up. 11 Wrap up cheat-sheet Tell the reviewer what happened, and keep this table handy. Final message to the reviewer The release is complete . List the two generated files . Remind them which branch they're on (the version branch). Open both files so they can review them. The whole release at a glance # Step Confirmation? 1 Determine context (branch, major) — 2 Show commits since last tag stop if none 3 Ask for the new version wait for answer 4 Bump all __manifest__.py — 5 Commit the bump — 6 Push QA branch confirm 7 Merge QA → Stable, push confirm push 8 Create + push version branch confirm push 9 Write both release-notes files — 10 Report done open the files — ✅ That's a full release. Three pushes, two notes files, one frozen version branch — and a confirmation before anything that touches origin . ★ Release Glossary The terms in this handbook, decoded. Release Stamping a new version onto every module, publishing it on Stable, and freezing it as a version branch — plus release notes. MAJOR The big version number everything is built from (e.g. 19 ). QA branch (X.latest-qa) Where tested work accumulates. A release always starts here. Stable branch (X.latest) The released, production-facing branch that customers install from. Version branch An immutable branch named exactly for the release (e.g. 19.0.1.1.4 ) — the permanent snapshot. __manifest__.py Each Odoo module's descriptor file; holds the \"version\" string that a release bumps. Tag (vX…) A git marker for a past release, carrying a v prefix ( v19.0.1.1.3 ). Used to find \"what changed since last time.\" Fast-forward A merge where the target branch simply advances to match the source — no new merge commit, no divergence. The normal, healthy outcome when merging QA into Stable. Signed-off-by A trailer line on the commit message identifying who made the change (name + email from git config). gh The GitHub CLI. Used here to fetch contributor handles via the compare API. PR number (#97) The pull-request identifier, found in merge-commit messages; cited in the release notes. Buchungsstapel (Not this guide — that's DATEV 😉.) See the DATEV handbook. Internal training · much. GmbH · git + GitHub release workflow (the /release command) Related reading: the much. DATEV Integration handbook · When in doubt, ask before you push 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){ if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')} }); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={}; links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){ entries.forEach(function(e){ if(e.isIntersecting){ links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')}); var a=map[e.target.id]; if(a){a.classList.add('active')} } }) },{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-accounting-getting-started.html", "title": "Odoo Accounting &amp; Invoicing — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Accounting Invoicing — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Odoo eLearning Odoo Accounting Invoicing The complete beginner's guide — every lesson of the course in one place, in plain English. No accounting background needed. Odoo 19 · Accounting Invoicing Chapters 1 Getting Started 2 Fiscal Localization 3 Chart of Accounts 4 Journal Entries Items 5 Customer Invoices 6 Vendor Bills 7 Payments 8 Bank Cash 9 Taxes Fiscal Positions 10 Reporting Advanced 11 Multi-Currency 12 Analytic Accounting 13 Assets Deferrals 14 Budgets Year-End 15 E-Invoicing Online Pay ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Accounting is where the business tracks every euro that comes in and goes out — customer invoices, supplier bills, bank movements, taxes and reports. This handbook walks you through the whole Accounting Invoicing course, chapter by chapter, written for someone who has never done accounting before . Read top to bottom, or jump around using the menu on the left. Take it slow — you don't need to memorise anything. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea When the database was created, someone picked a country . That choice automatically installed a “fiscal localization package” — which loads a ready-made, legally-compliant Chart of Accounts and a set of taxes . So a lot is already done for you. Your real job is to record the daily documents (invoices, bills, payments) correctly — Odoo does the accounting math behind the scenes. 1 Getting Started When you open Accounting for the first time, an onboarding banner walks you through four setup steps. Do them in order. 1 📅 Accounting Periods Tell Odoo when your fiscal year starts and ends (default Jan 1 → Dec 31 ). Also set how often you file taxes — monthly, quarterly or yearly — so Odoo reminds you before deadlines. Sets your reporting calendar 2 🏦 Bank Account Automatic: find your bank → Connect → follow prompts. Manual: type Name , Account Number (IBAN) and BIC/SWIFT . Feeds real bank data into Odoo 3 🧾 Taxes Your country's tax rates are usually already loaded by the localization. Here you create, edit or switch off individual rates. For most people: just check they look right. Mostly pre-filled for you 4 📗 Chart of Accounts Review the list of accounts, set any opening balances (starting numbers when you begin using Odoo), and open Setup to see details. More in Chapter 3 👇 Review, don't rebuild 2 Fiscal Localization A country \"starter pack\" that makes Odoo comply with your local tax and accounting laws automatically. A fiscal localization package is a country-specific module Odoo installs based on the company's country. Instead of building accounting rules from scratch, you inherit a package built to match your country's regulations. It includes: 📗 Chart of Accounts Account structure matching local standards. 🧾 Taxes Country-specific tax rates and rules. 🔀 Fiscal Positions Rules that auto-adjust taxes accounts by situation (see Ch. 9). 📊 Legal Reports Templates for mandatory tax filings and statements. How it's installed changed When the Accounting app is installed, Odoo detects the company's country and installs the right package automatically — no manual work. To view or change it: Accounting → Configuration → Settings → Fiscal Localization → Package ⚠️ You can only switch packages before posting any accounting entries. Once real transactions exist, the localization is locked in — this keeps your financial data compliant and consistent. 3 Chart of Accounts The master list of every account used to record transactions — your company's financial roadmap . Every account belongs to one of five types . In plain English: 💰 Owns Assets What you own — cash, equipment, money customers owe you. 📤 Owes Liabilities What you owe — supplier bills, loans, credit cards. 🏛️ Stake Equity The owner's share — assets minus liabilities. 📈 Earns Income Money the business earns — sales and revenue. 💸 Spends Expenses Money spent to operate — rent, salaries, supplies. Find it add an account Accounting → Configuration → Chart of Accounts → Create Fill three required fields: Code (an identifying number), Account Name , and Type . Two settings worth knowing 🔗 Reconciliation Tick this and the account becomes matchable against bank transactions. Only reconcilable accounts appear when matching. 🧾 Default Taxes Taxes set here apply automatically whenever this account is used in a sale or purchase. 💡 You rarely start from zero. The localization already filled this list with your country's standard accounts. Day-to-day you're reviewing and occasionally adding — not building it all yourself. 4 Journal Entries Items How every transaction is actually recorded behind the scenes. This is the engine of accounting — and Odoo runs it for you. Every financial document — an invoice, a bill, a bank payment, a payslip — becomes a journal entry : the formal bookkeeping record of that transaction. Each entry is made of individual lines called journal items , and every item is either a debit or a credit to a specific account. The golden rule: debits = credits Accounting is double-entry : every transaction touches at least two accounts — one goes up, another goes down — and the two sides always balance. ⚖️ For any journal entry, total debits must equal total credits. That's what keeps the books accurate. If they don't match, Odoo won't let you post the entry. A worked example A customer pays you €100. Odoo records one entry with two items: Account Debit Credit Bank (money coming in ↑) €100 — Accounts Receivable (customer owes less ↓) — €100 Both sides are €100 — balanced. ✅ What's a \"journal\"? A journal is simply a logbook that groups one category of transactions — e.g. the Sales journal (customer invoices), the Purchases journal (vendor bills), the Bank journal, the Cash journal. 💡 You almost never write journal entries by hand. When you post an invoice, bill or payment, Odoo builds the balanced journal entry for you. Understanding this just helps you read reports and know what's happening under the hood. 5 Customer Invoices A document you send a customer to request payment for what you sold. It creates a receivable — money owed to you. The invoice lifecycle Draft → Posted → Paid Draft — a work-in-progress. Edit or delete freely; nothing has hit your accounts yet. Posted (confirmed) — Odoo locks it, assigns an official number (e.g. INV/2025/0001 ), and creates the journal entry. Paid — the customer's payment has been matched (reconciled) to the invoice. Create an invoice Accounting → Customers → Invoices → New Customer — pick who you're billing. Auto-fills their address, payment terms and accounts. Invoice Date — drives the due date and the accounting period. Invoice Lines — one line per item: pick a Product (auto-fills price, description, taxes), set Quantity , Price , Taxes . Click Confirm to post. This assigns the official number and records the accounting: Accounts Receivable ↑, Income ↑, Tax recorded . Payment terms Payment terms define when a customer must pay. Common examples: Immediate , Net 30 (30 days after invoice), installments (e.g. 30% now / 70% end of month), or 2/10 Net 30 (2% off if paid within 10 days). Set a default on the customer so it applies automatically. Configure under Accounting → Configuration → Payment Terms . Credit notes (refunds corrections) A credit note reverses an amount a customer owes — the only legal way to cancel or correct a posted invoice (you can't just delete it). Use it for mistakes, returns, or refunds. On the invoice click Credit Note , then choose Reverse (editable draft, good for partial refunds) or Reverse and Create Invoice (cancel re-bill). Send it Once posted, open the invoice and click Send — by Email (PDF to the customer), Download , or Print . ⚠️ Draft = safe, Posted = official. Do all editing in Draft. To fix a posted invoice, use Reset to Draft (if not yet paid) or issue a credit note — never try to delete it. 6 Vendor Bills A supplier's invoice to your company — money you owe. The mirror image of a customer invoice. Vendor Bill Customer Invoice Direction Money you owe (out) Money owed to you (in) Who issues it Your supplier You Accounting effect Accounts Payable ↑ Accounts Receivable ↑ Found under Accounting → Vendors Accounting → Customers Lifecycle Draft → Posted → Paid Posting records the debt (Accounts Payable) but moves no money — paying is a separate step (Chapter 7). Create a bill Accounting → Vendors → Bills → New Vendor — the supplier. Bill Reference — the supplier's own invoice number. Important: lets Odoo warn you about duplicates. Bill Date (supplier's date) vs Accounting Date (when it hits your books). Lines — products or free-text charges, quantities, prices, taxes. Four ways bills get into Odoo 1 Manual entry — type it in (best for learning). 2 Upload/scan a PDF — Odoo's AI reads the document and auto-fills it; you just review. 3 Email alias — suppliers email invoices to a dedicated address; Odoo creates drafts automatically. 4 From a Purchase Order — a confirmed PO generates a matching draft bill. ⚠️ Always fill the Bill Reference — it stops you paying the same bill twice. And remember: posting ≠ paying . 7 Payments \"Registering a payment\" tells Odoo money changed hands and links it to the right invoice or bill. ⬇️ Customer payment Money you received . Open the invoice → Register Payment . ⬆️ Vendor payment Money you paid out . Open the bill → Register Payment . In the pop-up set the Journal (Bank/Cash), Payment Date , Payment Method and Amount (lower it for a partial payment — Odoo tracks the remaining balance), then Create Payment . \"In Payment\" vs \"Paid\" — the two-stage idea Outstanding account — when you register a payment, Odoo parks it in a temporary holding account. The document shows In Payment = \"we've noted it.\" Bank reconciliation — later you match it to the real bank statement line. Only then does it become Paid = \"the bank proves it.\" ℹ️ This protects accuracy — a check might bounce or a transfer might take days. If everything is stuck at In Payment , you still need to do bank reconciliation (Chapter 8). Payment methods batches Methods: Manual (cash/transfers — the simple default), Checks, SEPA (European bank file), or online providers (Stripe, PayPal). Batch payments bundle many payments into one file/record — handy when the bank shows a single lump sum, so you reconcile the whole batch at once. Enable under Settings → Batch Payments (one payment method per batch). Follow-ups (chasing overdue invoices) Automatic reminders to customers with overdue invoices, escalating by levels (gentle → firm → final) based on days overdue. Configure under Accounting → Configuration → Follow-up Levels . Send via email, SMS, WhatsApp, letter or print. ⚠️ Reconcile your bank before sending follow-ups — otherwise you'll chase customers who already paid. 8 Bank Cash Getting your real bank activity into Odoo and matching it to your books. Bank accounts Each bank account you add creates a journal (its logbook) and a ledger account (its running balance). Add one from the Accounting Dashboard → Search over 26,000 banks , then either Connect (auto-sync) or Record transactions manually . Bank synchronization Odoo pulls transactions straight from your bank — no typing. It syncs every 12 hours (click Fetch Transactions to refresh now). Your bank login is never stored on Odoo's servers ; secure providers (Plaid, Yodlee, Salt Edge…) handle it. Requires Odoo Enterprise. No sync? Import a statement file from your bank instead — CSV , OFX or QIF . Odoo flags likely duplicates automatically. Bank reconciliation Reconciliation = confirming your bank's records match your Odoo books. Why it matters: 🔍 Catches errors Before they pile up 🛡️ Spots fraud Unfamiliar transactions 💵 True cash view Know what you have ✅ Stays compliant Required for most 1 Review unmatched lines from the bank. 2 Match each to the right invoice, bill or payment. 3 Balance anything left over to the correct account. Reconciliation models (time-savers) A model is a saved rule for a recurring transaction — build it once, Odoo reuses it. E.g. a Bank Fees model auto-books the bank's charge every time. Models can be manual (a button you click) or automated (fire by themselves on a match). Find them via the bank journal's menu → Reconciliation Models . Cash A cash journal tracks physical cash (a till or petty-cash box). Unlike bank journals it must be created manually under Accounting → Configuration → Journals (type: Cash). 9 Taxes Fiscal Positions Odoo calculates tax for you, records what's owed, and produces the reports you file with the government. Add a product to an invoice and Odoo applies the right tax, calculates the amount, creates the accounting record, and keeps a running total for your tax return. Three tax types: 🧾 On sales Sales Applied to customer invoices . 🛒 On purchases Purchase Applied to vendor bills . 🔧 Internal None Used only inside tax groups . 1 Turn on any company-wide tax settings your country needs. 2 Activate the pre-built taxes from your localization — enable only the ones you use. 3 Set default sales purchase taxes on each product. Fiscal positions (automatic tax swapping) A fiscal position is a rule that automatically changes the taxes and accounts on a transaction depending on who the customer is or where they are — domestic vs EU vs export, B2B vs B2C. Without it you'd edit every invoice by hand. 🌍 Example — \"EU B2B\": enable Detect Automatically → Country Group: Europe , map 21% VAT → 0% VAT and the domestic revenue account → EU revenue account. Now every EU-business invoice is zero-rated automatically. Set it up under Accounting → Configuration → Fiscal Positions . Your localization already ships sensible defaults. 10 Reporting Automatic summaries of your financial data. You never do the math — Odoo builds these from the invoices, bills and payments you record. Accounting → Reporting ⚖️ Balance Sheet A snapshot on one day: what you own vs owe. Always Assets = Liabilities + Equity . 📈 Profit Loss A period view: Income − Expenses = Profit or Loss . \"Did we make money?\" 🧾 Tax Report Tax collected vs paid — the figures you file with the government. ⏳ Aged Receivable Money customers owe you, grouped by how overdue. ⏳ Aged Payable Money you owe suppliers, grouped by how overdue. 📚 General Ledger Every transaction, by account — the master logbook. Tools on every report Date filter — choose the period (a \"wrong-looking\" report is usually just the wrong date range). Comparison — put two periods side by side to see trends. Drill-down — click a line to expand it, all the way down to the original invoice. Export — PDF (print-ready) or XLSX (Excel, for your accountant). Filing your tax return (the short version) 1 Review the tax return and fix any warnings (draft entries, missing bills). 2 Lock the period with a Tax Lock Date so figures can't change after reporting. 3 Validate — Odoo posts the closing entry and moves the lock date forward. 4 Export file — Odoo prepares it, but you submit to the tax office yourself. 💡 Start with two reports: the Balance Sheet (\"what we own vs owe\") and the Profit Loss (\"did we make money\"). They tell you most of what you need. 11 Multi-Currency Invoicing, billing and getting paid in currencies other than your company's main currency. Your main (company) currency is what your books and reports are expressed in. With multi-currency on, you can trade in others (e.g. bill a US client in USD). The key idea: Odoo always stores two values for a foreign transaction — the foreign amount and its company-currency equivalent — so your official books stay in one currency. Enable it add currencies Accounting → Configuration → Settings → Currencies Turn on multi-currency, then activate the currencies you use under Configuration → Currencies (every currency exists but is off by default). Exchange rates An exchange rate is how much one currency is worth in another (1 EUR = 1.08 USD). Enter rates manually, or — recommended — enable Automatic Currency Rates (daily/weekly/monthly) from a source like the European Central Bank. Exchange gains losses Rates move between invoicing and payment. Invoice 1,000 USD worth €920 today; if it's worth €935 when paid, that €15 is an exchange gain (or a loss if less). Odoo posts this automatically to the gain/loss accounts you set once — you never calculate it by hand. 💡 Set up the Exchange Difference journal + gain/loss accounts before invoicing in foreign currencies, and give a foreign bank account its own journal locked to that currency. 12 Analytic Accounting A parallel way to track where money is earned and spent — by project, department or cost center — without touching your legal books. 📚 Regular accounting The legal books — required by law. Answers \"how much did we earn and spend?\" 🏷️ Analytic accounting Internal labels/tags on transactions. Answers \"which project or department did this belong to?\" Adding an analytic tag does not change your legal numbers — it just enriches them. Example: a €1,000 consultant bill still lands in \"Consulting Expenses\" (legal), but you also tag it Project Alpha to see what that project really cost. Plans accounts Analytic Plan = a category, e.g. \"Departments\" or \"Projects\". Analytic Account = a value within it (Sales, Marketing, Project Alpha…). Despite the name, it's really a tag , not a legal account. Tagging (analytic distribution) On an invoice/bill line, use the Analytic Distribution column — and you can split by percentage (e.g. an electricity bill 60% Warehouse / 40% Head Office). Automate repetitive tagging with Distribution Models . Enable it all under Settings → Analytics → Analytic Accounting . ℹ️ Start small — one plan (e.g. \"Projects\") with a few accounts, kept Optional at first. Switch a plan to Mandatory only when the team is ready (it blocks posting until a tag is added). 13 Assets Deferrals Three features that spread a lump sum across many months or years, so your books match reality instead of showing one giant spike. Feature Money direction Sits on books as Example Asset / Depreciation You pay for a long-lived thing Asset you own €27k van over 5 years Deferred Revenue Customer pays you up front Liability (you owe service) €1,200 subscription over 12 months Deferred Expense You pay a supplier up front Asset (prepaid benefit) €1,200 insurance over 12 months Assets depreciation A fixed asset is something expensive that lasts years (vehicle, machine). Depreciation spreads its cost across those years instead of expensing it all at once. In Accounting → Assets you create an asset (linkable to its vendor bill), and Odoo builds a depreciation board and posts each slice automatically. Methods: Straight Line (equal amounts — simplest), Declining , or a mix. Deferred revenues expenses Both work the same way: on the invoice/bill line, add a start end date , and Odoo recognizes a slice each period. Deferred revenue = income received before it's earned (a yearly subscription → €100/month). Deferred expense = a cost paid ahead of the benefit (annual insurance → €100/month). Configure the accounts under Accounting → Configuration → Settings . 💡 One takeaway: enter the document once, tell Odoo the time span, and it quietly posts the monthly slices — keeping your Profit Loss honest instead of lumpy. 14 Budgets Year-End Closing Planning your money ahead of time, then finalizing and locking the year when it's done. Budgets A budget is a plan for money: what you expect to earn and spend. Odoo compares plan vs reality so you spot overspending while you can still react. Two kinds: Analytic budgets (per project/department) and Financial budgets (per account, shown on the Profit Loss). Set up via Accounting → Accounting → Analytic Budgets , then track in the Budget Report . Column Plain meaning Budgeted What you planned. Achieved What actually happened so far. Committed Achieved + money already promised (e.g. approved purchase orders). Theoretical Where you should be by today if spending were even — \"am I ahead or behind pace?\" Year-end closing \"Closing the year\" means finalizing and locking all accounts for the fiscal year, so the figures become official and tamper-proof. The checklist: 1 Reconcile every bank account against its statement. 2 Finish all invoices, bills and payments for the year. 3 Review outstanding receivables payables (chase the overdue). 4 Check taxes in the reports. 5 Post adjusting entries (depreciation, deferrals, year-end earnings). 6 Lock the period with a lock date. Lock dates A lock date is a cutoff: any transaction dated on or before it becomes read-only . Set it under Accounting → Accounting → Lock Dates . It only freezes the past — your work on current dates is unaffected. ⚠️ Reconcile as you go — monthly, not all in December — and set the lock date only once your checklist is complete. Unsure about an adjusting entry like depreciation? Ask your accountant; it affects official figures. 15 E-Invoicing Online Payments Sending invoices in a legal machine-readable format, and letting customers pay you online. Electronic invoicing An e-invoice is a structured digital file (usually XML) another system can read automatically — not just a PDF a human retypes. Governments increasingly require it (faster tax reporting, less fraud). Odoo supports many regional formats — Factur-X (FR/DE), Peppol/UBL (Europe-wide, free even in Community), XRechnung , NLCIUS . Set the format per customer in their Accounting tab; general settings under Settings → Peppol Electronic Invoicing . Odoo then attaches the right file automatically. Online payment providers Services like Stripe and PayPal let customers pay invoices online by card. Odoo marks the invoice paid automatically, and the provider handles the card data — so your business never stores card numbers . Configure under Accounting → Configuration → Payment Providers . ⛔ Disabled Off — customers can't use it. 🧪 Test mode Safe sandbox with fake credentials — no real money . Always test here first. 💡 Rule of thumb: online payment providers make money come in faster; e-invoicing keeps you legally compliant in how invoices are sent. Set a provider to Test mode , run a fake payment, then switch to Enabled for real money. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Journal entry The bookkeeping record of a transaction — money moving between accounts. Journal item One debit or credit line inside a journal entry. Journal A logbook grouping one kind of transaction (Sales, Purchases, Bank, Cash). Debit Credit The two sides of every entry. They must be equal — that keeps the books balanced. Accounts Receivable Money customers owe you. Accounts Payable Money you owe suppliers. Reconciliation Matching a real bank movement to its record in Odoo, so both agree. Posting Confirming a document so it becomes official and creates its journal entry. Credit note A reverse-invoice used to cancel, correct or refund a posted invoice. Payment terms The rules for when a customer must pay (e.g. Net 30). Fiscal localization A country starter pack: chart of accounts + taxes + reports. Fiscal position A rule that auto-swaps taxes/accounts based on the customer's situation. IBAN / BIC (SWIFT) Your international bank account number and your bank's identifier code. Opening balance The starting figure in an account on the day you begin using Odoo. Exchange rate How much one currency is worth in another (1 EUR = 1.08 USD). Analytic account An internal tag (project, department…) for tracking where money goes — separate from the legal books. Depreciation Spreading a long-lived asset's cost across the years you use it. Deferred revenue / expense Income or cost spread over future periods instead of counted all at once. Lock date A cutoff date; transactions on or before it become read-only, freezing the past. E-invoice An invoice sent as a structured file a computer can read, not just a PDF. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Accounting docs 🌍 Fiscal Localizations 🧾 Customer Invoices 💳 Payments 📊 Reporting ▶️ Getting Started video Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Accounting Invoicing · You've got this 💪 ↑ // Back-to-top button var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){ if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')} }); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); // Scroll-spy: highlight the current chapter in the sidebar var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={}; links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){ entries.forEach(function(e){ if(e.isIntersecting){ links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')}); var a=map[e.target.id]; if(a){a.classList.add('active')} } }) },{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-elearning-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo eLearning — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo eLearning — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Online Courses Odoo eLearning Build and publish online courses — for training staff internally or teaching customers, with quizzes, certificates and progress tracking. Odoo 19 · eLearning Chapters 1 What eLearning Is 2 Courses 3 Content Types 4 Access & Enrolment 5 Quizzes & Certification 6 Gamification 7 Publishing & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo eLearning lets you build online courses from videos, documents and quizzes. We use it to onboard new joiners and to teach customers how our products work. Learners watch, read, take quizzes and earn certificates — and you can see exactly how far each person has got. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea A course is a list of content grouped into sections . Content can be a video, a document, a web page or a quiz . Set who can enrol , optionally add a certification , publish it — and Odoo tracks every learner’s progress. 1 What eLearning Is Package knowledge into courses people work through at their own pace. eLearning is Odoo’s course builder. You assemble lessons, decide who can take the course, and publish it on your website. Learners progress through the content while Odoo records completion — perfect for repeatable training you don’t want to deliver live every time. 🎓 Course A complete lesson set on one topic. 🧱 Content A single lesson: video, document, page or quiz. 📑 Section A group of contents inside a course. 🏅 Certification A graded quiz that awards a certificate. 2 Courses The container for a topic’s lessons. eLearning → New (course) Give the course a title , a description, tags and a cover image. Inside, organise lessons into sections (e.g. “Basics”, “Advanced”) so long courses stay tidy. Reorder content by dragging. 3 Content Types Mix formats to keep courses engaging. 🎬 Video An embedded or uploaded video lesson. 📄 Document A PDF or slide deck to read. 🌐 Web page A rich page written right in Odoo. ❓ Quiz Questions that test and reinforce learning. Add content inside a course and pick its type. Each item can require the learner to finish it before the next unlocks, if you want a strict path. 4 Access & Enrolment Decide who can take the course and how they get in. Enrol policy Meaning Public / Free Anyone can take it. On invitation Only people you invite can join. On payment Learners buy access (ties into eCommerce). Course visibility controls who even sees it exists (everyone, signed-in members, or invited only). Combine visibility + enrol policy to make courses public, members-only or paid. 5 Quizzes & Certification Check understanding and hand out proof of completion. A quiz is content made of questions, each worth points. A certification course requires passing a quiz to earn a certificate the learner can share. Great for “you must pass this to be signed off” training. 💡 Keep quizzes short and focused on the must-know points — they reinforce learning as much as they test it. 6 Gamification & Community Points and badges that make learning stick. ⭐ Karma Points learners earn for finishing content and passing quizzes. 🏅 Badges Awards for milestones — recognition that motivates. 🥇 Ranks Levels reached as karma grows. 💬 Reviews & Forum Learners can rate courses and ask questions. ℹ️ Gamification is optional — turn it on when you want engagement, leave it off for straightforward internal training. 7 Publishing & Reporting Put the course live, then watch progress. Courses live on your website . Toggle Published to make a course visible; unpublish to hide a draft. Once live, reporting shows attendees , how far each has progressed, and completion/quiz results — so you know who has actually done the training. 💡 For onboarding, check the attendee list to confirm new joiners have completed the required courses. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Course A complete set of lessons on one topic. Content A single lesson: video, document, web page or quiz. Section A group of contents within a course. Enrol policy Who can join: public, on invitation, or on payment. Visibility Who can see that a course exists. Quiz Question-based content that tests learning. Certification A course that awards a certificate on passing. Karma Points earned for learning activity. Badge / Rank Awards and levels that gamify progress. Attendee A learner enrolled in a course. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 eLearning docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ eLearning basics (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 eLearning · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-getting-started-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Getting Started — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Getting Started — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Odoo Basics Odoo Getting Started The complete beginner's guide to finding your way around Odoo — the screen, the apps, the buttons and the habits. No prior Odoo experience needed. Odoo 19 · Getting Started Chapters 1 What Odoo Is 2 Logging In the Screen 3 Apps Modules 4 Views 5 Working with Records 6 Finding Things 7 The Chatter 8 Activities Discuss 9 Users Companies 10 Settings Dev Mode Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo is the software the whole business runs on — sales, projects, support, stock, accounting and more, all in one place . Before you learn any single app, it helps to learn the ground rules : how to log in, move around, create and find records, and use the little panel that follows you everywhere. Learn these once and every Odoo app suddenly feels familiar. Read top to bottom, or jump around with the menu on the left. Nothing to memorise — you'll build the habits by clicking around. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea Odoo is modular . It's one database with many apps bolted on — Sales, Inventory, Helpdesk, Accounting… — that all share the same customers, products and users. Because every app is built from the same building blocks, once you learn how one screen works, you know how all of them work. This handbook teaches those shared building blocks. 1 What Odoo Is A single, connected business system — not a pile of separate programs. Odoo is an ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) system: one place where a company manages most of what it does. What makes it special is that all the pieces are connected . Sell a product in Sales and the stock drops in Inventory , an invoice appears in Accounting , and the customer's history updates — automatically. No copy-pasting between tools. Apps, not programs Each business area is an app you switch on when you need it. You only see the apps that are installed. A small company might run three; a big one, thirty. They all live in the same database and share the same data. 🧩 Modular Turn apps on/off as needs change. Start small, grow later. 🔗 Connected One customer, one product list — shared by every app. 🎯 Consistent Every app uses the same buttons, views and search. Learn once. 🌐 Web-based Runs in your browser. Nothing to install on your laptop. Community vs Enterprise Odoo comes in two editions. Community is the free, open-source core. Enterprise is a paid subscription that adds more apps and features (accounting automation, studio, more integrations) plus official support. At much. you'll mostly work with Enterprise features. ℹ️ Where Odoo runs: it can be hosted by Odoo ( Odoo Online ), on a managed platform ( Odoo.sh ), or on a company's own servers ( on-premise ). As a user the screen looks the same everywhere — the difference is who maintains the server. 2 Logging In the Screen Your first five minutes: where everything lives on screen. Getting in Open the database's web address in your browser, enter your email and password , and you land on your home screen. Bookmark the address. If you forget your password, use Reset Password on the login page (it emails you a link). The four corners of the screen Almost every Odoo screen has the same frame. Learn these four spots and you'll never feel lost: 1 ▦ Apps menu — top-left Click the icon (or the current app's name) to open the grid of all your apps . This is how you jump from Sales to Inventory to Helpdesk. Your main way to switch apps 2 ☰ Top menu bar Inside an app, the bar along the top holds that app's menus (e.g. in Sales: Orders, Products, Reporting, Configuration). It changes with each app. Navigate within the current app 3 🍞 Breadcrumbs The trail just under the top bar (e.g. Orders › S00023 ) shows where you are. Click any earlier step to go back without losing your place . Always know where you are 4 🔔👤 Right side Top-right holds the Activities (clock) and Messages (speech bubble) icons, and your user menu (your name/avatar) for preferences and logging out. Your notifications profile 💡 Lost? Click the app name (top-left) then your app again — it takes you to that app's home. And breadcrumbs are your friend: click backwards through them instead of the browser's Back button. 3 Apps Modules How apps get added, and what \"installing\" really means. The Apps app is the store where you browse and install everything Odoo offers. Installing an app switches on its menus, screens and automations across the whole database — for everyone , not just you. Apps → search a name (e.g. \"Project\") → Install App vs module — same thing, different size 📱 App A big, user-facing area like Sales or CRM. What you see as a tile on your home screen. 🧱 Module A smaller add-on that extends an app (a specific integration, report or tweak). Same install mechanism. ⚠️ Installing changes the database for everyone. On a real customer system, apps are installed deliberately by the project team — not clicked on to \"have a look\". Explore freely in a test database; be careful in production. ℹ️ Some technical modules are hidden until you switch on Developer Mode (Chapter 10) and remove the \"Apps\" filter. You rarely need those as a new joiner. 4 Views The same data, shown in different shapes. Switch views with the little icons at the top-right of a list. A view is just a way of displaying records. The same 200 sales orders can be shown as a spreadsheet-like list, a drag-and-drop board, or a chart — you pick whichever helps right now. Not every screen offers every view; you'll see icons for the ones available. 📄 List Rows columns, like a spreadsheet. Great for scanning many records. 🗂️ Kanban Cards in columns you drag between stages. Great for pipelines tasks. 📝 Form One record in full detail. Where you actually read and edit. 📅 Calendar Records placed on dates. Great for meetings, deadlines, activities. 📊 Pivot A cross-table to summarise numbers (totals by month, by salesperson…). 📈 Graph Bar, line or pie chart of the same data. ⏰ Activity A grid of who owes what follow-up, by type. 🗺️ Map Records with an address plotted on a map. 💡 Switching view keeps your filters. Filter a list down to \"this month's orders\", flip to the Graph icon, and you're charting exactly that subset. Views are just different lenses on the same filtered data. 5 Working with Records Create, edit, save, and the safe ways to get rid of things. A record is a single item — one customer, one product, one order. You open one in a form view , and everything you do to your data is really \"editing a record\". Create save 1 Click New to start a blank record. 2 Fill the fields. Bold / highlighted fields are required — Odoo won't save without them. 3 Odoo saves as you go : an unsaved record shows a cloud/☁ or the breadcrumb turns into Save (✓) and Discard (✕) buttons. Click ✓ to keep, ✕ to throw away. ⚠️ Don't navigate away with unsaved changes. If you see the Save/Discard buttons, decide first — otherwise Odoo will nag you (or lose the edit). When in doubt, hit Discard to safely undo everything since you opened the record. The cog / action menu On most forms a ⚙ Actions menu (or the gear icon) offers extra options for that record: 📑 Duplicate Copy a record to make a similar one quickly. 🗄️ Archive Hide a record you no longer use without deleting it . It disappears from normal views but data (and history) stays. The safe choice. 🗑️ Delete Permanently remove. Often blocked once a record is linked to accounting or confirmed documents. 🔗 Share / Export Export selected records to a spreadsheet, or generate a shareable link where supported. ℹ️ Archive, don't delete. If you're unsure, archive. It's reversible (filter for \"Archived\" and un-archive), and it never breaks links to past documents the way deleting can. 6 Finding Things The search bar is the most powerful tool on the screen once you know its three jobs. The bar at the top of any list does far more than text search. Click into it and you'll see three menus drop down: 🔎 Search — type a word; Odoo suggests searching by name, customer, reference, etc. Pick which field you meant. ⛃ Filters — pre-built conditions like My Orders , This Month , To Invoice . Combine several to narrow down. ▤ Group By — stack records into groups (by Salesperson, by Stage, by Month). Turns a flat list into a collapsible summary. Save a search as a Favorite Built the perfect filter + grouping? Open Favorites → Save current search , name it, and it's one click next time. You can even set it as your default for that screen, or share it with the team. 💡 A \"wrong-looking\" list is usually a leftover filter. If records seem missing, check the coloured filter tags in the search bar and click the ✕ on each to clear them. Filters stick as you move around. 7 The Chatter The panel beside (or below) most forms — the record's built-in history, notes and messaging. You'll use it constantly. The chatter turns every record into a little conversation and audit trail. Open a customer, an order or a ticket and you'll see it: a message box, a list of followers, and a timeline of everything that ever happened to that record. Send Message vs Log Note — know the difference ✉️ Send message Goes out — it emails the followers of the record (often the customer). Use for external communication. 🗒️ Log note Stays internal — a private note for colleagues. The customer never sees it. Use for \"called them, no answer\". ⚠️ This is the classic new-joiner trap. Send message can email the customer; Log note does not. If it's an internal comment, always use Log note . Check which tab you're on before you type. The rest of the chatter 👥 Followers People who get notified about this record. Add colleagues so they stay in the loop; remove yourself to stop notifications. ⏰ Activities Schedule a to-do on the record — \"Call back Tuesday\". More in Chapter 8. 📎 Attachments Drag files straight onto the chatter to store them with the record. 🕓 Tracked history Odoo auto-logs important changes (\"Stage: New → Won\") so you can see who changed what, when. 💡 Use @mentions. Type @ and a colleague's name in a log note to ping them directly — they get a notification without you leaving the record. 8 Activities Discuss How Odoo keeps track of what you need to do, and how the team chats. Activities — your to-do list, attached to real work An activity is a scheduled to-do linked to a record: a call, an email, a meeting, an upload. Instead of a separate to-do app, the reminder lives on the order/ticket/customer it's about. The clock icon (top-right) shows everything due across all apps — red = overdue , amber = today , grey = later. 1 On any record's chatter, click Activities → pick a type, a due date and a note. 2 It appears in your clock menu and on the Activity view of that app. 3 Done it? Click Mark Done — optionally logging a note and scheduling the next one in one go. Discuss — the built-in chat The Discuss app (speech-bubble icon) is Odoo's internal messaging: direct messages, and channels for teams or topics (like Slack/Teams). Messages you're mentioned in, plus follower notifications from records, all gather here so nothing slips through. 💡 Live by the clock icon. Start your day there: clear the red overdue items first. If work isn't scheduled as an activity, it's easy to forget — so schedule the next step before you close a record. 9 Users, Companies Your Preferences Who can do what, working across multiple companies, and setting up your own profile. Users access rights Every person is a user . What each user can see and do is controlled by access rights — grouped into roles per app (e.g. in Sales: User vs Administrator ). This is why a colleague might see a menu you don't: different rights, same database. 👤 Internal user A staff member with a proper login and app access. (These are what a subscription counts.) 🌐 Portal user An external contact (customer/vendor) who only sees their own documents through a limited portal — no back-office access. Multi-company One Odoo database can hold several companies . If you have access to more than one, a company switcher appears near your name — tick which companies you're working in. Records belong to a company, so the switcher controls what you see. Your preferences Open the user menu (your avatar, top-right) → Preferences / My Profile to set the things that make Odoo yours: Language — the whole interface translates instantly. Timezone — get this right or your times/deadlines will look off. Email signature — added to messages you send from Odoo. Notification preference — handle notifications by email or in-app (Discuss). ℹ️ Set your timezone first. A wrong timezone is the #1 reason activities and meeting times look one or two hours off for new joiners. 10 Settings Developer Mode Where behaviour is configured, and the \"show me more\" switch consultants use daily. Settings The Settings app has a General Settings page plus a section for each installed app . This is where features are switched on/off and defaults are set. Two habits: 1 Use the app filter/menu at the left of Settings to jump straight to, say, Sales settings. 2 Toggling a setting often reveals more options below it — read before you save. Developer Mode Developer (debug) mode unlocks technical information helpful when configuring or troubleshooting: real field names, model names, extra menus, and the ability to inspect automated rules. It changes what you can see , not your data. Settings → scroll to the bottom of General Settings → Developer Tools → Activate the developer mode 🔍 See field names Hover a field to get its technical name — essential when writing docs, imports or asking for help. 🐞 The bug menu A ⚙/bug icon appears in the top bar with tools to view the record's metadata, fields and automations. ⚠️ Developer mode is for looking, not breaking. It exposes powerful technical screens. As a new joiner, use it to read field/model names and understand the setup — don't change technical settings on a customer's live database without guidance. 💡 Turn it off when done (same place → Deactivate ) so your day-to-day screens stay clean and simple. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. ERP Enterprise Resource Planning — one connected system that runs many parts of a business. App / Module A switch-on-able business area (app) or smaller add-on (module) inside the same database. Community / Enterprise The free open-source edition vs the paid edition with more features and support. Record A single item of data — one customer, product, order or ticket. View A way of displaying records: List, Kanban, Form, Calendar, Pivot, Graph, Activity, Map. Form view The detailed screen for one record, where you read and edit it. Breadcrumbs The clickable trail showing where you are, used to step back without losing your place. Chatter The messaging + history panel on a record: messages, log notes, followers, activities, attachments. Send message vs Log note A message notifies followers (can email the customer); a log note is internal-only. Follower A person who gets notified about changes to a record. Activity A scheduled to-do (call, email, meeting) attached to a record; shown under the clock icon. Discuss Odoo's built-in chat — direct messages and team channels. Filter / Group By / Favorite Search tools: narrow records, stack them into groups, and save a search for reuse. Archive Hide a record without deleting it — reversible and safe. User (internal / portal) A staff login with back-office access, vs an external contact with a limited portal. Access rights Rules that decide what each user can see and do, grouped into roles per app. Multi-company Several companies in one database, chosen with the company switcher. Developer mode A switch that reveals technical info (field/model names, debug tools) for configuring and troubleshooting. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Odoo docs ⚙️ General settings docs 🎓 Free courses (eLearning) 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Navigate in Odoo (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Getting Started · You've got this 💪 ↑ // Back-to-top button var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){ if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')} }); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); // Scroll-spy: highlight the current chapter in the sidebar var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={}; links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){ entries.forEach(function(e){ if(e.isIntersecting){ links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')}); var a=map[e.target.id]; if(a){a.classList.add('active')} } }) },{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-helpdesk-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Helpdesk — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Helpdesk — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Customer Support Odoo Helpdesk How support teams receive, prioritise and resolve customer tickets — with deadlines (SLAs) and satisfaction tracking built in. Odoo 19 · Helpdesk Chapters 1 What Helpdesk Is 2 Helpdesk Teams 3 Channels 4 Tickets 5 Stages & Pipeline 6 SLA Policies 7 Working & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Helpdesk is where customer questions and problems become tickets that a team works through to resolution. It brings every channel — email, web form, live chat — into one pipeline, adds deadlines (SLAs) so nothing is forgotten, and measures how happy customers are afterwards. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea A ticket is just a task for support . It arrives through a channel , lands in a team , moves across stages until solved, and is answered through the same chatter you already know. Master those four words and Helpdesk is easy. 1 What Helpdesk Is Turn scattered customer requests into a single, trackable queue. Instead of support living in someone’s inbox, Helpdesk collects every request as a ticket in a shared pipeline . The team sees what is open, who owns it, how urgent it is and whether it is running late — so problems get solved instead of lost. 🎫 Ticket One customer request or problem. 👥 Team A group that handles a category of tickets. 🗂️ Stage Where the ticket is: New → In Progress → Solved. ⏰ SLA A promised deadline to respond or resolve. 2 Helpdesk Teams A team defines who handles which tickets, and how they are shared out. Helpdesk → Configuration → Teams → New Each team has its own pipeline and settings. The key choice is the assignment method — how new tickets are given to agents: ✋ Manually Agents pick tickets from the queue themselves. 🎲 Randomly Odoo spreads tickets evenly at random. ⚖️ Balanced Odoo assigns to whoever has the fewest open tickets. ℹ️ You also set visibility (who can see the team’s tickets) and which channels feed it — covered next. 3 Channels — how tickets arrive Customers reach you many ways; Helpdesk funnels them all into tickets. ✉️ Email alias — customers email e.g. support@company.com and a ticket is created automatically. 🌐 Website form — a “Submit a ticket” page on your site. 💬 Live chat — a chat widget on the site can spin a conversation into a ticket. ⌨️ Manual — an agent creates a ticket by hand (e.g. after a phone call). 💡 Turn channels on per team in the team’s settings. The email alias is the workhorse for most support teams. 4 Tickets — the ticket form Everything about one request in one place. 1 🧑 Customer Who reported it — links their contact and history. Auto-filled from email 2 📝 Subject & Description What the problem is. 3 ⚡ Priority Star rating that pushes urgent tickets up. Drives SLA targets 4 🏷️ Tags & Assignee Categorise the ticket and set who owns it. 5 ⏰ SLA deadline The promised response/resolution time. Colour shows if at risk 5 Stages & Pipeline Tickets flow across a kanban board just like project tasks. 1 New — just arrived, unassigned or unstarted. 2 In Progress — an agent is working it. 3 Solved — answer given, awaiting confirmation. 4 Done / Closed — resolved and put to rest. Drag tickets between stages as you work. A folded stage (like Done) keeps the board tidy. You can merge duplicate tickets so one conversation holds the whole story. 6 SLA Policies A promise to answer within a set time — measured automatically. An SLA (Service Level Agreement) policy sets a target like “ High-priority tickets reach Solved within 8 working hours ”. Odoo tracks the clock and colours the ticket: on time or breached . Policies can depend on priority , tags or team . Helpdesk → Configuration → SLA Policies ℹ️ SLAs measure in working hours , not wall-clock time — so a Friday-evening ticket isn’t unfairly “late” by Monday. 7 Working a Ticket & Reporting Answer through the chatter, and learn from the numbers. Reply to the customer with Send message ; keep colleague notes as Log note (the same message/note rule from Getting Started — get this right so customers never see internal chatter). If enabled, log time on the ticket via a Timesheets tab. Close when resolved. Satisfaction & reports ⭐ Ratings After closing, customers rate the help — great/okay/bad. ⏰ SLA Success Share of tickets that met their deadline. 📊 Ticket Analysis Volume, resolution time and load by team/agent. 📚 Help Center Publish Knowledge articles so customers self-serve. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Ticket A single customer request or problem tracked to resolution. Team A group handling a category of tickets, with its own pipeline. Assignment method How new tickets are shared out: manual, random or balanced. Channel A way tickets arrive: email, web form, live chat or manual. Stage A kanban column showing a ticket’s progress. SLA A promised response/resolution deadline, tracked automatically. Priority A star rating that ranks urgency and can drive SLAs. Merge Combining duplicate tickets into one. Rating A customer’s satisfaction score after a ticket closes. Help Center A public site of Knowledge articles for customer self-service. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Helpdesk docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Helpdesk overview (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Helpdesk · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-inventory-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Inventory — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Inventory — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Stock & Warehouses Odoo Inventory How goods move through warehouses and locations — receipts, deliveries and transfers — using Odoo’s elegant “stock is never created or destroyed” model. Odoo 19 · Inventory Chapters 1 What Inventory Is 2 Products & Types 3 Operations 4 Double-Entry Stock 5 Replenishment 6 Traceability 7 Counts & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Inventory tracks physical goods: what you have, where it is, and every move it makes. It underpins Sales, Purchase and our warehouse integrations (Shopware, Order Base). The core idea is beautifully simple once it clicks — stock is only ever moved between locations . 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea Stock is never created or destroyed — only moved . Every receipt, delivery or adjustment is a move of product from one location to another (even “Vendors” and “Customers” are locations). Get this and all of Inventory makes sense. 1 What Inventory Is Always know how much of each product you have, and where. Inventory keeps a live count of stock across warehouses and the locations inside them (shelves, zones). Every physical event — a supplier delivery, a customer shipment, moving goods between shelves — is recorded so the numbers stay accurate without manual tallies. 🏭 Warehouse A physical site that holds stock. 📍 Location A precise spot inside (or outside) a warehouse. 🔄 Move Stock going from one location to another. 📦 Operation A grouped set of moves: receipt, delivery, transfer. 2 Products & Stock Types Only some products are actually counted. 📦 Storable Stock is tracked — counts go up and down. The real inventory items. 🧰 Consumable Handled but not counted (e.g. packing tape). 🛎️ Service No physical stock at all (e.g. a support hour). Each product also has a unit of measure (pieces, kg, litres). Odoo can even convert between buying and selling units. ℹ️ If a product’s stock “isn’t moving”, check its type — only storable products are tracked. 3 Operations — Receipts, Deliveries, Transfers The three everyday jobs, all visible on the Inventory dashboard. 📥 Receipts Goods coming IN from a supplier. 📤 Deliveries Goods going OUT to a customer. 🔀 Internal Transfers Goods moving between your own locations. The Inventory Overview dashboard shows a card per operation type with a “to process” count. Click in, open a transfer, and Validate it to confirm the goods actually moved. 4 The Double-Entry Stock Model The one concept that explains everything else. Just like accounting balances debits and credits, Inventory balances every move between two locations . Stock is never invented — it comes from somewhere and goes to somewhere. Odoo models even the outside world as locations: Event From location To location Receipt Vendors Stock Delivery Stock Customers Internal transfer Shelf A Shelf B Scrap Stock Scrap 💡 Because “Vendors” and “Customers” are locations too, every unit is always accounted for — this is what makes Odoo’s stock traceable end-to-end. 5 Replenishment Never run out — let Odoo tell you when to buy or make more. A reordering rule sets a minimum and maximum for a product at a location. When stock drops below the minimum, Odoo proposes replenishing back up to the maximum (a purchase order or manufacturing order). The Replenishment screen lists everything that needs ordering. ℹ️ Set realistic lead times (how long a supplier takes) so reordering triggers early enough to avoid stockouts. 6 Traceability — Lots & Serials Track exactly which unit went where. 🔢 Lot numbers A batch shares one number — good for food, pharma, batches. #️⃣ Serial numbers Every single unit gets its own unique number. 🧭 Traceability report Follow a lot/serial’s whole journey in and out. ⏳ Expiry Optionally track best-before dates on lots. Turn tracking on per product. It’s essential where you must prove where a specific unit came from or went. 7 Counts, Adjustments & Reporting Keep the numbers honest and read the stock story. Real shelves drift from the system. A physical inventory / count lets you enter the counted quantity and Odoo posts an adjustment move to reconcile. Reporting then answers the big questions: 📊 On-hand What you physically have now, by location. 🔮 Forecasted Future stock, factoring incoming and outgoing orders. 💰 Valuation What your stock is worth — feeds accounting. 📚 Moves History Every move ever, fully searchable. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Warehouse A physical site that holds stock. Location A precise place inside or outside a warehouse. Move Stock going from one location to another. Operation / Transfer A grouped set of moves: receipt, delivery or internal transfer. Storable / Consumable / Service Product types — only storable is stock-tracked. Reordering rule A min/max that triggers replenishment. Lead time How long replenishment takes to arrive. Lot / Serial number Batch vs per-unit traceability identifiers. Adjustment A move that corrects system stock to match a physical count. Valuation The monetary value of stock on hand. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Inventory docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Inventory basics (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Inventory · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-knowledge-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Knowledge — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Knowledge — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Company Wiki Odoo Knowledge Odoo’s built-in wiki — a tree of articles for capturing product know-how, processes and notes the whole team can find. Odoo 19 · Knowledge Chapters 1 What Knowledge Is 2 The Article Tree 3 Writing Articles 4 Content Blocks 5 Sharing & Permissions 6 Organising 7 Knowledge + Apps Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Knowledge is where the company writes things down: how products work, how processes run, meeting notes, checklists. It is a living tree of articles that everyone can search, so know-how lives in one place instead of in people’s heads and old chats. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea Knowledge is a nested tree of articles . Articles can contain other articles, live in a shared workspace or stay private , and are written in a rich editor driven by the “/” command . That’s the whole model. 1 What Knowledge Is One searchable home for everything the team needs to remember. Knowledge replaces scattered documents and “ask a colleague” with a single, structured wiki. Every note is an article ; articles nest inside each other to form a tidy tree; and a fast search finds anything in seconds. 📄 Article One page of content — a note, guide or checklist. 🌳 Tree Articles nested under articles, forming sections. 🏢 Workspace The shared area everyone in the company can see. 🔒 Private Articles only you (or invited people) can see. 2 The Article Tree The left sidebar is your table of contents for the whole company. Articles are grouped into three areas in the sidebar: 🏢 Workspace Shared with the whole company — the main knowledge base. 👥 Shared Articles shared with specific people. 🔒 Private Your personal drafts and notes. Click the ＋ next to any article to add a child article beneath it — that is how you build sections and sub-sections. Drag articles in the tree to reorganise. 3 Writing — the editor & “/” command Type like a document; press “/” whenever you want to insert something. The editor works like any rich text tool — headings, bold, lists, links. The magic key is / : type it anywhere to open the powerbox , a menu of everything you can drop in (a table, an image, a checklist, a file, a heading, an embedded view…). 💡 Learn one shortcut and you’re fluent: / then start typing what you want (“table”, “image”, “todo”) and press Enter. 4 Content Blocks Articles hold more than text — they can embed live, interactive pieces. ☑️ Checklists Tick-off to-do lists inside an article. 📊 Tables Simple tables for structured info. 📎 Files & images Drag in attachments and screenshots. 🗂️ Embedded views Drop in a live list/kanban of real Odoo records. 🧩 Templates Reusable article layouts to start faster. 🔗 Links Link to other articles or to any Odoo record. ℹ️ An embedded view means an article can show a live to-do list or kanban that stays in sync with real data — not a stale copy. 5 Sharing & Permissions Control who can read and who can edit each branch of the tree. Use Share on an article to set access. Permissions flow down the tree, so setting them on a parent covers its children. Levels are typically No access , Can read , or Can write . You can invite specific colleagues, or publish an article to the web for anyone with the link. Level What it allows No access Hidden from that person. Can read View only. Can write View and edit. 6 Organising Articles Small touches that keep a growing wiki usable. ⭐ Favourites Star the articles you open often for one-click access. 🎨 Icon & cover Give articles an emoji icon and cover image to scan visually. 🏷️ Properties Add custom fields (owner, status, tags) to articles. 🕓 History See past versions and who changed what. 💡 Agree a simple top-level structure early (e.g. Products / Processes / Clients) so the tree stays navigable as it grows. 7 Knowledge with Other Apps Knowledge isn’t an island — it plugs into the rest of Odoo. You can insert an article link into a record’s chatter (e.g. attach a “how-to” to a ticket), and Helpdesk can turn Knowledge articles into a public Help Center so customers self-serve. Articles can also embed live views of records from any app. ℹ️ Writing a process once in Knowledge and linking it everywhere beats re-explaining it in every ticket and email. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Article A single page of content in Knowledge. Tree The nested structure of articles and child articles. Workspace The company-wide shared area of the tree. Private / Shared Articles only you see vs shared with specific people. Child article An article nested beneath another. Powerbox (“/”) The insert menu opened by typing a slash. Embedded view A live list/kanban of real records inside an article. Template A reusable article layout. Permission Access level on an article: no access, read, or write. Help Center A public site built from Knowledge articles. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Knowledge docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Knowledge & Odoo apps (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Knowledge · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-project-timesheets-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Project & Timesheets — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Project & Timesheets — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Project Management Odoo Project & Timesheets How we plan work, track it across stages, and log the time we spend — the backbone of delivery at a consultancy. Odoo 19 · Project & Timesheets Chapters 1 What Project Is 2 Projects & Stages 3 Tasks 4 Kanban & Lifecycle 5 Timesheets 6 Planning & Progress 7 Collaboration & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Project is where the team plans and tracks work, and Timesheets is how we record the hours we spend on it. Together they answer the two questions a consultancy lives by: “what needs doing?” and “where did our time go?” Learn the handful of ideas below and you can run any project board. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea Everything is a task inside a project . A project is the container (a client, an initiative); tasks are the pieces of work inside it that move across stages from “to do” to “done”. Log your hours as timesheets on those tasks, and Odoo shows planned-vs-real time automatically. 1 What Project Is Organise work into projects and tasks, and see status at a glance — no “where are we?” emails. Odoo Project turns work into visible, trackable items. Each project holds many tasks , and each task sits in a stage (a kanban column) that shows how far along it is. Anyone can open the board and instantly see what is in progress, what is blocked, and what is done. 📁 Project A container for related work — often one client or initiative. ✅ Task A single piece of work: “Configure Helpdesk SLA”. 🗂️ Stage A column (To Do → In Progress → Done) showing progress. ⏱️ Timesheet Hours logged against a task. 2 Projects & Stages Create the container and shape its columns to match how your team works. Project → New Give the project a name , optionally link a customer , and choose whether it allows timesheets and billing . Each project has its own stages — rename or add columns (e.g. To Do , In Progress , Review , Done ) so the board mirrors your real process. ℹ️ A project can be tied to a customer and a sales order , so tasks and logged time trace straight back to what was sold and can be billed. 3 Tasks — the unit of work The task form is where the actual work is described, assigned and scheduled. 1 👤 Assignees Who is doing it. A task can have one or several people. Filter “My Tasks” by this 2 📅 Deadline When it is due — drives the calendar and overdue warnings. Feeds the Calendar view 3 ⏳ Planned hours Your estimate of the effort — compared later against logged time. Powers progress bars 4 🏷️ Tags & Priority Label and star tasks so the important ones surface first. Star = high priority Big tasks can be split into sub-tasks , and you can set dependencies so one task only starts once another is finished. 4 Kanban Stages & Task Lifecycle Work flows left-to-right across the board. You move it by dragging. Drag a card from one stage to the next as work progresses. Each card also has a kanban state — a small traffic light: green “ready” , red “blocked” , or grey “in progress” — to flag problems without moving the card. 1 To Do — captured, not started. 2 In Progress — someone is actively working it. 3 Review / Done — finished, or awaiting a check. ℹ️ Personal stages let each person organise their own view of tasks without changing the shared project columns. 5 Timesheets — logging time Record how long work actually took. This is what makes billing and estimates honest. With the Timesheets app installed and the project set to allow it, every task gets a Timesheets tab . Add a line with the date, a description and the hours — or use the Grid view to fill a whole week at once. Project task → Timesheets tab → Add a line · or Timesheets app → Grid 💡 Use the built-in timer (start/stop on a task) to capture time as you work instead of guessing at the end of the day. 6 Planning & Progress Estimate vs reality, at a glance. Each task compares planned hours (your estimate) with effective hours (what you logged). A progress bar shows how much of the estimate is used — so you spot a task about to blow its budget while there is still time to react. Term Plain meaning Planned / Allocated hours What you estimated the task would take. Effective hours What was actually logged via timesheets. Remaining Planned minus effective — budget left. 7 Collaboration & Reporting Keep the conversation on the work, and read the numbers that come out of it. Every task has the chatter (Chapter 7 of the Getting Started handbook): message the customer, log internal notes, @mention colleagues, attach files and schedule follow-up activities — all pinned to the task. Reports worth knowing 📊 Task Analysis Counts and status of tasks by project, stage, assignee. ⏱️ Timesheets Analysis Hours by person, project or task — the billing view. 📉 Burndown Work remaining over time — are we on track to finish? 🗓️ Project dashboard Per-project overview of open work and logged time. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Project A container for related tasks — usually a client or initiative. Task A single piece of work that moves across stages. Sub-task A smaller task nested under a parent task. Stage A kanban column showing how far along a task is. Kanban state A task’s traffic light: ready (green), blocked (red) or in progress. Personal stage Your own private way to organise tasks, separate from shared columns. Timesheet A logged entry of hours spent on a task. Planned / Effective hours The estimated effort vs the hours actually logged. Dependency A rule that one task can’t start until another is done. Billable Whether logged time can be invoiced to a customer. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Project docs ⏱️ Timesheets docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Project basics (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Project & Timesheets · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-sales-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Sales — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Sales — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Quotations to Orders Odoo Sales From a quotation to a confirmed sales order to an invoice — the core selling flow that most Odoo integrations plug into. Odoo 19 · Sales Chapters 1 What Sales Is 2 Products 3 Quotations 4 Quote → Order 5 Pricing 6 Order to Cash 7 Customers & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Sales is where selling happens: you build a quotation , send it, the customer accepts, it becomes a sales order , and that drives delivery and invoicing. Almost every integration we build touches Sales, so this flow is worth knowing cold. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea One document changes state. A quotation and a sales order are the same record at different stages: Draft → Sent → confirmed Sales Order . Confirming it is the moment a proposal becomes a commitment that feeds Inventory and Accounting. 1 What Sales Is Turn interest into a priced offer, then into a firm order. Sales manages the paperwork of selling: quotations (offers), sales orders (accepted offers), and the hand-off to delivery and invoicing. Because it shares products and customers with every other app, a confirmed order automatically ripples out to stock and accounting. 🧾 Quotation A draft offer you send a customer. ✅ Sales Order A confirmed, accepted quotation. 📦 Order line One product + quantity + price on the order. 💶 Invoice The bill created from the order. 2 Products for Selling What you put on an order — set up once, reused everywhere. A product carries the info Sales needs: its sales price , taxes , unit of measure , and its type . The type matters: 📦 Storable Tracked in Inventory — stock goes up/down. 🧰 Consumable Bought/sold but stock isn’t tracked. 🛎️ Service No stock — e.g. consulting hours. The invoicing policy (ordered vs delivered) decides whether you can bill on order or only after delivery — see Chapter 6. 3 Quotations Build the offer. Sales → Orders → New 1 🧑 Customer Pick who you’re quoting — auto-fills address, terms, pricelist. 2 📦 Order lines Add products; price, tax and description auto-fill. Set quantities. 3 ➕ Optional products Suggest add-ons the customer can tick to include. Upsell nicely 4 📄 Quotation template Start from a saved template for common offers. Big time-saver 4 Quotation → Sales Order The lifecycle, and the moment of commitment. 1 Draft (Quotation) — editable proposal, nothing committed. 2 Sent — emailed to the customer as a PDF / online quote. 3 Sales Order — customer accepts → you Confirm . Now it’s a commitment. 4 Locked — finalised so it can’t be changed by mistake. ℹ️ Customers can accept, e-sign and even pay online from the quotation link — which confirms the order automatically. 5 Pricing — Pricelists & Discounts Charge the right price to the right customer automatically. A pricelist is a set of pricing rules — per customer group, currency, quantity or date (e.g. “-10% for wholesalers”, “USD prices”, “bulk discounts”). Assign one to a customer and quotations price themselves. You can also apply a manual discount per order line. 💡 Let pricelists do the work instead of hand-editing prices — it keeps pricing consistent and auditable. 6 From Order to Cash How a confirmed order becomes a delivery and an invoice. Confirming an order can create a delivery in Inventory (if it has storable products) and lets you create an invoice in Accounting. When you can invoice depends on the invoicing policy : Invoicing policy You can invoice… Ordered quantities As soon as the order is confirmed. Delivered quantities Only what has actually been delivered. Click Create Invoice on the order and it hands off to Accounting (see the Accounting & Invoicing handbook). 7 Customers, Pipeline & Reporting Who you sell to, where deals come from, and how it’s going. Customers and contacts are shared across Odoo. Before a quotation, opportunities often live in CRM as leads/opportunities in a pipeline; a won opportunity becomes a quotation. Sales Analysis reporting then shows revenue by product, salesperson, customer and period. ℹ️ The same customer record ties together their quotations, orders, invoices and support tickets — one 360° view. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Quotation A draft offer sent to a customer. Sales Order A confirmed, accepted quotation. Order line One product, quantity and price on an order. Quotation template A reusable starting point for common offers. Optional product A suggested add-on the customer can include. Pricelist Rules that set prices per customer, currency or quantity. Invoicing policy Whether you bill on ordered or delivered quantities. Delivery The Inventory operation that ships an order’s goods. Invoice The bill generated from a sales order. CRM / Opportunity The pipeline of potential deals that precede a quotation. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Sales docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Sales basics (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Sales · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-sh-instances.html", "title": "Installing an Instance on Odoo.sh", "text": "Installing an Instance on Odoo.sh ← Back to Guide Hub Installing an instance on Odoo.sh How to spin up a fresh staging instance on Odoo.sh and wire it to a GitHub submodule (a much. module you want to work on). Access to Odoo.sh Access to the GitHub repository The right Odoo version in mind Light / dark Print / PDF The big idea. Odoo.sh runs the Odoo instance; GitHub holds the module code. To let Odoo.sh pull a private repo, the two must shake hands with a deploy key : Odoo.sh generates a key → you paste it into the GitHub repo → GitHub now trusts Odoo.sh. Then Odoo.sh can add that repo as a submodule and build the instance. 🔑 Remember — the deploy key is the handshake. If the build can't fetch the submodule, it's almost always because the key wasn't added to the GitHub repo (or was added to the wrong repo). And always stay on your staging branch while doing this. much. Consulting · Onboarding · Installing an instance on Odoo.sh const STEPS=[ {title:'Create a new project in the correct version', where:'Odoo.sh', action:'Create a new project on Odoo.sh for the Odoo version you need. Remember there are 3 versions — make sure you pick the right one.', note:'The version you choose here has to match the module/branch you plan to work on.'}, {title:'Add a staging branch', where:'Odoo.sh → left sidebar', action:'On the left side, under Staging , press the + and give it a name — something like staging-shopware .', note:'Name it after what you\\'re working on so it\\'s easy to recognise later.'}, {title:'Register the submodule repository', where:'Odoo.sh → Settings → Submodules', action:'Go to Settings → Submodules . Click Add and paste the SSH link of the GitHub repository you want to work on.', note:'Odoo.sh then generates a public key . Copy that key — you\\'ll paste it into GitHub in the next step.'}, {title:'Authorise Odoo.sh in GitHub (deploy key)', where:'GitHub → repo → Settings → Deploy keys', action:'Open the same GitHub repository → Settings → Deploy keys → Add deploy key . Paste the key from Odoo.sh, and for the name use your Odoo.sh project name.', note:'This is the handshake: GitHub now knows you want Odoo.sh to use this repository.'}, {title:'Go to Branches — stay on staging', where:'Odoo.sh → Branches', action:'Back in Odoo.sh, go to Branches . Don\\'t forget to keep yourself on the staging branch you created.', note:'Doing the next step on the wrong branch is the most common mistake.'}, {title:'Add the submodule & generate', where:'Odoo.sh → Branches → top-right', action:'In the top-right corner you\\'ll see the submodule option. Add the link / the last QA branch (19) , then click Generate .', note:'The build will take some time — let it finish before you start working on the instance.'}, {title:'Connect to the instance', where:'Odoo.sh → your staging branch', action:'Click Connect on your staging branch. Odoo opens in a new browser tab.', note:'This is your running instance — the one the build just created.'}, {title:'Enable debug (developer) mode', where:'Odoo — in the browser URL', action:'In the Odoo URL, add ?debug=1 at the end and reload the page. Example: https://staging-xxx.odoo-host.dev/odoo?debug=1 ', note:'Or just click the debug icon (the little 🐞 “monkey/bug”) in the top bar — it does the same thing.'}, {title:'Update the apps list', where:'Odoo → Apps', action:'Go to Apps (main menu). With debug active, an Update Apps List button appears at the top — click it. Odoo reads the code from the submodules you added and detects the new available modules.', note:'This button is only visible in debug mode . Without this step, the new modules won\\'t show up in the Apps search.'}, {title:'Install the module', where:'Odoo → Apps', action:'Now you can search for and install your module — e.g. Procuros . 🎉', note:'If it doesn\\'t appear, re-run “Update Apps List”, or check on Odoo.sh that the submodule built without errors.'} ]; document.getElementById('rail').innerHTML=STEPS.map((s,i)=>` ${i+1} ${s.title} 📍 ${s.where} ${s.action} ${s.note?` 💡 ${s.note} `:''} `).join('');"}, {"href": "odoo-subscription-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Subscription — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Subscription — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Recurring Revenue Odoo Subscription Recurring sales orders that renew and invoice themselves — the engine behind any “pay every month” product, and the numbers (like MRR) that measure it. Odoo 19 · Subscription Chapters 1 What Subscriptions Are 2 Recurring Products & Plans 3 Creating a Subscription 4 Lifecycle 5 Invoicing & Payment 6 Grow & Keep 7 Automation & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Subscriptions handles anything a customer pays for repeatedly — monthly or yearly. It is a sales order that keeps going: it renews on schedule , invoices automatically , and can even charge a saved card. It also gives you the recurring-revenue metrics (MRR, churn) that subscription businesses live by. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea A subscription is a sales order that repeats . Put a recurring product on an order with a recurrence (e.g. monthly), confirm it, and Odoo generates invoices every period and renews it until someone stops it. Everything else is managing that cycle. 1 What Subscriptions Are Sell once, get paid on a repeating schedule. A subscription bills a customer again and again on a set rhythm — perfect for SaaS, memberships, maintenance plans or retainers. Odoo automates the boring part: creating each period’s invoice, collecting payment, and renewing the contract, while tracking the health of your recurring revenue. 🔁 Subscription A recurring sales order. ⏱️ Recurrence How often it renews/bills (monthly, yearly…). 📈 MRR Monthly Recurring Revenue — the headline metric. 📉 Churn Customers leaving — lost recurring revenue. 2 Recurring Products & Plans The building blocks: a price that repeats, and how often. A recurring product carries a price meant to be charged each period. A recurrence / subscription plan defines the rhythm — Monthly , Quarterly , Yearly — and rules like duration and how it self-renews or self-closes. ℹ️ The same product can be offered on several recurrences (e.g. cheaper per month if billed yearly) via pricing. 3 Creating a Subscription It starts life as a quotation, just like a normal sale. Subscriptions → New 1 🧑 Customer Who is subscribing. 2 🔁 Recurrence Pick the billing rhythm (monthly/yearly). Drives auto-invoicing 3 📦 Recurring products Add the plan lines and quantities. 4 ✅ Confirm Turns the quotation into a running subscription. The clock starts A quotation template for subscriptions preloads the recurrence and terms so sales reps start in one click. 4 The Subscription Lifecycle A subscription has a life of its own after confirmation. 1 In Progress — active and invoicing each period. 2 Renewed — extended for another term (kept the customer). 3 Churned — the customer left; recurring revenue is lost. 4 Closed — ended and no longer billing. ℹ️ Renewal and churn are the two events that move MRR up or down — the whole business is about maximising the first and minimising the second. 5 Recurring Invoicing & Payment The part that runs without you. On each period, Odoo generates the invoice automatically. If the customer saved a payment token (a stored card via a payment provider), Odoo can charge it automatically and mark the invoice paid — true hands-off recurring billing. Failed payments can trigger reminders. 💡 Automatic payment tokens are what turn subscriptions from “remember to invoice” into a self-running revenue stream. 6 Growing & Keeping Subscriptions The levers that move recurring revenue. ⬆️ Upsell Add products or upgrade the plan mid-term — MRR goes up. ⬇️ Downgrade Reduce the plan — MRR goes down but you keep the customer. 🔁 Renewal Extend the term at period end. 📉 Churn A cancellation — the number to keep low. Odoo tracks these events so you can see why MRR changed, not just that it did. 7 Automation & Reporting Set rules once; read the health of the book of business. Subscription plans can auto-renew, auto-close after a set time, or send alerts (e.g. “expiring soon”). Reporting centres on recurring-revenue metrics: 📈 MRR / ARR Monthly / annual recurring revenue. 📊 Retention Share of customers/revenue kept over time. 📉 Churn analysis Where and why revenue is lost. 🔮 Forecast Expected recurring revenue ahead. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Subscription A recurring sales order that renews and invoices on a schedule. Recurrence How often a subscription bills/renews (monthly, yearly…). Subscription plan The rules for a recurrence: duration, renewal, closing. Recurring product A product priced to be charged each period. MRR / ARR Monthly / Annual Recurring Revenue — the headline metrics. Churn Loss of a customer and their recurring revenue. Renewal Extending a subscription for another term. Upsell Increasing a subscription’s value mid-life. Payment token A stored payment method used to auto-charge invoices. Retention The share of recurring revenue/customers kept over time. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 Subscriptions docs 🎓 Free courses 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Subscription basics (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Subscription · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "odoo-website-ecommerce-handbook.html", "title": "Odoo Website & eCommerce — New Joiner Handbook", "text": "Odoo Website & eCommerce — New Joiner Handbook ← Back to Guide Hub New Joiner Handbook · Online Store Odoo Website & eCommerce Build a website with drag-and-drop blocks and turn it into an online shop that sells the very same products already in Odoo. Odoo 19 · Website & eCommerce Chapters 1 What It Is 2 The Website Builder 3 Products Online 4 Shop & Categories 5 Cart & Checkout 6 Accounts & Promotions 7 SEO & Reporting Reference ★ Glossary 👋 Welcome aboard! Odoo Website is a drag-and-drop site builder, and eCommerce makes that site sell. Because the shop uses the same products, stock and customers as the rest of Odoo, an online order flows straight into Sales, Inventory and Accounting — no separate system to reconcile. This underpins our Shopware, Shopify and ChannelEngine work. 🧠 Read this first — the one big idea The shop is just your Odoo products, published to a web page . Build pages by dragging building blocks , tick a product as Published , and it appears in the shop — where a sale becomes a normal Odoo sales order. One database, storefront included. 1 What Website & eCommerce Is A website builder and an online shop, sharing one database with everything else. Website lets you build and edit pages visually — no code. eCommerce adds a storefront, cart and checkout on top, selling the products you already manage in Odoo. Every online order is a real Odoo sales order , so stock and accounting update automatically. 🌐 Website The pages, built from drag-and-drop blocks. 🛒 Shop The product catalogue customers browse. 🧺 Cart & Checkout Where a visitor buys and pays. 👤 Portal A customer’s account and order history. 2 The Website Builder Design pages by dragging blocks — what you see is what visitors get. Click Edit to enter build mode, then drag building blocks (text, images, columns, banners, pricing, forms) onto the page and tweak them in the side panel. Save to keep changes; toggle Published to make a page public or keep it a draft. ✏️ Edit — enter build mode. 🧱 Drag blocks — drop in sections and style them. 💾 Save — store your changes. 🌍 Publish — make it live for visitors. 3 Products Online Getting an existing Odoo product into the shop. Open a product and set it Published to list it in the shop. The product page shows images, description, price and an Add to Cart button. Variants (size, colour) let one product page offer multiple options. ℹ️ Good images and descriptions sell — the product page is your storefront window. 4 Shop & Categories Help customers find things fast. The shop page lists published products. eCommerce categories group them into a browsable menu (e.g. Men / Women / Accessories), and filters and sorting let shoppers narrow by attribute or price. You can arrange and resize product tiles right on the page. 5 Cart & Checkout Turning a full cart into a paid order. 1 🧺 Cart The visitor collects products and reviews quantities. 2 🚚 Delivery method Shipping options and costs are chosen. Ties into Inventory 3 💳 Payment provider Card/online payment (Stripe, PayPal…) collects the money. Marks the order paid 4 ✅ Confirmation A sales order + invoice are created in Odoo. Fully automatic 💡 Test payments in the provider’s test mode first — never go live with real cards untested. 6 Customer Accounts & Promotions Repeat customers and incentives to buy. Shoppers can create an account to see their order history and documents through the customer portal . To drive sales, use discounts, coupon/promo codes and pricelists — the same pricing engine as Sales, applied online. 🎟️ Coupons One-off codes for a discount. 🏷️ Promotions Rules like “buy 2 get 1”, auto-applied. 💷 Pricelists Customer/quantity/currency-based online pricing. 👤 Portal Self-service account and order history. 7 SEO & Reporting Get found on Google, and see what sells. Each page has SEO settings — title, description and keywords — so search engines index it well. Sales made online appear in normal Sales reporting , and eCommerce adds views for online-specific metrics (abandoned carts, top products). ℹ️ Because online orders are just Odoo sales orders, all your usual Sales and Accounting reports already include them — no separate reconciliation. ★ Plain-English glossary The words you'll keep hearing — decoded. Website Odoo’s drag-and-drop site builder. Building block A draggable page section (text, image, banner…). Publish Making a page or product visible to visitors. Shop The catalogue page listing published products. eCommerce category A group used to organise products for browsing. Variant A product option like size or colour on one product page. Cart / Checkout Where a visitor collects items and pays. Payment provider A service (Stripe, PayPal…) that collects online payment. Portal A customer’s self-service account and order history. SEO Settings that help search engines rank your pages. Official Odoo 19 sources: 📖 eCommerce docs 🌐 Website docs 📚 much. Knowledge ▶️ Build an eCommerce site (video) Made for new joiners · Odoo 19 Website & eCommerce · You've got this 💪 ↑ var topBtn=document.getElementById('top'); window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){if(window.scrollY>400){topBtn.classList.add('show')}else{topBtn.classList.remove('show')}}); topBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}); var links=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('aside.sidebar a')); var map={};links.forEach(function(a){map[a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)]=a}); var obs=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){entries.forEach(function(e){if(e.isIntersecting){links.forEach(function(a){a.classList.remove('active')});var a=map[e.target.id];if(a){a.classList.add('active')}}})},{rootMargin:'-20% 0px -70% 0px'}); document.querySelectorAll('section[id]').forEach(function(s){obs.observe(s)});"}, {"href": "payment-providers-paypal.html", "title": "Payment Providers (PayPal) — User Guide", "text": "Payment Providers (PayPal) — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub Payment Providers (PayPal) — User Guide Connect your PayPal Business account to your Odoo shop and offer PayPal, Pay Later, Venmo and card payments — with saved methods and automatic status updates. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? This is the much. PayPal suite — a bundle of Odoo modules built with PayPal. It links your PayPal Business account to your Odoo online shop so customers can pay with the PayPal wallet, Pay Later instalments, Venmo , Google Pay, Apple Pay and credit/debit cards. It also handles merchant setup with a guided wizard, lets shoppers securely save payment methods (\" vaulting \"), and keeps Odoo and PayPal in step automatically using webhooks . Why it exists: Odoo ships with a basic built-in PayPal option, but it is limited to the classic redirect. This suite gives the full modern experience in one place — no-copy-paste onboarding, extra payment methods, saved methods, on-site \"Pay in 4\" banners and express-checkout buttons. What's in the suite PayPal: Payment Integration The core engine — connects Odoo to your PayPal Business account and handles payments, refunds and logging. PayPal: Merchant Configurator The \"Display Options\" that let you turn methods on/off and control where banners buttons appear. PayPal: Vaulting Lets logged-in customers save PayPal / Venmo / card details and manage them from their account. PayPal: Webhook Base Behind-the-scenes plumbing that safely receives and queues PayPal's status updates. PayPal: Website Sale Storefront touches — Pay Later / Venmo banners, express-checkout buttons, shipping. A few words you'll hear Payment provider The configured connection to a payment service (here \"PayPal Business Integration\"). Onboarding wizard The guided popup that logs you into PayPal and fills your Merchant/Client ID automatically. Vaulting Securely saving a customer's payment method for faster repeat purchases. Webhook An automatic message PayPal sends Odoo when something happens (e.g. a payment is captured). Sandbox vs Production Sandbox = safe test mode with fake money; Production = live, real payments. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the plain-language explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears in Odoo Choice / option External / customer side Where to check One-time setting Onboarding aid for the much. PayPal suite ( payment_paypal_suite , Odoo 19). Menus, field labels and the webhook cron are drawn from the module source README. Always test in Sandbox before switching to Production. Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'connect',label:'A · Connect your PayPal account',dir:'One-time setup',scn:'A new merchant links their PayPal Business account so the shop can take payments.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Enter partner credentials',ex:'The Secret URL / keys from much.',mo:'Invoicing > Configuration > Settings > PayPal Partner Credentials',st:[],biz:'A one-time step that lets Odoo talk to PayPal on your behalf. These values are normally provided to you — you do not invent them.',cfg:'Treat these like a password; do not share them.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Open the PayPal provider',ex:'\"PayPal Business Integration\"',mo:'Website (or Invoicing) > Configuration > Payment Providers',st:['Disabled','Test','Enabled'],biz:'This record represents PayPal in your shop — all the settings live here.',cfg:'Turn on developer mode first if you don’t see every option.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Choose environment & country',ex:'Environment: Sandbox',mo:'The Connect wizard (PayPal Payment Panel)',st:['Sandbox','Production'],biz:'Sandbox tests with fake money; switch to Production only when you are ready for real payments. Set the account country too.',cfg:'The wizard warns you if the country does not match your company.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Click \"Connect with PayPal\"',ex:'Log into PayPal in the popup',mo:'Connect button in the wizard',st:[],biz:'PayPal fills your Merchant ID and Client ID back into Odoo automatically — no copy-pasting keys.',cfg:'Use a current Chrome/Edge; the popup can misbehave on older browsers.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Publish the provider',ex:'Make it visible at checkout',mo:'Provider form → Publish / visibility',st:['Unpublished','Published'],biz:'Until published, customers cannot see PayPal at checkout.',cfg:'Run one full Sandbox order end-to-end before publishing to Production.'} ]}, {id:'methods',label:'B · Turn on methods & banners',dir:'You choose what customers see',scn:'The merchant decides which payment options appear and where Pay Later banners show.',steps:[ {m:'m-blue',t:'Enable the payment methods',ex:'Cards · Pay Later · Venmo · Google/Apple Pay',mo:'Provider form → Configuration tab',st:[],biz:'Each toggle adds that option to the customer’s payment page.',cfg:'Some toggles are read-only if unsupported in your country (e.g. Venmo is US-only).'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Enable Vaulting & Express Checkout',ex:'Saved methods + on-page buttons',mo:'Provider form → Configuration tab',st:[],biz:'Vaulting lets customers save methods; Express Checkout shows PayPal buttons earlier in the journey.',cfg:'Only logged-in customers can save methods.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Open Display Options',ex:'Where buttons & banners appear',mo:'Provider form → Display Options tab',st:[],biz:'Controls placement across your site — product page, cart, payment page.',cfg:'Use the \"Preview\" control to see how it will look.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Place the checkout buttons',ex:'Product Page · Cart · Payment',mo:'Display Options → Checkout Buttons',st:[],biz:'Lets customers start an express PayPal payment before the final step.',cfg:'A few well-placed buttons usually convert better than all of them.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Configure Pay Later / Venmo messaging',ex:'\"Pay in 4\" banners + styling',mo:'Display Options → Messaging Placement & Styling',st:[],biz:'Shows promotional instalment banners to encourage purchases.',cfg:'Match the banner colour to your theme so it looks native.'} ]}, {id:'daily',label:'C · Saved methods & refunds',dir:'Day-to-day',scn:'A returning customer reuses a saved card, and later the merchant refunds an order.',steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Customer saves a method',ex:'\"Save your card\" at checkout',mo:'Website payment page (customer side)',st:[],biz:'A logged-in shopper can store their PayPal/Venmo/card securely for next time.',cfg:'Guests can’t save methods — they must be logged in.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Customer manages saved methods',ex:'My Account → Payment Methods',mo:'Website > My Account > Payment Methods',st:[],biz:'Customers can view or delete their saved methods themselves.',cfg:'Deleting here also removes the token on PayPal’s side.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Merchant issues a refund',ex:'Refund button on the transaction',mo:'The order’s Payment Transaction record',st:['Posted','Refunded'],biz:'Sends a full refund through the PayPal API, straight from Odoo.',cfg:'Open the transaction from the order to find the Refund button.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Check webhook queue & logs',ex:'Status stuck? Look here',mo:'Provider form → Queue & Logs stat buttons',st:['Info','Error'],biz:'The \"PayPal: Process Webhook Queue\" cron keeps statuses in sync; Logs show raw API calls for troubleshooting.',cfg:'If a status looks stuck, check the Queue for a failed webhook item.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad|block|disabled/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|active|ok|success|info|enabled|published|posted|refunded|production/.test(s))return'ok';if(/sandbox|test|draft|unpublished|manual/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "procuros-onboarding.html", "title": "Procuros Connector — Onboarding Process Map", "text": "Procuros Connector — Onboarding Process Map ← Back to Guide Hub Procuros Connector — Onboarding Process Map How Odoo exchanges EDI trade documents with partners over the Procuros network — the business flows and the code behind them. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF Why this connector exists Large retailers and grocery chains do not accept PDF orders or emailed invoices — they trade over EDI (Electronic Data Interchange), where every document is a structured message and every partner, product and pack is a standardised GS1 code (partner = GLN, product = GTIN). Procuros is the network that carries those messages between trading partners. This module is the bridge that lets an Odoo company speak that language: it turns Odoo purchase orders, sales orders, deliveries, invoices, credit notes and payments into Procuros EDI documents and back again — automatically, on a schedule, with full logging. It is symmetric , so the same module serves two personas depending on which side of a deal you are on: Purchase-to-Pay (P2P) — you are the buyer. You send Orders to a supplier and receive back Shipping Notices, Invoices and Credit Notes; when you pay, a Remittance Advice goes out. Order-to-Cash (O2C) — you are the supplier. You receive Orders from a customer and send Order Responses, Shipping Notices, Invoices and Credit Notes; you receive Remittance Advice when they pay. Audience for this map: new engineers and support/PM colleagues. Every step below reads at two levels — a plain-English sentence for the business, and the exact Odoo model, method or API path for the engineer. One concrete deal runs through all of it: buyer ACME Co. Ltd. (GLN 1100001016310 , Berlin) ordering from supplier Testsupplier (GLN 1100001016312 ) on order PO9383-R45 . 1. The big picture Odoo sits on one side, the Procuros EDI network (and, behind it, your trading partners) on the other. In between sits this single module, procuros_connector . Everything it does is built from just four kinds of building block, and colour means the block throughout this whole map: the connector cron that drives it, the GS1 mapper that translates codes to Odoo records, the records API calls that do the work, and the logs that make every exchange auditable. Procuros Connector — big picture Odoo on the left connects through the Procuros Connector (connector and cron, GS1 mapper, records and API calls, logs) to the Procuros EDI network and trading partners on the right, with bidirectional arrows. Odoo Your ERP Sales · Purchase Inventory Accounting Procuros Connector Connector cron GS1 mapper · GLN / GTIN / UoM Records API calls Logs acknowledge Procuros EDI network Trading partners buyers · suppliers GS1 / EDI 2. The architecture Under the hood the module is two mirror-image lanes driven by scheduled actions. Inbound , the Auto-Process cron calls GET /v2/transactions , then dispatches each document to a handler by its type string ( process_incoming_order , process_incoming_invoice , …), resolving GS1 codes to Odoo records as it goes. Outbound , each Odoo document serialises itself with a generate_*_payload() method and is POSTed back. A single connector.configuration record plus three ir.cron jobs are the engine; connector.log records every step and the Auto-Mark cron acknowledges handled transactions back to Procuros so they are never re-sent. Procuros Connector — architecture Two lanes. Inbound: Procuros to fetch, dispatch by type, GS1 resolve, into Odoo. Outbound: Odoo to serialize, send, writeback status, to Procuros. A connector and cron engine band and a logging and acknowledge band span both lanes. Procuros EDI network Odoo records Inbound · Procuros → Odoo Fetch GET /v2/transactions Dispatch by type process_incoming_* GS1 resolve partner · product Outbound · Odoo → Procuros Serialize generate_*_payload Send POST /v2/transactions Writeback status → TRANSMITTED connector.configuration · ir.cron one validated connector per company · scheduled actions every 10 / 10 / 20 min connector.log — every step logged info · data issue · response error · Auto-Mark → transactions/bulk/mark-processed 3. The workflows Pick a workflow to walk its numbered steps. Each step's colour is its building block; click a step for the business meaning, the exact model / method / endpoint, the states it moves through, and config notes. The one deal — ACME buying from Testsupplier on PO9383-R45 — runs through every workflow, closing the full procure-to-pay / order-to-cash loop. Connector cron GS1 mapper (GLN · GTIN · UoM) Record / API call Log acknowledge Generated as an onboarding aid for the procuros_connector Odoo module (v19). Identifiers, methods, cron ids and API paths are quoted from the module source; the running example (ACME / Testsupplier / PO9383-R45) is drawn from the module's own mock data. This file is self-contained, works offline, and is untracked by git — move or share it freely. Print or use \"Handout view\" for slides. const WF = [ { id:'setup', label:'0 · Setup & first run', dir:'Odoo ⇄ Procuros · one-time', scn:'You bring up a brand-new connector for ACME Co. Ltd. : enter the Procuros URL token, validate, mirror the GS1 master data (GLN, GTIN, UoM) onto Odoo records, then switch on the three crons.', steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Create the connector',ex:'One connector per company',mo:'connector.configuration',st:['Draft'], biz:'The connector record is the single bridge to Procuros. It stores the API host and token plus every default an incoming order needs — warehouse, payment term, salesperson, sales team. You create exactly one per Odoo company.', cfg:'Menu Procuros Connector → Connectors . Set host_url = https://api.procuros.io , api_token (used as a Bearer token), external_identifier = your own GS1 account name, api_version = v2 . A _company_unique index enforces one connector per company.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Test connection & validate',ex:'Draft → Validated flips it live',mo:'test_connection() · action_validate_connector()',st:['Draft','Validated'], biz:'Test Connection pings Procuros with your token so you catch a bad URL or key immediately. Validate then makes the connector live — only validated connectors are ever picked up by the crons, and its config fields become read-only.', cfg:' test_connection is the television-icon button. Validate sets state=\\'validated\\' . Two required fields: payment_term_id (Sales Settings tab) and payment_journal_id (Incoming Transactions tab — needed for remittance advice, else payments log a data issue).'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Mirror GS1 master data',ex:'ACME GLN 1100001016310 → res.partner.ref',mo:'res.partner.ref · product.product.barcode · uom.uom.unit_type',st:[], biz:'Procuros speaks GS1: every partner is a GLN, every product a GTIN, every unit an EDI code. You copy those identifiers onto the matching Odoo records so inbound and outbound documents resolve to the right partner, product and quantity. This is the single most common source of errors if skipped.', cfg:'Partner GLN → res.partner.ref (the Reference field). Product GTIN → product.product.barcode ; carton/pack GTIN → product.uom.barcode (v19) or product.packaging.barcode (v17/18). UoM: Odoo \"Units\" ↔ partner \"EA\" via uom.uom.unit_type (unique per UoM, enforced by a constraint).'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Set partner flags',ex:'Testsupplier: Require Order Response ✓',mo:'res.partner (procuros_connector_ids, send_invoice, …)',st:[], biz:'Each partner carries switches deciding which documents flow to or from them — whether you expect an order response, auto-send invoices, send shipping notices, and so on. These gate every automated exchange, so a missing flag simply means \"nothing happens\".', cfg:'On the partner\\'s Sales Purchase tab: procuros_connector_ids , require_order_response (auto-confirms sent POs), send_invoice , send_shipping_notice , send_order_response , order_response_comment , send_credit_note_reason .'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Activate the three crons',ex:'Every 10 / 10 / 20 min',mo:'ir.cron (procuros_connector.*)',st:['Inactive','Active'], biz:'Three scheduled actions run the day-to-day: pull inbound documents, mark them processed back on Procuros, and push outbound documents. They ship switched off — you enable them from the connector\\'s Scheduled Actions button.', cfg:' ir_cron_auto_process_incoming_transactions (10 min) → _auto_process_incoming_transactions() ; ir_cron_auto_mark_incoming_transactions (10 min); ir_cron_auto_process_outgoing_transactions (20 min) → cron_send_outgoing_transactions() . All shipped active=False .'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'First run & read logs',ex:'Get Incoming Transactions → Info / Data Issue',mo:'connector.log',st:['Info','Data issue','Response Error'], biz:'You do one manual pull and read the logs. Green Info means it worked; a Data Issue means a GLN or GTIN did not match and needs fixing on the receiving record. The full JSON of every exchange is on the log for debugging.', cfg:'Button Get Incoming Transactions → action_process_incoming_transactions() . Logs at Procuros Connector → Logs ; level ∈ info / data_issue / data_issue_error / response_error / connection_issue . Critical logs email the connector\\'s salesperson.'} ]}, { id:'order', label:'1 · Order handshake (PO → Sales Order)', dir:'Buyer Odoo → Procuros → Supplier Odoo', scn:' ACME raises PO PO9383-R45 to Testsupplier for 20 × GTIN 4300348765432 . It leaves ACME\\'s Odoo as an EDI ORDER and lands in Testsupplier\\'s Odoo as a sales quotation.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Buyer raises & sends the PO',ex:'PO9383-R45 draft → Send Order to Procuros',mo:'purchase.order.action_send_order_to_procuros()',st:['Draft'], biz:'The buyer creates an ordinary RFQ/PO to the Procuros vendor and clicks Send Order to Procuros. The connector validates first, so a half-configured product or partner is caught before anything is sent.', cfg:' check_order_validity : PO must be draft , partner_id must carry this connector in procuros_connector_ids , and every line\\'s product_uom.unit_type must be set.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Serialize to ORDER payload',ex:'name → orderIdentifier, barcode → GS1',mo:'generate_order_payload() → {\"type\":\"ORDER\"}',st:[], biz:'Odoo translates the PO into the Procuros EDI ORDER shape: your own GS1 as buyer, the vendor\\'s GLN as supplier, and each line\\'s GTIN, quantity, unit type and price.', cfg:' name→orderIdentifier , date_planned→requestedDeliveryDate , partner_id.ref→supplier (GS1), external_identifier→buyer , company.partner_id.ref→billTo , warehouse partner → shipTo . Lines: product_id.barcode→identifiers (GS1), product_uom_id.unit_type→unitOfMeasure , discount → modificationGroups .'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'POST to Procuros',ex:'HTTP 201 → TRANSMITTED',mo:'POST /v2/transactions',st:['TRANSMITTED','NO_ACTION'], biz:'The outgoing cron POSTs the order. On success the PO is stamped with its transaction id and status. If the partner does not require an order response, the PO auto-confirms right away.', cfg:' action_send_orders → POST /v2/transactions (expect 201/202). 201 → TRANSMITTED ; 202 → NO_ACTION + procuros_retry_disabled . If not require_order_response → automatic button_confirm() .'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Supplier resolves who & what',ex:'GLN 1100001016310 → ACME · GTIN → product',mo:'process_incoming_order → _get_partner_by_identifier / _get_product_by_gs1_identifier',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'On the supplier\\'s side the inbound cron reads the ORDER and looks up the buyer by GLN and each line by GTIN (falling back to a carton GTIN). Ambiguous or missing matches stop the order and log a data issue rather than guessing.', cfg:'Buyer / shipTo / billTo GS1 → res.partner.ref . GTIN → product.product.barcode , then carton product.uom.barcode (payload qty × carton size). A duplicate GLN/GTIN, or an unmatched shipTo/billTo, is fatal — the order is skipped.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create the sales quotation',ex:'Draft SO for ACME, 20 × line 1',mo:'sale.order.create() · state=draft',st:['Draft'], biz:'A draft sales order is created with ACME as customer, the resolved products and quantities, and the connector\\'s salesperson, team and payment term. It is idempotent — the same Procuros transaction never creates two orders.', cfg:'Seeds partner_id / partner_shipping_id / partner_invoice_id , order_identifier , procuros_transaction_id , and user_id / team_id / payment_term_id from the partner or, as fallback, the connector. Deduplicates on procuros_transaction_id .'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log & acknowledge',ex:'Info \"SO Created\" → mark-processed',mo:'connector.log → transactions/bulk/mark-processed',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'Every outcome is logged, and the Auto-Mark cron tells Procuros the transaction was handled so it moves from In Progress to Delivered and is never re-sent.', cfg:' _auto_action_mark_transaction_processed → POST transactions/bulk/mark-processed . The connector\\'s transaction_to_be_marked chooses \"Only Succeeded\" vs \"All Transactions\".'} ]}, { id:'response', label:'2 · Order response (accept / change / reject)', dir:'Supplier Odoo → Procuros → Buyer Odoo', scn:' Testsupplier answers the imported order for ACME : confirming it unchanged sends ACCEPT ; trimming a line sends CHANGE with the confirmed quantity; cancelling sends REJECT . ACME\\'s PO updates to match.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Supplier acts on the order',ex:'Confirm / modify / cancel the SO',mo:'sale.order.action_confirm() · action_cancel()',st:['Draft','Sale','Cancelled'], biz:'The supplier confirms, edits or cancels the imported quotation exactly as they would any order. The connector quietly remembers the quantities and dates it originally received so it can tell what changed.', cfg:'On import the connector stored procuros_original_qty and procuros_original_commitment_date . _has_order_changes() compares the current line values against those originals.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Determine the response type',ex:'line 19 → 15 ⇒ CHANGE',mo:'_determine_order_response_type()',st:['ACCEPT','CHANGE','REJECT'], biz:'Odoo decides the answer automatically — cancelled → REJECT, confirmed-with-edits → CHANGE, confirmed-untouched → ACCEPT — so the user never has to pick a document type by hand.', cfg:'cancel → REJECT ; state sale + changes → CHANGE ; otherwise ACCEPT . If the partner has order_response_comment set, a wizard prompts for a reason on CHANGE / REJECT.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Send the order response',ex:'CHANGE with confirmedQuantity 15',mo:'generate_order_response_payload() → {\"type\":\"ORDER_RESPONSE\"}',st:['TRANSMITTED'], biz:'The response is serialised and posted. For a CHANGE it carries the confirmed quantities and delivery dates line by line, so the buyer sees exactly what will actually be delivered.', cfg:'Button Send Order Response to Procuros (needs a transaction id + send_order_response + a confirmed/cancelled state + no prior response). CHANGE items map orderedQuantity=procuros_original_qty , confirmedQuantity=product_uom_qty . Sent via action_send_order_responses .'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Buyer matches PO & lines',ex:'orderIdentifier PO9383-R45 → draft PO',mo:'process_incoming_order_response',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'Back on the buyer\\'s side, the inbound cron finds the original draft PO by its identifier and routes the message to the right handler for accept, change or reject.', cfg:'PO found by orderIdentifier → purchase.order.name . Dispatches order_response_handle_{accept|change|reject} . For CHANGE, each line is matched by product barcode + ordered qty + unit_type.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Apply to the purchase order',ex:'ACCEPT → confirm · CHANGE → qty 15 · REJECT → cancel',mo:'button_confirm() / button_cancel() / write(product_qty)',st:['Sale','Cancelled'], biz:'The buyer\\'s PO is updated to reflect the supplier\\'s answer — confirmed, quantity-adjusted, or cancelled — and a chatter note records what happened.', cfg:' order_response_handle_accept → button_confirm() ; _change writes product_qty=confirmedQuantity then confirms; _reject → button_cancel() . Payment terms validated via compare_payment_term_payload .'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log & acknowledge',ex:'Info + chatter note on the PO',mo:'connector.log · mark-processed',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'The outcome and any payment-term mismatch are logged, and the transaction is marked processed on Procuros.', cfg:'A note is posted to the PO chatter; a term mismatch raises a data issue. Acknowledged via the Auto-Mark cron.'} ]}, { id:'ship', label:'3 · Shipping notice (despatch → receipt)', dir:'Supplier Odoo → Procuros → Buyer Odoo', scn:' Testsupplier ships against the order for ACME : 20 of line 1 in full, but only 15 of 19 for line 2 (the other 4 discarded). Despatch SN9383-R45 , with carrier and tracking, becomes a goods receipt in ACME\\'s warehouse.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Validate the delivery',ex:'Picking done · 20 + 15 shipped',mo:'stock.picking.action_send_picking_to_procuros()',st:['Done'], biz:'The supplier picks and validates the outgoing delivery, then clicks Send Picking to Procuros. Only a done picking for a send-shipping-notice partner is allowed.', cfg:' check_picking_validity : state done + partner.send_shipping_notice . Shipping 15 of 19 leaves a backorder open for the 4 short units.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Build transport units & batches',ex:'SSCC package · CARTON / EURO_PALLET · lot + expiry',mo:'get_transport_unit_list_payload() · get_stock_move_lots()',st:[], biz:'The physical shipment is described the way EDI needs it: pallets and cartons with their SSCC ids and unit types, and — for tracked goods — the batch numbers and expiry dates inside each.', cfg:' result_package_id.name→unitIdentifier , package_type_id.unit_type→unitType ( EURO_PALLET / CARTON ). Lots: lot_id.name→batchIdentifier , expiration_date→expirationDate (why product_expiry is a dependency).'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Send the shipping notice',ex:'SN9383-R45 · tracking + openQuantityAction',mo:'generate_picking_payload() → {\"type\":\"SHIPPING_NOTICE\"}',st:['TRANSMITTED'], biz:'The despatch advice is posted with carrier and tracking and, where less was shipped than ordered, whether the remainder follows later or is dropped.', cfg:' name→shippingNoticeIdentifier , carrier_id.name→carrier , carrier_tracking_ref→trackingNumber . A short line adds openQuantityAction = DELIVERED_LATER (backorder exists) else DISCARDED .'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Buyer matches PO & products',ex:'orderIdentifier → PO · GTIN → product',mo:'process_incoming_shipping_notice',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'On the buyer\\'s side the notice is matched to the original purchase order and each shipped line to its product, converting carton GTINs to base units.', cfg:'PO found by orderIdentifier . Copies the draft receipt picking, splits shippedQuantity per transport-unit item, matching by product or carton barcode.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create the receipt',ex:'Receipt in ACME warehouse · 20 + 15',mo:'stock.picking (incoming, carrier stamped)',st:['Processed'], biz:'A goods receipt appears in the buyer\\'s warehouse, linked to the PO and pre-filled with the shipped quantities, carrier and tracking — ready to receive.', cfg:' shippedQuantity→stock.move.product_uom_qty ; carrier_id / carrier_tracking_ref from the header. procuros_inc_transaction_status=\\'Processed\\' .'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log & acknowledge',ex:'Info → Delivered on Procuros',mo:'connector.log · mark-processed',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'Success is logged and acknowledged; the Procuros shipping-notice transaction moves to Delivered.', cfg:'Acknowledged via the Auto-Mark cron.'} ]}, { id:'invoice', label:'4 · Invoice → vendor bill', dir:'Supplier Odoo → Procuros → Buyer Odoo', scn:' Testsupplier invoices ACME as IV9383-R45 — 20 × line 1 plus 15 × line 2 at 19% VAT, on 28-day / 3% skonto terms. It arrives as a vendor bill on ACME\\'s purchase order.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Post & send the invoice',ex:'IV9383-R45 posted → Send Invoice',mo:'account.move.action_send_invoice_to_procuros()',st:['Posted'], biz:'The supplier creates the customer invoice from the sales order, posts it, and sends it. The connector requires a posted invoice for a send-invoice partner, backed by a completed delivery.', cfg:' check_invoice_validity : posted + partner.send_invoice . The header needs a source SO ( invoice_origin ) and a done delivery, else a \"not delivered\" ValidationError.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Serialize INVOICE + taxes + terms',ex:'barcode → GS1 · 19% tax · 28d/3% terms',mo:'generate_invoice_payload(_type=\"inv\")',st:[], biz:'The invoice is translated to EDI: line GTINs, gross unit prices, per-line tax, discounts as ALLOWANCE blocks, and full totals — plus the payment terms with any early-payment (skonto) discount.', cfg:'Gross unitPrice (discount only via modificationGroups ALLOWANCE/DISCOUNT), per-line tax , summary.grossAmount/netAmount/dueAmount , paymentTerms from the SO term. orderLineNumber links each item back to the order line.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Send the invoice',ex:'HTTP 201 → TRANSMITTED',mo:'POST /v2/transactions · {\"type\":\"INVOICE\"}',st:['TRANSMITTED','NO_ACTION'], biz:'The invoice is posted to Procuros and stamped TRANSMITTED. Credit notes reuse this exact path.', cfg:'Via action_send_invoices (credit notes: same method with _type=\"cn\" ).'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Buyer matches PO, lines & tax',ex:'orderIdentifier → PO · barcode → line · tax check',mo:'process_incoming_invoice → parse_inv_to_vbill',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'On the buyer\\'s side the invoice is matched to its purchase order, each line to a PO line by GTIN, and taxes validated against the buyer\\'s own product configuration.', cfg:' orderIdentifier→purchase.order , supplier GS1 → partner_id , line product by barcode then carton; purchase_line_id.taxes_id applied; a tax-% mismatch is logged.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create the vendor bill',ex:'Draft vendor bill on ACME\\'s PO',mo:'account.move · move_type=\"in_invoice\"',st:['Processed'], biz:'A vendor bill is created in the buyer\\'s accounting, linked to the purchase order and ready to check and pay.', cfg:' move_type=\\'in_invoice\\' , purchase_id set, procuros_inc_transaction_status=\\'Processed\\' . With no matching PO the bill is still created, unlinked, plus a data issue.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log & acknowledge',ex:'Info → Delivered',mo:'connector.log · mark-processed',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'Logged and acknowledged back to Procuros.', cfg:'Acknowledged via the Auto-Mark cron.'} ]}, { id:'creditnote', label:'5 · Credit note', dir:'Supplier Odoo → Procuros → Buyer Odoo', scn:' Testsupplier issues a credit note against IV9383-R45 — a CORRECTION for a wrong quantity, or a VALUE_CREDIT for a rebate. ACME receives a vendor credit note that reverses the original bill.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Reverse invoice → credit note',ex:'Credit Note wizard · pick the reason',mo:'account.move.reversal (cn_type)',st:['CORRECTION','VALUE_CREDIT'], biz:'The supplier reverses the posted invoice. The reversal type sets the EDI credit-note type: a full/partial correction of a wrong invoice, or a standalone commercial credit like a rebate.', cfg:' refund_moves→cn_type=\\'VALUE_CREDIT\\' , modify_moves→cn_type=\\'CORRECTION\\' . cn_type and procuros_connector_id propagate onto the reversal move.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Serialize CREDIT_NOTE',ex:'creditNoteIdentifier + reason + orig invoice',mo:'generate_invoice_payload(_type=\"cn\")',st:[], biz:'The same invoice serializer, credit-note flavour: it references the original invoice and carries the type and the reason text.', cfg:' type=\\'CREDIT_NOTE\\' , reversed_entry_id.name→invoiceIdentifier , cn_type→type , ref→reason (format \"Reversal of: …, Reason\"). Items carry isInvoicedItem: true .'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Send the credit note',ex:'201 → TRANSMITTED',mo:'POST /v2/transactions · {\"type\":\"CREDIT_NOTE\"}',st:['TRANSMITTED'], biz:'Posted to Procuros and stamped.', cfg:'Via action_send_invoices(_type=\"cn\") . Needs partner.send_invoice (and optionally send_credit_note_reason ).'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Buyer resolves the original bill',ex:'invoiceIdentifier → original vendor bill',mo:'process_incoming_credit_note → parse_vendorbill_creditnote',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'The buyer finds the original vendor bill that the credit note refers back to.', cfg:'Original bill searched by invoiceIdentifier ; the reversal is built through the account.move.reversal wizard.'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create vendor credit note',ex:'Draft vendor credit note · bill reversed',mo:'_revserse_vendor_bill() · in_refund',st:['Processed'], biz:'A vendor credit note is created as draft and, for corrections, offsets the original bill.', cfg:'Created move status Processed . CORRECTION links to and reverses the original bill; VALUE_CREDIT stands alone.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Log & acknowledge',ex:'Info → Delivered',mo:'connector.log · mark-processed',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'Logged and acknowledged.', cfg:'Acknowledged via the Auto-Mark cron.'} ]}, { id:'remittance', label:'6 · Remittance advice (payment)', dir:'Buyer Odoo → Procuros → Supplier Odoo', scn:' ACME pays vendor bill IV9383-R45 (less the 3% skonto). Procuros carries the remittance to Testsupplier , where it becomes a customer payment reconciled against the invoice — closing the loop.', steps:[ {m:'m-gray',t:'Buyer registers the payment',ex:'Register Payment on IV9383-R45',mo:'account.payment (register)',st:['Posted'], biz:'The buyer pays the vendor bill in Odoo as normal. That payment becomes the remittance advice Procuros forwards to the supplier — no separate document to raise.', cfg:'The outgoing remittance is transmitted on the next outgoing cron run. Any skonto / early-payment discount is reflected in the amounts.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Supplier resolves partner & currency',ex:'buyer GLN → ACME · EUR',mo:'process_incoming_remittance_advice → _remittance_resolve_partner',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'On the supplier\\'s side the inbound cron identifies who paid — by GLN, falling back to name — and in which currency.', cfg:'Partner from items[0].buyer / header.buyer / header.payFrom GS1 → res.partner.ref . Requires payment_journal_id on the connector, else a data issue and no payment.'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Match the invoices',ex:'documentNumber IV9383-R45 → out_invoice',mo:'_remittance_resolve_invoices()',st:['Info','Data issue'], biz:'Each referenced document is matched to the supplier\\'s open invoices (or credit notes) so the payment can be reconciled against them.', cfg:' documentNumber / externalDocumentNumber → account.move.name/ref , posted + not fully paid + same company. documentType=\\'CREDIT_NOTE\\' → out_refund .'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Create & reconcile the payment',ex:'Customer payment · amount = remittedTotal',mo:'account.payment (_remittance_create_payment)',st:['Processed'], biz:'A customer payment is created and reconciled against the matched invoices. Several invoices in one remittance become a single grouped payment; no match means a standalone inbound payment for the total.', cfg:'Matched → account.payment.register ( group_payment when 1) + _create_payments() . Amount from summary.remittedTotal . Negative amounts are rejected.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Chatter breakdown & log',ex:'Invoice refs, amounts, skonto table',mo:'connector.log + _remittance_build_chatter_html',st:['Info','Processed'], biz:'The payment\\'s chatter shows a full breakdown — which invoices, what amounts, any skonto/correction — and the outcome is logged and acknowledged.', cfg:'An HTML chatter table of documents / corrections / summary is posted. Acknowledged via the Auto-Mark cron.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|critical|down|archiv|cancel|retour|unchanged|reject/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|active|linked|info|shipped|ship|changed|checked|success|transmit|processed|deliver|posted|validated|sale|accept/.test(s)&&!/not checked/.test(s))return'ok';if(/progress|retry|partial|warning|manual|inactive|draft|open|not checked|no_action|change/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st&&st.length?' States '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Under the hood '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' · '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "procuros-testing.html", "title": "Procuros — Testing Setup", "text": "Procuros — Testing Setup ← Back to Guide Hub Procuros — testing setup How to set up two companies and test the full Procuros order flow (Buyer ↔ Seller) end to end. Light / dark Print / PDF Before you start. Installing the module is the same as the Odoo.sh instance install — see the Install an Odoo.sh instance guide. Do everything below after you have logged into the testing Odoo.sh environment . Credentials come from 1Password (Procuros Buyer / Seller / Supplier Credentials). 💡 Remember — each company needs a Warehouse configured, and an Accounting setup so you can have VAT . Without them the order flow won't complete. 📖 Full instructions (KB). All the steps above are documented in the Knowledge base: erp.muchconsulting.de/odoo/knowledge/2929/knowledge/2957 much. Consulting · Onboarding · Procuros — testing setup (Buyer ↔ Seller flow) const STEPS=[ {title:'Create the two companies', where:'Settings → Companies', action:'You need two companies to play both sides of the trade.', groups:[{items:[ 'Go to Settings → Companies → New ', 'Create Empresa-Buyer and save', 'Repeat and create Empresa-Seller ' ]}], note:'One company buys, the other sells — the whole test runs between them.'}, {title:'Install the Procuros module', where:'Apps', action:'Install the connector app.', groups:[{items:[ 'Go to Apps → search Procuros ', 'Install the Procuros Connector module' ]}], note:'The dependencies install automatically. (Installing the module itself is the same as in the Odoo.sh instance guide.)'}, {title:'Create the Buyer company\\'s connector', where:'Procuros Connector → Connectors', action:'Go to Procuros Connector → Connectors → New and fill it in with the data from 1Password → Procuros Buyer Credentials :', groups:[ {label:'Fill the form:',items:[ ' Procuros Account Name → the 1Password username ', ' URL → the 1Password url field (no trailing slash)', ' API Token → the 1Password credential field', ' Company → Empresa-Buyer' ]}, {label:'Then:',items:[ 'In the Sales Settings tab, set Payment Terms and Warehouse ', 'Click Test Connection → Validate ' ]} ], note:'Do exactly the same for Empresa-Seller (using the Seller credentials from 1Password).'}, {title:'Configure Units of Measure', where:'Settings → Units of Measures', action:'Map Odoo\\'s unit to the Procuros unit type.', groups:[{items:[ 'Go to Settings → Units of Measures and enable Units & Packaging ', 'Open Units & Packagings ', 'Select the Unit → add EA as the Procuros Unit Type ' ]}]}, {title:'Link the partners to Procuros', where:'Contacts + Procuros Connector', action:'Connect each company\\'s contact record to its Procuros account.', groups:[{label:'In Empresa-Buyer — the Empresa-Seller contact:',items:[ 'Go to Contacts → open Empresa-Seller ', 'In the Reference field, put the 1Password username (Procuros Supplier Credentials)', 'In the Procuros connector, set the Seller and Buyer company' ]}], note:'Do the same on the other company.'}, {title:'Configure products with a barcode', where:'Sales → Products', action:'Procuros matches products by barcode, so both sides must share the same one.', groups:[{items:[ 'Open a product → Barcode field → enter an EAN-13 (e.g. 1234567890128 )', 'Make sure the same barcode exists on the product in both companies' ]}]}, {title:'Test the flow — Purchase Order', where:'Purchase (Buyer) → Sales (Seller)', action:'Send a PO from the Buyer and receive it as a Quotation on the Seller.', groups:[ {label:'In Empresa-Buyer:',items:[ 'Go to Purchase → New RFQ ', 'Vendor = Empresa-Seller ; add a product that has a barcode', 'Confirm → click Send Order to Procuros ', 'Check the Procuros Data tab → status should be TRANSMITTED ' ]}, {label:'In Empresa-Seller:',items:[ 'Go to Procuros Connector → open the connector → Get Incoming Transactions ', 'Check that a new Quotation appears in Sales → status DELIVERED ' ]} ], note:'If it works, the round-trip is complete — the Buyer\\'s PO became the Seller\\'s Quotation over Procuros. 🎉'} ]; document.getElementById('rail').innerHTML=STEPS.map((s,i)=>` ${i+1} ${s.title} 📍 ${s.where} ${s.action} ${(s.groups||[]).map(g=>` ${g.label?` ${g.label} `:''} ${g.items.map(it=>` ${it} `).join('')} `).join('')} ${s.note?` 💡 ${s.note} `:''} `).join('');"}, {"href": "release-process-onboarding.html", "title": "much. release process — onboarding", "text": "much. release process — onboarding much. release process — onboarding What a release is, when they happen, how it ships, and how we tell the world — for Product Owners Support. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. The big picture A release is a tagged version of a much. module (e.g. v18.0.1.2.0 ) bundling everything merged and QA-tested since the last version, then shipped to customers. It doesn't run on a fixed calendar — it's cut when enough fixes and features have piled up on the QA branch, or for an urgent fix. The dashed block is the customer-email step that doesn't exist yet — a future project. What a release touches Merged QA code flows through the release process out to customers and partners. Merged QA code 18.latest-qa Customers partners · support the release process Trigger /release Version branch Release notes ×2 GitHub release CI build deploy Support docs Email announce 2. The four phases The process runs left to right through the three phases you care about — the technical prep, publishing, and announcing — plus the future customer-email step (dashed, not built yet). The release in four phases Technical prep, publish and CI, announce, and a future email step. Technical prep bump · merge Publish CI GitHub · Jenkins Announce notes · support Future: email mail marketing 3. The flows (interactive) Pick a view: what a release is and when they happen, the technical flow, the business/announcement flow, and the coming-soon customer email. Click any step for the business meaning and the technical detail. The real v18.0.1.2.0 release is the running example. (Use \"Handout view\" above to expand every view for printing.) Cadence trigger Git versioning Release notes CI / deploy Announce (external) Support docs Future / to-do Built for PO support onboarding. Grounded in the /release workflow, the real release notes for v18.0.1.2.0, and the much product repos. Buttons: theme toggle, handout view (expands every view), print / save-as-PDF. The \"Coming soon — customer email\" view is a proposed project, not yet built. const WF=[ {id:'basics',label:'1 · What & when',dir:'Orientation',scn:'A release is a tagged version of a much. module — e.g. v18.0.1.2.0 — bundling the fixes and features merged since the last version.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'What is a release',ex:'A tagged, versioned bundle of changes',biz:'A release packages everything merged and tested since the last version into one named version that customers can install. Nothing reaches a customer until it is released.',mo:'git tag v18.0.1.2.0',st:[],cfg:'One release = one version tag + two release-note files + a GitHub release'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Version scheme',ex:'18.0.1.2.0 = Odoo 18 · much 1.2.0',biz:'The first part (18.0) is the Odoo version the module targets; the rest (1.2.0) is much&#39;s own version that steps up every release.',mo:'__manifest__.py \"version\"',st:[],cfg:'Branches: 18.latest-qa (staging) 18.latest (stable) a version branch 18.0.1.2.0 '}, {m:'m-purple',t:'When do they happen',ex:'When QA-approved changes have piled up',biz:'No fixed calendar. A release is cut when meaningful fixes and features have accumulated on the QA branch and passed testing; an urgent fix can trigger an out-of-band release.',mo:'commits on {major}.latest-qa since last tag',st:[],cfg:'The tool starts by listing every commit since the last v{major}* tag — no commits, no release'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Who is involved',ex:'Engineer runs it; PO & support need to know',biz:'A developer executes the release. The Product Owner validates what is in scope, and Support must know what changed to answer customers — which is exactly why this map exists.',mo:'product@muchconsulting.de',st:[],cfg:'PO owns the \"what shipped & why\"; support owns \"how do we explain it\"'} ]}, {id:'tech',label:'2 · Technical flow',dir:'Engineering',scn:'Cutting v18.0.1.2.0 : bump versions, merge QA stable, branch, tag, then let CI build and deploy.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Trigger /release',ex:'Run on the QA branch (18.latest-qa)',biz:'The engineer runs the release workflow. It works out the major version from the branch, finds the last tag, and shows every commit since then so scope is clear.',mo:'/release [major]',st:['on 18.latest-qa'],cfg:' git log LAST_TAG..HEAD --oneline · stops if there are no new commits'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Pick version & bump manifests',ex:'18.0.1.2.0 written to every __manifest__.py',biz:'The team decides the new number, and the tool stamps it into every module&#39;s manifest so all modules move to the same version together.',mo:'__manifest__.py \"version\"',st:['confirmed'],cfg:'Commit chore[*] update manifest version , signed-off'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Merge, branch & push',ex:'18.latest-qa 18.latest branch 18.0.1.2.0',biz:'The QA branch is merged into the stable branch, then a branch named after the exact version is created. Every push asks for confirmation first.',mo:'git merge · git branch 18.0.1.2.0',st:['pushed'],cfg:'QA = {major}.latest-qa · stable = {major}.latest '}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Generate release notes (×2)',ex:'A technical file and a customer file',biz:'Two files are written from the real code diffs: a technical changelog for engineers and partners, and a plain-language version for customers.',mo:'RELEASE_NOTES_v*.md · USER_RELEASE_NOTES_v*.md',st:['Breaking','Features','Fixes'],cfg:'Commits sorted: fix Fixes, feat Features, break/BREAKING Breaking · contributors via gh api '}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Publish GitHub release',ex:'Tag v18.0.1.2.0 + changelog compare link',biz:'A GitHub release is published with the tag and a link comparing the previous version to this one, crediting all contributors.',mo:'gh release',st:['tagged','published'],cfg:'Changelog URL compare/v18.0.1.1.6...v18.0.1.2.0 '}, {m:'m-gray',t:'CI builds & deploys',ex:'Jenkins picks up the pushed branch/tag',biz:'The CI pipeline builds the modules and makes the new version available to install on customer Odoo instances.',mo:'Jenkinsfile · jenkinsfilePy()',st:['build','deploy'],cfg:'Shared jenkins-templates library'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Install / upgrade instances',ex:'\"Standard module upgrade applies\"',biz:'Customer and hosting instances upgrade the modules to the new version — most releases need no special steps.',mo:'Odoo module upgrade',st:['upgraded'],cfg:'Release notes flag any new dependencies or special upgrade steps'} ]}, {id:'biz',label:'3 · Business flow — telling the world',dir:'Communication',scn:'Once v18.0.1.2.0 is out, the people who need to know are told — today this is mostly pull-based.',steps:[ {m:'m-teal',t:'User release notes ready',ex:'\"Cleaner B2C customer records …\"',biz:'The customer-facing notes summarise what changed in everyday language — new features, improvements, bug fixes — with no developer jargon.',mo:'USER_RELEASE_NOTES_v18.0.1.2.0.md',st:['Features','Improvements','Fixes'],cfg:'Ends with a feedback contact: product@muchconsulting.de '}, {m:'m-amber',t:'GitHub release / changelog',ex:'Public release page + full compare link',biz:'Partners and technical customers can see exactly what shipped through the GitHub release and its changelog.',mo:'github.com/much-GmbH/…/releases',st:['published'],cfg:'Technical notes route support/partners to product@erp.muchconsulting.de '}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Update product documentation',ex:'Refresh the Knowledge article for the module',biz:'The Odoo Knowledge docs are updated so the help centre matches the new behaviour — the same docs support uses every day.',mo:'knowledge.article (muchdoo)',st:['updated'],cfg:'e.g. the iHub / connector user-doc articles'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Enable support',ex:'Support reads the notes, ready for tickets',biz:'Support specialists review the user notes so they can answer questions and set expectations about what changed for customers.',mo:'product@muchconsulting.de',st:['briefed'],cfg:'This is the key PO support handover moment'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Customer finds out — today',ex:'Passive: they read notes or ask support',biz:'Right now customers mostly learn about a release by reading the notes, spotting the changelog, or contacting support. There is no proactive push — which the next view fixes.',mo:'manual / on-request',st:['pull-based'],cfg:'This is the gap the To-Do project closes'} ]}, {id:'todo',label:'4 · Coming soon — customer email (To-Do)',dir:'Future project',scn:' Not built yet. Proactively email customers when a product they use releases, via Odoo Email Marketing.',steps:[ {m:'m-coral',t:'The gap today',ex:'No proactive announcement to customers',biz:'Customers are not actively told when a product they use gets a new release — they have to go looking. The goal is to move from pull to push.',mo:'future project',st:['To do'],cfg:'Turns a release into an outbound moment, not just a git tag'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Dynamic customer list',ex:'Auto-built list of product subscribers',biz:'Use Odoo Email Marketing to define a mailing list whose members are computed from a filter — e.g. every customer with an active subscription to the released product — so it stays current with no manual upkeep.',mo:'mailing.list · mailing.contact',st:['To do'],cfg:'Dynamic segment = a domain filter on subscribers'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Announcement mailing',ex:'Branded email built from the user notes',biz:'Compose a release-announcement email whose body is the customer-facing notes, targeted at that dynamic list.',mo:'mailing.mailing',st:['To do'],cfg:'Reuse the USER_RELEASE_NOTES content as the email body'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Trigger on release',ex:'Create/queue the mailing when published',biz:'Ideally the mailing is generated as part of the release itself, so announcing becomes automatic rather than a manual afterthought.',mo:'new final step in /release',st:['To do'],cfg:'Hook after step 10 (user notes) in the release workflow'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Measure & iterate',ex:'Opens/clicks show what customers value',biz:'Email Marketing&#39;s built-in stats reveal which releases and features resonate, feeding back into the roadmap.',mo:'Email Marketing statistics',st:['To do'],cfg:'Closes the loop between shipping and customer awareness'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|down|to do|pull-based|breaking|cancel/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|published|tagged|upgraded|updated|briefed|deploy|features|confirmed|pushed/.test(s))return'ok';if(/build|manual|improvements|fixes|on 18/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st&&st.length?' States / tags '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Under the hood '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' · '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+' Good to know '+s.cfg+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "shopware-Onboarding.html", "title": "Shopware ↔ Odoo — User Guide", "text": "Shopware ↔ Odoo — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub Shopware &#x2194; Odoo User Guide A plain-language guide for new team members. No technical background needed start with the basics, then click through what happens, what you need to do, and where to find things. Light / dark Print / PDF 1. What is this integration? Shopware is your online shop where customers browse and buy. Odoo is the ERP system that runs the business behind it orders, stock, invoices and deliveries. On their own, the two don't talk to each other. The integration (or \"connector\") is a little messenger that sits between them and keeps both sides in step, on its own. So when a customer buys something, or you change a price, the messenger carries it across nobody has to copy it by hand. What the integration connects Odoo and Shopware exchange orders, products, stock, shipments and returns through the integration connector. Odoo your ERP system Shopware your online shop Integration connector &#x2190; Order Import &#x2194; Product Sync &#x2192; Stock Export &#x2192; Shipment Export &#x2192; Return Export Taxes Currencies What the connector does 👆 Click any coloured block inside the connector above to see what it does. (function(){ const D={ order:['m-teal','&#x2190; Order Import','Orders placed in your Shopware shop are pulled into Odoo automatically (once a day) and become normal Sales Orders — ready to pick, pack and invoice. Direction: Shopware → Odoo. '], product:['m-blue','&#x2194; Product Sync','Products can travel both ways: pull shop products into Odoo, or push Odoo products up to Shopware. Names, prices, images, variants, category and tax are kept in step. Direction: both ways. '], stock:['m-coral','&#x2192; Stock Export','Odoo sends the real available stock to Shopware every day, so the shop shows correct availability and you avoid overselling. Only the numbers that changed are sent. Direction: Odoo → Shopware. '], shipment:['m-amber','&#x2192; Shipment Export','When your warehouse validates a delivery in Odoo, Shopware is marked “shipped” and the tracking number is sent so the customer can follow their parcel. Direction: Odoo → Shopware. '], 'return':['m-pink','&#x2192; Return Export','When you process a customer return in Odoo, the matching Shopware order is updated to “returned”. (Any refund or credit note is a separate step in Odoo Invoicing.) Direction: Odoo → Shopware. '], taxes:['m-gray','Taxes Currencies','A one-time setup step: Odoo matches Shopware’s tax rates and currencies to its own, using the Import Taxes and Map Currencies buttons. Do this once, right after enabling the integration.'] }; const box=document.getElementById('swDef'),t=document.getElementById('swDefT'),p=document.getElementById('swDefP'); window.swShow=function(k){const d=D[k];if(!d)return;box.className='sw-def '+d[0];t.innerHTML=d[1];p.innerHTML=d[2];}; })(); A few words you'll hear Import Bringing information into Odoo (for example, an order from the shop). Export Sending information out to Shopware (for example, a new stock number). Sync Keeping both sides showing the same thing. Scheduled action A task that runs automatically on a timer (usually once a day), so you don't have to start it. Log A diary the system keeps, so you can see what happened and why your first place to look if something seems off. Integration Hub The menu in Odoo where all of this lives ( Integration Hub Shopware ). 2. What happens automatically vs what you do Most of the integration runs on its own. You only need to act in a few specific situations. Automatic actions versus manual actions Left column shows what happens automatically daily. Right column shows what you need to do yourself. Happens automatically &#x2190; Import orders from Shopware Every day nothing to do &#x2190; Import new products from Shopware Every day nothing to do &#x2192; Send stock levels to Shopware Every day nothing to do &#x2192; Send shipment return updates When you validate a delivery in Odoo You do this when needed &#x2192; Export a product to Shopware Sales > Products > action menu &#x2190; Import one specific order now Integration Hub > Shopware > Import &#x21BA; Process a return Inventory > Deliveries > Return &#x1F50D; Check logs if something looks wrong 3. Step by step Click any step to see a plain-language explanation, where to find it in Odoo, and a practical tip. Use the drop-down to switch between tasks. Step you need to take What appears in Odoo Choice or option Happens automatically External system Where to check One-time setting Testing For testing purposes , please follow this Knowledge base article: https://erp.muchconsulting.de/odoo/knowledge/2591/knowledge/1541 Based on the Shopware Odoo integration User Documentation (Knowledge article 1541) much. Consulting. Beginner glossary and plain-language intro added for new team members. Use the buttons above to switch theme or print a handout (printing shows every task at once). const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'0 · First-time setup',dir:'Done once with IT',scn:'Connect Odoo to your Shopware store for the first time. This is done once and does not need to be repeated unless you add a new company or a new Shopware store.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'IT installs the modules',ex:'Nothing to do on your end',biz:'Your IT team or Odoo administrator installs the integration modules in the background. You do not need to do anything at this step. Once installed, the integration menu appears in Odoo under Integration Hub.',mo:'Odoo > Apps',st:[],cfg:'This only needs to happen once per Odoo environment'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Create the connection record',ex:'Integration Hub > Integrations > New',biz:'In Odoo, go to Integration Hub and click New. Give the integration a name (for example \"Shopware Production\"), select Shopware as the type, and choose the Odoo company it applies to. If you have two companies or two Shopware stores, you create one integration for each.',mo:'Integration Hub > Integrations > New',st:[],cfg:'One connection per company and one per Shopware store'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Enter your Shopware credentials',ex:'Shopware Admin > Settings > System > Integrations',biz:'In your Shopware admin panel, go to Settings > System > Integrations and create a new integration with Administrator access. Copy the two keys it gives you (Access key ID and Secret access key). Back in Odoo, go to Integration Settings and paste them into the URL, API key and API Password fields.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Integration Settings',st:[],cfg:'Treat these keys like a password — do not share them'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Enable the integration',ex:'Click Enable Integration',biz:'Once the credentials are saved, click the Enable Integration button at the top of the page. Odoo will verify that it can reach Shopware. If it works, the status changes to Active and the automatic daily syncs will start.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware',st:['Inactive','Active'],cfg:'If it fails, double-check the Shopware URL and that the keys were copied correctly'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Import taxes and map currencies',ex:'Two buttons at the top of the page',biz:'Before doing anything else, click two buttons on the integration page: Import Taxes (Odoo fetches all tax rates from Shopware automatically) and Map Currencies (Odoo links Shopware currencies to Odoo currencies). These only need to be clicked once.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware (header buttons)',st:[],cfg:'Required before any products are synced or orders imported — do this right after enabling'} ]}, {id:'se',label:'1 · Stock is kept in sync',dir:'Odoo to Shopware — automatic daily',scn:'Orders are shipped and stock decreases in Odoo. Every day, Shopware is automatically updated to show the correct available quantities.',steps:[ {m:'m-coral',t:'Stock is sent to Shopware every day',ex:'Automatic — nothing to do',biz:'Every day, Odoo sends the current available stock for all synced products to Shopware. This means your online shop always shows accurate stock levels, updated overnight. You do not need to trigger this manually.',mo:'Automatic (Integration Hub > Shopware > Scheduled Actions)',st:['Automatic'],cfg:'To change how often this runs, go to Integration Hub > Shopware > Scheduled Actions'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Which products are included',ex:'Only products already synced between both systems',biz:'Only products that exist in both Odoo and Shopware are included in the stock export. The quantities come from the warehouse and location that are configured in the connector settings.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Integration Settings > Sales Settings',st:[],cfg:'Check the Warehouse and Location in the connector Sales Settings if stock figures look wrong'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Products removed from Shopware are archived',ex:'Happens automatically during the sync',biz:'During the daily stock export, the integration also checks whether each product still exists in Shopware. If a product has been deleted from Shopware, it is automatically archived in Odoo. The product is not deleted — just hidden from normal views.',mo:'Sales > Products (archived)',st:[],cfg:'Archived products can be restored if needed — search for them with the Archived filter in Sales > Products'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Stock looks wrong in Shopware?',ex:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',biz:'If Shopware is showing incorrect stock levels, check the Logs for the last stock export run. Any errors will appear there. If there was an error, the integration will try again automatically on the next daily run.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',st:[],cfg:'Stock export errors retry automatically on the next scheduled run'} ]}, {id:'sh',label:'2 · Shipping an order',dir:'Odoo to Shopware — automatic on validation',scn:'Your warehouse validates a delivery in Odoo. Shopware automatically updates the order status to Shipped and shows the tracking number to the customer.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Validate the delivery in Odoo',ex:'Inventory > Deliveries > Validate',biz:'When the package is ready to go and has been handed to the carrier, the warehouse team validates the delivery in Odoo (clicks Validate to set it to Done). Make sure the tracking number from the carrier is entered on the delivery before validating — it will be sent to Shopware automatically.',mo:'Inventory > Deliveries > Validate',st:[],cfg:'Enter the tracking number on the delivery before validating so the customer can track their parcel'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Shopware is notified automatically',ex:'Immediately or on the next daily sync',biz:'As soon as the delivery is validated, the integration sends the shipment information to Shopware. If \"Auto-export Shipment\" is turned on in the connector settings, this happens right away. Otherwise it happens the next morning with the daily sync.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Integration Settings > Sales Settings > Auto-export Shipment',st:['Immediate','Daily'],cfg:'Turn on Auto-export Shipment for real-time updates — customers get their tracking number straight away'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Shopware shows the order as Shipped',ex:'Shopware > Orders',biz:'The order in Shopware is updated to Shipped and the tracking number is visible. If only part of the order was shipped (a partial delivery), Shopware reflects a partial shipment status — you do not need to do anything extra.',mo:'Shopware > Orders > Deliveries',st:[],cfg:'Partial shipments are fully supported — send what you have and Shopware reflects the partial status'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Shopware not updated?',ex:'Check: Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',biz:'If the Shopware order still shows the wrong status, check the Logs. The integration retries automatically on the next daily run if the first attempt failed.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',st:[],cfg:'The delivery stays flagged for export until Shopware confirms it was received'} ]}, {id:'re',label:'3 · A customer returns something',dir:'Odoo to Shopware — automatic on validation',scn:'A customer sends a product back. You process the return in Odoo and Shopware automatically updates the order status to Returned .',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Create the return in Odoo',ex:'Inventory > Deliveries > Return',biz:'Open the original delivery order in Odoo under Inventory > Deliveries. Click the Return button, select which products and how many are coming back, then confirm. Odoo creates a new incoming receipt for the returned items.',mo:'Inventory > Deliveries > Return',st:[],cfg:'You can do a partial return — you do not have to return everything from the order at once'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Validate the return receipt',ex:'Inventory > Receipts > Validate',biz:'Once the returned items physically arrive at your warehouse, open the return receipt in Odoo (Inventory > Receipts) and validate it to confirm the items have been received back into stock. This is the action that triggers the notification to Shopware.',mo:'Inventory > Receipts > Validate',st:[],cfg:'The returned stock goes back into Odoo inventory when you validate the receipt'}, {m:'m-coral',t:'Shopware is notified automatically',ex:'Immediately or on the next daily sync',biz:'As soon as the return receipt is validated, the integration sends the update to Shopware. The same \"Auto-export Shipment\" setting that controls shipment notifications also covers returns.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Integration Settings > Sales Settings > Auto-export Shipment',st:['Immediate','Daily'],cfg:'The Auto-export Shipment setting covers both outbound shipments and returns — one setting, both directions'}, {m:'m-gray',t:'Shopware shows the order as Returned',ex:'Shopware > Orders',biz:'The Shopware order delivery status is updated to Returned. Note: any refund or credit note for the customer is a separate step in Odoo Invoicing — the integration only updates the delivery status in Shopware.',mo:'Shopware > Orders > Deliveries',st:[],cfg:'Refunds to customers are handled separately in Odoo — go to Invoicing to create a credit note if needed'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Shopware not updated?',ex:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',biz:'If the Shopware order status was not updated, check the Logs. The integration retries automatically on the next daily run.',mo:'Integration Hub > Shopware > Logs',st:[],cfg:'Check the Logs smart button on the integration page for a clear history of what was sent'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|wrong|bad/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|active|confirmed|automatic|immediate|ok|success/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|daily|manual|inactive|choice|high volume/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "supplier-compliance.html", "title": "Supplier Compliance — User Guide", "text": "Supplier Compliance — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub Supplier Compliance — User Guide Make sure a vendor is qualified — and their certificates are still valid — before you confirm a purchase order. Written for a new team member. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? This module adds a compliance layer to Odoo's purchasing flow. Each product can require a minimum supplier certification, and each vendor is given one or more supplier levels. When you try to confirm, email, or print a purchase order, Odoo checks whether the vendor's level actually covers what the products require. It also watches vendor certificates for expiry, and can warn you — or block the order — when one has lapsed. Why it exists: procurement teams need to avoid ordering products from suppliers who aren't qualified, or whose quality certificates have expired. Doing this from memory doesn't scale and leaves no audit trail. This enforces the policy at the moment of purchase and records every exception. Two independent checks run on the purchase order: 1) Product vs. vendor — if a product needs a certification the vendor's level doesn't cover, Odoo opens the Compliance Fail Wizard . It's a block-with-override : you either delete the product line or write a fail reason (per line or one general reason) to proceed. 2) Expired certificate — on Confirm, an expired vendor certificate can either warn (you can continue) or block (no confirm button until it's fixed), depending on its setting. A few words you'll hear Supplier Level A simple quality tier — the module ships with three: A, B, C. Supplier Compliance Type A level + an industry (e.g. \"A Automotive\"); this is what you assign to vendors and products. Included Levels Lower tiers a compliance type also counts as — so a high tier automatically satisfies lower-tier requirements. Minimum Supplier Certification The compliance type(s) a product requires from its supplier. Only products with this set are ever checked. Certificate A document on a vendor with an expiry date and a state (Valid / Expiring Soon / Expired); can warn or block on PO confirm. Compliance Fail Reason A written justification a buyer records to push an order through despite a mismatch — logged for audits. 2. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What Odoo does Choice / option One-time setup Onboarding aid for the po_compliance_check module (\"Purchase Order Vendor Compliance Check\", Odoo 19). Checks, wizard messages and fields are drawn from the module source. Shipped levels are A/B/C. Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'A · Set up compliance on a vendor',dir:'One-time per vendor / product',scn:'A new supplier should be marked as \"A Automotive\" so orders for A-level products go through cleanly.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Create the compliance type',ex:'Level A + Industry \"Automotive\"',mo:'Contacts > Configuration > Supplier Compliance > Types',st:[],biz:'Pick a level and type an industry; the name becomes \"A Automotive\" automatically. This is what you assign to both vendors and products.',cfg:'Use Included Levels so an A supplier also satisfies B and C product requirements.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Assign the level to the vendor',ex:'Set the Supplier Level tag',mo:'Vendor contact > Supplier Level field',st:[],biz:'Tells Odoo which tier(s) this vendor is qualified for.',cfg:'Leave it empty on a child contact to inherit the parent company’s level.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Tag the product',ex:'Set Minimum Supplier Certification',mo:'Product form > Minimum Supplier Certification',st:[],biz:'Only products with this field set are ever checked against the vendor.',cfg:'No certification set = the product is never blocked.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Add a certificate (optional)',ex:'Name + expiry + warn/block behaviour',mo:'Vendor contact > Supplier Compliance tab',st:['Valid','Expiring Soon','Expired'],biz:'Upload the document, set its expiration date, and choose whether expiry should warn or block PO confirmation.',cfg:'Set a \"Compliance Notification User\" in Purchase settings so someone is emailed when it expires.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Save',ex:'Future POs are now validated',mo:'Vendor / product form',st:[],biz:'From now on, purchase orders to this vendor are checked automatically.',cfg:'A reminder activity is created ~29 days before a certificate expires.'} ]}, {id:'confirm',label:'B · Confirm a PO for a non-compliant vendor',dir:'What happens at Confirm',scn:'You raise an order for an A-level product, but the chosen vendor is only level C.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Create the PO and add the product',ex:'Add the A-level product + vendor',mo:'Purchase > Orders',st:['Draft'],biz:'Build the order as usual with the product line and vendor.',cfg:'The check runs on Confirm, Send by Email, and Print.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Click Confirm → wizard pops up',ex:'\"…can not be bought at this supplier\"',mo:'Compliance Fail Wizard',st:[],biz:'Instead of confirming, Odoo lists the products that can’t be bought from this supplier.',cfg:'An expired \"block\" certificate would stop you even earlier, before this wizard.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Decide per line',ex:'Delete a line, or write a reason',mo:'Wizard > per-line \"Compliance fail reason\"',st:[],biz:'For each failing line you either remove it or record why you are ordering it anyway.',cfg:'Deleting the line is the \"correct\" fix; a reason is the documented exception.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Or set a general reason',ex:'One reason covers all lines',mo:'Wizard > General compliance fail reason',st:[],biz:'A single header reason justifies every failing line at once.',cfg:'You need either a general reason OR a reason on every failing line.'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Click Confirm → order proceeds',ex:'Reason saved on the PO',mo:'Purchase Order',st:['Purchase'],biz:'With the reasons in place, the original action completes and the order confirms. The reason stays on the PO as an audit note.',cfg:'Cancelling the PO clears the stored fail reasons.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad|block|expired/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|ok|success|valid|purchase/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|manual|expiring/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "transaction-importer.html", "title": "Deep Dive: Transaction Importer — User Guide", "text": "Deep Dive: Transaction Importer — User Guide ← Back to Guide Hub Deep Dive: Transaction Importer Automatically pull payments out of PayPal, Stripe and friends, drop them into Odoo, and match them to the right invoices — with no copy-pasting. A thorough but plain-language guide for a new joiner. Light / dark Handout view Print / PDF 1. What is this? When a business sells online, money ends up sitting at payment companies — PayPal, Stripe, and so on — each with its own list of payments. The Transaction Importer reaches into those companies, copies each payment into Odoo, and then matches it to the invoice it paid . So your accounting stays up to date on its own. Two words to get straight right away: Transaction Here it means one payment held by a provider — e.g. \"customer paid €40 by PayPal on 3 March, PayPal kept €1.20 in fees\". (It is not a database thing — it's a real money movement.) Provider The outside payment service the money is sitting in. This module knows six of them: PayPal, Stripe, Mollie, Heidelpay, Shopify and SIX (a Swiss card processor). Why it exists: without it, someone has to download those payment lists and hand-type (or CSV-import) them into Odoo, then manually tick off which payment paid which invoice — slow and easy to get wrong. This module does both halves automatically. 2. The big picture End to end, a payment travels like this — and every step can run on a timer or be triggered by hand: Step 1 · Source Provider PayPal/Stripe/… holds the payment → Step 2 · Fetch Import Odoo logs in and downloads the payments for a date range → Step 3 · Land Bank statement line Each payment becomes a row on a bank statement in Odoo → Step 4 · Match Reconcile Odoo links each payment to the invoice it settles Safe to re-run. The importer remembers what it already brought in (it checks each payment's id). If you import an overlapping date range twice, it simply skips the ones it already has — no duplicates. A few words you'll hear Integration One saved connection to one provider account (its login details + schedule). One PayPal account = one integration. Setting Line A rule that says \"payments matching these conditions go into this accounting book, and match to invoices like this .\" An integration can have several. Journal The accounting book (a specific bank/cash account) where the imported payments land. Bank statement line The Odoo record each imported payment becomes — one row of a bank statement (money in or out). Reconcile Officially linking a payment to the invoice it pays, so the invoice is marked settled. Scheduled action (cron) A background job that runs on a timer. This module makes two per integration — one to import, one to reconcile. Log The system's diary of what happened (successes, skips, errors) — your first place to look when something seems off. Production vs Test Production = the real, live account. Test (staging) = a safe practice account; the module uses separate \"Test Credentials\" there automatically. 3. Under the hood (the deep-dive bit) You don't need this to use it — but it helps you understand what you're clicking: The Integration record The heart of it. It holds the provider login and runs the two jobs (import + reconcile). It's built on much.'s shared Integration Hub foundation, so it behaves like our other connectors (same states, same logging). Setting Lines do the routing Each line points at a Journal and carries the matching rules. No active line = nothing imports (the log will literally say \"No importer setting lines configured\"). Matching options You can require an exact amount match, or allow small over/underpayment tolerances. You also choose to match by Partner (the customer) or by Transaction ID . Matching on neither is risky — it could tie a payment to any invoice with the same amount. Fees Optionally, the importer adds one extra statement line that sums the provider's fees, so your books reflect what the provider kept. Safety nets Work happens in batches; a bad batch is rolled back without spoiling the others. Long runs pause after ~10 minutes and continue next time. Empty date ranges stop retrying after a few tries so nothing loops forever. 4. Step by step Pick a task below. Click any step for the plain explanation, where to find it, and a tip. Step you take What appears / result Choice / option Where to check One-time setting Onboarding aid for the transaction_import module (\"Transaction Importer\", Odoo 19). Models, fields, buttons and cron names are drawn from the module source. It builds on the private Integration Hub ( ihub ) base, so a few menu names / states come from that framework. Six built-in providers: PayPal, Stripe, Mollie, Heidelpay, Shopify, SIX. Self-contained offline. const WF=[ {id:'setup',label:'A · Set up a provider',dir:'One-time setup',scn:'Connect your company\\'s PayPal account so its payments flow into Odoo.',steps:[ {m:'m-pink',t:'Create the integration',ex:'Name: \"PayPal Production\"',mo:'Integration Hub > Integration Settings > New',st:['Inactive'],biz:'This is one saved connection to one provider account. Choosing the provider type (e.g. PayPal) tells Odoo which service to talk to.',cfg:'One integration = one provider account. It starts inactive until you activate it.'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Enter the credentials',ex:'PayPal Client ID + Secret',mo:'Integration form > Connection Settings tab',st:[],biz:'These are the login keys the importer uses to reach the provider. Each provider shows only the fields it needs (PayPal calls them \"Client ID\" and Secret).',cfg:'On a test/staging instance, fill the \"Test Credentials\" instead — the module picks the right set automatically.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Set the schedule',ex:'Every 2 hours, delay 1 hour',mo:'Integration form > Import Transaction group',st:[],biz:'Controls how often the automatic import runs, and how far back it looks each time.',cfg:'The delay must never be bigger than the interval — Odoo will refuse to save if it is.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Add at least one setting line',ex:'See task B',mo:'Integration form > Reconciliation Settings tab',st:[],biz:'Without an active setting line, nothing imports — the log will say \"No importer setting lines configured\".',cfg:'You need one line per journal/rule; most people start with a single line.'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Test Connection → go live',ex:'Creates the two timed jobs',mo:'Integration form > Test Connection button',st:['Staging','Active'],biz:'This checks the login works and creates the two scheduled jobs (\"IHub: …\" for import and \"IHub: Reconcile - …\").',cfg:'After this, the importer runs on its own on the schedule you set.'} ]}, {id:'line',label:'B · Route & match with a Setting Line',dir:'The rule that does the work',scn:'Send completed PayPal payments into your \"PayPal\" journal and auto-match them to invoices.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Add a line',ex:'One rule = one row',mo:'Integration > Reconciliation Settings > Setting Lines',st:[],biz:'Each line is one routing/matching rule. You can add several if different payments need different handling.',cfg:'Untick \"Active\" later to pause a line without deleting it.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Pick the Journal (required)',ex:'Journal = \"PayPal\"',mo:'Setting line > Journal field',st:[],biz:'The accounting book where the imported payments land. One bank statement is created per import for this line.',cfg:'This field is required — the line does nothing without it.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Set Parameters (optional filter)',ex:'e.g. only \"successful\" payments',mo:'Setting line > Parameters field',st:[],biz:'A comma-separated filter. Leave it blank to import everything; fill it to import only certain kinds (e.g. only completed payments).',cfg:'Only editable when the provider defines what the parameter means.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Turn on Reconcile + matching',ex:'Match by Partner · exact amount',mo:'Setting line > Reconcile / Match over',st:[],biz:'Tick Reconcile, then choose exact-amount matching (default) or allow small over/underpayment, and match by Partner or by Transaction ID.',cfg:'Matching on neither Partner nor Transaction ID is risky — it can grab any invoice with the same amount.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Fees (optional)',ex:'Add a summed-fee line',mo:'Setting line > Fees checkbox',st:[],biz:'Adds one extra statement line totalling the provider’s fees, so your books show what the provider kept.',cfg:'It’s flagged so fees are never counted twice.'}, {m:'m-pink',t:'Keep the line Active',ex:'Leave \"Active\" ticked',mo:'Setting line > Active',st:['Active'],biz:'Only active lines are used during import.',cfg:'Untick to temporarily switch this rule off.'} ]}, {id:'manual',label:'C · Import & fix things by hand',dir:'When you can’t wait for the timer',scn:'Back-fill last week\\'s payments and clear a batch that didn’t match.',steps:[ {m:'m-purple',t:'Open \"Run Manually\"',ex:'Opens the Manual Run popup',mo:'Integration form > Run Manually button',st:[],biz:'Lets you import or reconcile right now instead of waiting for the scheduled job.',cfg:'The button shows once the integration isn’t inactive.'}, {m:'m-blue',t:'Choose the dates',ex:'From / To date range',mo:'Manual Run wizard > from & to',st:[],biz:'Picks which period of payments to pull. Defaults to yesterday → today.',cfg:'Safe to overlap with earlier runs — duplicates are skipped automatically.'}, {m:'m-purple',t:'Pick actions and Run',ex:'Tick Import and/or Reconcile',mo:'Manual Run wizard > Run',st:[],biz:'You can import, reconcile, or both. Manual runs show problems as clear pop-up errors (not just hidden in logs).',cfg:'Ticking neither box gives \"Please select at least one option to run\".'}, {m:'m-teal',t:'Read the result',ex:'\"N transactions imported / reconciled\"',mo:'On-screen pop-up',st:[],biz:'Tells you how many payments came in and how many matched.',cfg:'Re-running the same range is safe if the count looks low.'}, {m:'m-amber',t:'Review skips in the log',ex:'\"no matches\" · \"multiple matches\"',mo:'Integration > log messages',st:['Info','Warning','Error'],biz:'Payments that couldn’t be matched are logged with the reason — no matching invoice, several possible matches, or no customer on the invoice.',cfg:'Fix by adjusting the setting line’s tolerances or the match option, then re-run Reconcile.'} ]} ]; let wi=0,sel=0; const selEl=document.getElementById('wf'); WF.forEach((w,i)=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=i;o.textContent=w.label;selEl.appendChild(o);}); function tone(s){s=s.toLowerCase();if(/fail|error|bad|inactive/.test(s))return'bad';if(/done|ok|success|info|active|staging/.test(s))return'ok';if(/draft|manual|warning|choice/.test(s))return'warn';return'';} function chipHtml(st){return st.length?' Status '+st.map(x=>' '+x+' ').join('')+' ':'';} function render(){ const w=WF[wi]; document.getElementById('dir').textContent=w.dir; document.getElementById('scn').innerHTML=w.scn; const rail=document.getElementById('rail');rail.innerHTML=''; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ const stg=document.createElement('div');stg.className='stg '+s.m+(i===sel?' sel':''); stg.innerHTML=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '; stg.querySelector('.card').onclick=()=>{sel=i;render();}; rail.appendChild(stg); }); const s=w.steps[sel]; document.getElementById('det').innerHTML=' '+s.t+' '+s.biz+' Where to find it '+s.mo+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':''); } selEl.onchange=()=>{wi=+selEl.value;sel=0;render();}; render(); function renderDoc(){ const doc=document.getElementById('doc');let h=''; WF.forEach(w=>{ h+=' '+w.label+' '+w.dir+' '+w.scn+' '; w.steps.forEach((s,i)=>{ h+=' '+(i+1)+' '+s.t+' '+s.mo+' '+s.ex+' '+s.biz+' '+chipHtml(s.st)+(s.cfg?' &#128161; Tip '+s.cfg+' ':'')+' '; }); h+=' '; }); doc.innerHTML=h; } renderDoc();"}, {"href": "vscode-submodule-update.html", "title": "Updating a Submodule — VS Code", "text": "Updating a Submodule — VS Code ← Back to Guide Hub Updating a submodule Run these in the VS Code integrated terminal ( Terminal → New Terminal ). A beginner-friendly walkthrough. Light / dark Print / PDF What's a submodule? Our main project ( product-odoo-19-tests ) doesn't contain the module code directly — it contains links to other repositories called submodules (e.g. much-order-base-integration ). Each link is a bookmark pointing at one exact version (commit) of that module. Updating a submodule is a two-step dance : update the module, then update the bookmark. 🧠 The big takeaway Updating a submodule is a two-step dance: (1) Go inside the submodule and git pull to get its latest code. (2) Go back to the main project and git add + commit + push to save which version of the submodule the main project should use. If you skip step 2, teammates pulling the main project won't get the submodule update — they'll still be pointing at the old version! Before testing: pull the docs into each environment Do this once per testing-environment version, before you run any test. Why this step matters. A freshly cloned version folder only holds bookmarks to the product submodules — not their actual code or documentation. Until you initialise them, those folders sit empty. Pulling the submodules in is what gives each testing environment the proper product documentation to test against. For each testing-environment version, open its main folder and initialise the submodules once: $ git submodule update --init Copy This checks out every submodule the version links to and downloads its content — bringing in each product's code and documentation. Once it finishes, the environment has everything it needs and you're ready to test. Tip: add --recursive if a product has submodules of its own. much. Consulting · Onboarding · Updating a git submodule (VS Code / terminal) const STEPS=[ {cmd:'cd much-order-base-integration', exp:\"Move into the submodule folder. You're now inside the nested repo. (This is just an example module — use the real folder name of whichever submodule you're updating.) \"}, {cmd:'git branch', exp:\"Check which branch you're on. The * marks your current branch — here you're on 19.latest-qa :\", out:\" 18.latest\\n* 19.latest-qa\"}, {cmd:'git pull', exp:\"Pull the latest changes from GitHub. This downloads any new commits, branches and tags from the remote into your local submodule. In this example it pulled a batch of new test files — 71 files changed . Because it was a “fast-forward” , there were no conflicts: your branch simply moved forward to the newest version.\"}, {cmd:'cd ..', exp:\"Go back to the main project folder. You're now back in product-odoo-19-tests .\"}, {cmd:'git add much-order-base-integration', exp:\"Tell the main project “hey, the submodule has a new version.” Key beginner concept: the main repo doesn't store the submodule's actual files — it just stores a pointer (like a bookmark) to which exact commit of the submodule it should use. git add on the submodule folder updates that pointer to the newest commit you just pulled.\"}, {cmd:'git commit -m \"I am updating order base integration\"', exp:\"Commit that pointer update. This saves the change: “the main project now points to this newer version of the submodule.”\"}, {cmd:'git push', exp:\"Push it to GitHub. This uploads your commit (the updated pointer) to the remote repository, on the staging-qa branch.\"} ]; function esc(s){return s.replace(/&/g,' ').replace(/ /g,' ').replace(/\"/g,' ');} document.getElementById('rail').innerHTML=STEPS.map((s,i)=>` ${i+1} $ ${esc(s.cmd)} Copy ${s.exp}${s.out?` ${esc(s.out)} `:''} `).join(''); function copyCmd(btn,txt){ const t=txt.replace(/ /g,'&').replace(/ /g,' ').replace(/ /g,'\"'); navigator.clipboard.writeText(t).then(()=>{const o=btn.textContent;btn.textContent='Copied!';setTimeout(()=>btn.textContent=o,1200);}); }"}, {"href": "wms-integration-framework.html", "title": "WMS Integration Framework (Retired) — Reference", "text": "WMS Integration Framework (Retired) — Reference ← Back to Guide Hub Retired · End of life WMS Integration Framework A reference page for a retired product — written in plain language for colleagues supporting customers who still use it, and for anyone who wants to understand what it did. Light / dark Print / PDF ⚠️ Do not sell this to new customers This product has reached end of life (retired, no longer developed). Please do not offer or sell it to new customers — for new projects use Order Base instead (see section 5). This page exists to explain what it was and to help you support customers who are already using it. 1. First — what is a WMS? Let's unpack the jargon before anything else. WMS stands for Warehouse Management System — the software that runs a physical warehouse. It decides where each item is stored, and it guides the workers through picking (grabbing the items for an order off the shelves), packing (putting them in a box), and shipping (handing the box to a courier). Often the warehouse isn't even the customer's own — it's a 3PL ( Third-Party Logistics provider): an outside company you pay to store your goods and ship your orders for you. Either way, that warehouse runs its own WMS software, separate from Odoo. The core problem: Odoo knows about orders and stock on paper (the accounting and admin side). The warehouse/WMS knows about the physical goods (what's really on the shelf, what actually shipped). If they don't talk to each other, the two drift apart — Odoo thinks something shipped that didn't, or shows stock that's really gone. An integration keeps them in step. 2. What the \"WMS Integration Framework\" was It was much.'s older base layer — a shared foundation — for connecting Odoo to outside warehouse/3PL systems. A \"framework\" here means the common plumbing : the boring-but-essential machinery (running tasks on a timer, keeping logs, matching data between two systems, handling errors) that every individual warehouse connector needs. Instead of rebuilding that plumbing for each new warehouse, connectors were built on top of this framework. Admin side Odoo Orders, deliveries, stock, invoicing ⇄ The plumbing WMS Framework Shared jobs, logs, data matching ⇄ Physical side Warehouse / 3PL Pick, pack, ship, receive 3. What flowed where These integrations are easiest to understand by direction. There are two main journeys — goods going out to customers, and goods coming in from suppliers — plus keeping stock honest. What Direction In plain words Delivery orders Odoo → warehouse When a customer order needs shipping, Odoo tells the warehouse \"please pick, pack and send this.\" Shipment confirmation + tracking warehouse → Odoo Once the warehouse ships it, it reports back \"done, here's the tracking number\" — and Odoo marks the delivery complete. Expected receipts (ASN) Odoo → warehouse When new stock is on its way from a supplier, Odoo warns the warehouse in advance so they're ready to receive it. This heads-up is called an ASN. Received quantities warehouse → Odoo When the goods physically arrive and are counted, the warehouse reports the real quantities back, and Odoo's stock goes up. Stock levels warehouse → Odoo The warehouse is the source of truth for what's physically there, so it periodically syncs the real stock back to Odoo to prevent overselling. Returns both ways A customer return is registered and the stock is put back — kept in step between the two systems. Honest note: the exact screens, fields and settings of our old WMS framework aren't documented here — the code is retired and not in our current codebase. The flows above are how warehouse/3PL integrations generally work; the specifics varied per connector built on the framework. 4. Words you'll hear WMS (Warehouse Management System) Software that runs a warehouse — storage, picking, packing, shipping. 3PL (Third-Party Logistics) An outside company you pay to store and ship your goods for you. ASN (Advance Shipping Notice) A \"heads-up\" message that stock is on its way, so the receiving side can prepare. Pick / Pack / Ship The three warehouse steps: grab the items, box them, send them. SSCC A standard barcode number that identifies one specific pallet or carton, so it can be tracked as a unit. Master data The shared \"facts\" both systems must agree on — mainly the product list — so a message about \"product X\" means the same thing on both sides. Framework / base layer Shared plumbing that other, more specific connectors are built on top of, so common work isn't rebuilt each time. 5. Why it's retired — and what to use instead It has been replaced by a newer, cleaner foundation called Order Base , which itself sits on the Integration Hub (our current shared base for all integrations). Because the newer framework does the same job better, the old WMS framework was retired: no new features, no new sales. For any new project, start here instead: Order Base — our current integration framework. There's a proper guide for it: Integration Hub Order Base . Never begin new work on the retired framework. 6. If a customer still uses it What it does day to day It quietly moves information between Odoo and an outside warehouse — telling the warehouse to ship orders, and hearing back when they're shipped or received. Where to look when something breaks The logs (the system's diary of what happened) are your first stop, just like every other connector. Check the last scheduled run too — a message may simply not have been sent or received yet. Typical tickets \"An order wasn't sent to the warehouse\", \"a shipment didn't come back into Odoo\", or \"stock doesn't match\" — all usually traced through the logs. When in doubt Because this framework is retired and not in our current codebase, escalate anything tricky to a senior colleague — and steer any new requirements toward Order Base. Reference page for the retired WMS Integration Framework — end-of-life, do not sell to new customers; use Order Base instead. The warehouse-integration concepts here are general industry practice; the exact behaviour of our old framework is not documented, as the code is legacy and not in the current codebase. Self-contained works offline."}];
